      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A GAOL-BIRD in handcuffs is worth two in the bush. Fr is easy to realise that time is money by the way some people squander it. "CHARITY begins at home;" but it is not always 6' at home" when called upon. THE saying that "silence is golden" probably originated with some blackmailer. THE proudest moment of a man's life is when he Is telling all about it afterwards. TIME flies, yet the orchestral leaeler sits still and beatErtime. "MAMMA," said little Mabel, "if people eat up all the toadstools what will the toads do when they want to sit down?" "HE gives twice who gives quickly." Yes ; be- cause they come round later on and ask him for another subscription. "CAN the baby walk vet, Noopop ?" "No ; I am still attending to that part of it for hirn."— PRUYN "Are you installed in your new home?" Mrs. Younglove (haughtily) : " No, sir; our furni- ture was a gift from my parents. We have nothing on the insta ment plan. .</p>
        <p>HARRY: "I always wear a hat to .suit my head! han,,.; the style." Dick : "I notice that a soft hat is v,,ur favourite." I'ARISHIONER : "Do you ever speculate in bonds, Mr. Thumper?" Pastor: "Oaly matri- monial, Mr. Pewrent." "LOOKING for work, are you?" asked the good lady. "Oh, no that bad, mum !" answered Mr. Everett Wrest ; " jist metely waitin' fer it." "NATURE is a great provider," said Mr. Preach, solemnly. "Yes, indeed,'' returned Miss Jocose. "Look at all the capes she has scattered along the cold latitudes of the north coast!" _ _</p>
        <p>EDITOR: "Well, did you interview Mrs. Twaddle, of the Woman's Rights Club?" Re- porter: "I saw her, and she said she had nothing , to say." " Wed, sueeze it down into a column. Were crowded to-day." SUE caught his eye in passing, that womat sweet and shy. He found it most harassing When his eye was caught in passing: 'Twas the ferru e of her 'brella that had caught him in the eye. HE WAS-MISTAKEN. —" All is over between us," said the young man that had found a richer and was trying to take leave of his old love "You are mistaken," replied she. "All is not over. In fact, my breach of promise suit has not yet begun." Ho postponed the transfer of his affections. WHEN WOMEN ItrrLic.—" Have you been able to catch the Speaker's eye?" asked the first lady legislator. "Have 1?" rejoined the second legislator. "Wll, rather. I wore my navy blue bengaline with the heliotrope sleeves, and the Speaker couldn't keep his eyes off me." AN UNREHEARSED EFFECT. —Medium (who is giving private seanue, in sepulchral tones) : "The spirits are about us "—Sharp rat-tat heard in direction of door, shiver runs through audience, and— The New Servant: "Please, mum, am I to cook all them sausages for supper?' AN EXPLANATION. —Stranger in the Neighbour- hood: " What a terrible noise in there! Shrieks and shouts, crying and laughter, all mixed." Mrs. Hoolihan " Yis, sor ; 'tis Tim K egan's wake." (Proudly). "There's no wan enj'ys a t'ing of thot kind bike the Oirish, sor !" SUE: "I hope it isn't my twenty thousand that you're after, George?" Mr. Graeper "Believe me—no, (tiding. I'd marry you if you had only nineteen thousand." PUZZLED. —Miss PaSSee : "I told Mr. Fuljames I rode the bicycle for the good of my complexion, and he said it would be impossibla to improve my looks.' Miss Terry: " Well ? " Miss Pass6e : "I'm trying to think if he meant to insult or to com- pliment me !" IN a certain town not far from Birmingham on a recent Sunday, while the collection was being taken up in chureb, the organist played " What shall the harvest be?" A ci,oxtrmit has excited public curiosity by having a large apple painted outside his shop. When asked for an explanation he replied, "If it hadn't been for an apple, where would the ready- , made clothing shops be to-day ? " -</p>
        <p>MISSIONARY: "Ah, you have a pet, I see." Convict: "Yes—this rat. I feeds him every day. I think more o' that 'ere rat than any other Livia' creature." Missio ,ary " Ah, in every man there's something of the angel left, i one can only find it. How came you to take such a fancy to that rat?" Convict: "He bit a gaoler." GOVERNESS: "What is the continent ? " (Dead silence). "Does no one know ? " Johnny (aged eight) : "I know. It's a place where people go when they're married." _</p>
        <p>ESCAPED.—" Sir," questioned an irate female shopper as she pounced upon a small man who was pacing the store, "are you the shop-walker?" • N-n-no, ma'am," he gasped, "I—l'm o-o-only the pp-proprietor." VERY LIKELY. —Judge (to prisoner); "Did you really call this gentleman an old fool last night?" Prisoner (trying to collect his thoughts): The longer I look at him the more probable it seems to me that I did." A PIOUS WISH. —A little boy who was compelled, on a journey, to drink condensed milk, which he abominated, said to his mother:—" Mamma, I do wish they would kill that condensed cow." A COFFIN-MAKER in one of the leading streets of London, happening to have apartments to let, has pasted his bills upon the coffins in his window, announcing "Lodgings for single gentlemen." RESULTS OF INVESTIGATION. —Bronson : "Have</p>
        <p>the detect yes found out anything about that burglary yet?" ,Johnson: " Yes ; they've come to ' the conclusion that the motive for the crime was , money.'' Exc USED. —Oi tymann (spending the day in Lonelyville) : " Didn't it ever strike you that your , servant is impertinently inquisitive ? " Outertown : "My dear fellow, it's only tile way of a privileged old family retainer. Why, would you believe it, that girl has been wit' us over six weeks !" CARRYING OUT INSTRUCTIONS. —Grocer (to new boy) : 4' You must tell people we are very busy, James, whet er we are or not. They like to buy of a arm t at they think does a large trade." New Boy "All right, sir." Grocer (a little later) : "Did old Mrs. Bension want anything, James?" New Boy : "Yes, sir; she wanted a couple of tins of biscuits and ten pounds of brown sugar, an' I told i,er we was so busy we didn't know which way to turn, and so she said she was in a hurry, an' she'd get them round the corner." "WHAT are you doing right on the top of that tree, Mike? Pont you see that it's being cut down?" Mike: "Yes, your honour; the last toime ye had a tree cut down, it fell on the top of me, and k egarra, Oi'll be safe this toime." HALF-PRICE. —Cashier (at the theatre): "Sir, a ticket to the stalls is three shillings, and you have only put down eighteenpence. Old Gent: "Excuse me, I shan't pay any more, as I am deaf</p>
        <p>on one ear." PROMOTION IN CHINA. —A fairly decent China- man with a well filled puise can procure almost any distinction on which he has set his mind. The money he pays for it probably goes to the Ern- press-Mother in Pekin, whose extravagance knows no limits and causes her to resort to shady prac- tices to replenish her coffers. We extract the following from t•e tariff of orders. The title of " Yan Fob," about equal to knight, costs 200 dollars "Gne Dan," a degree higher, comes to 300 dollars; "Yuen Why," or rank of baron can be got for 1.000 dolls, s ; a still higler title for 2,000 dollars. For the round sum of 10,000 dollars a Chinese may become a " Chefoo," or mayor of a town of 10,000 inhabitants. G,tz2.'etou "Come, let's go home." Le Rounder " Heavens ; Why, it's only eleven o'clock ! I can't go home at this time of night." Guzzleton : "Can't?" Le Rounder: "No; wouid 't know what excuse to make to my wife for coming home so early." Mx. • SAMPSON: "I love you devotedly, Miss Cbumley, my pecuniary affairs have prevented my making a declaration before. But I have put enough away now to feel justified in asking you to become my wire." Miss Chumley (hesitatingly, but sweetly) : "I confess that I am not wholly indislerent to you, but—but—" "But what, dear? 'Would you mind telling me how much you , have put away?"</p>
      </div>
