      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>JOTTIIN GS.</head>
        <p>JOTTIIN GS.</p>
        <p>PRETTY TOUGH. —AMA "You look rather deli- cate. Are you perfectly well ? " Little Nephew: "Oh, I can stand anything. You ought to see some of the doctor's doses I've taken and lived through." A DANGEROUS THlNG—Lawyer: "What's that _„</p>
        <p>boa you are -reading ?" Laiw Student "Oh, it's a work on common sense." Lawyer: "Yes, sir; and one day with such a book as that would ruin your mind for legal work for ever."</p>
        <p>DOESN'T COST Mucu.---Sweet Girl: "Papa says YOU can't afford to marry." Ardent Youth : "Nonsense I I can get a preacher to perform the ceremony for two dollars." Sweet Girl : "Can YOU? How foolish papa is !"</p>
        <p>THEIR BUSINESS METIIODS.—" Well," said Bill Yuss, " I've taken a powder for my :headache, a pellet for my liver, and a capsule for my gouty foot. Now; what puzzles me is, how do the things</p>
        <p>know the right place to go to after they get</p>
        <p>inside?" YOUNG LADY: "You are a woncterfnl mas:er of</p>
        <p>the piano, I hear." P, ofessor Von Spieier : I blay aggoinpaniments zorne times. " " iki:ern- paniments to singing ? " " Aggoinpaninients to gonverzations." IN THE COUNTRY.—Tourist-: " Landlord ! have</p>
        <p>a nice, juicy steak cooked for the—good, fre-th meat—underdone beef not too red--nice and tender —and mind the butter's fresh ! " Landlord:</p>
        <p>"Lor', sir, if I had all that I'd eat it myself !" Ix is umierstood that a well-known champion pedestrian developed his great walking powers by trying to collect debts in London. Anxious: "You lost your head completely at the banquet last night." Soakley : " Well, I've got it back this morning." A MAN told his daughter that if she learned to work he would give her a surprise. She learned the art, and he surprised her by discharging the servant girl. THE man who wagered a lady that he could thread a needle in less time than she could sharpen a pencil won in sixteen minutes, at the end of which time the lady had whittled up her pencil. TRAMP (piteously) : " Please help a poor cripple." Kind old gent (handing him some money) : "Bless me, why, of course. How are you crippled, my poor fellow?" Tramp (pocket- ing the money) : 'Financially crippled, sir." HE: " What ! Is Mrs. Wedalot going to be led to the altar for the third time?" She: "No. I should fancy not. She ought to be able to find the way herself by this time." DAUGHTER: "Yea, I know Mr. Staylate comes very often; but it isn't my fault. I do every- thing I can to drive him away." Old Gentle- man : "Fudge! I haven't heard you sing to him once I" _ .</p>
        <p>LONDONER: "What fine air you have out here; it's so much fresher than in London." Farmer: "Jess so I That's jest what I was saying to my old woman. Why ain't all these big cities built out in the country?" A SUNDAY-SCHOOL boy some Sundays ago gave his teacher this illustrative definition of "respon- sibility : "Boys has two buttons for their braces so's to keep their trousers up. When ne bution comes off, why, there's a good deal of responsi- .bility on the other button."</p>
        <p>MRS. LoTos : "Poor Mrs. Loy9y is ill, and I must go and see her." Mr. Lotos : "An err,ncl of mercy, eh?" Mrs. Lotos: "Partly; and, then, I want to see how she looks with her hair down."</p>
        <p>MRS. Casey (enthusiastically) : "Sure, me darter has married the foine man. He makes iverybody shtand round whin he spakes. No one claret say worrud to him ! They lives on the fat of the land, too, for 'tis the tome position tie has!" Mrs. Mulligan (eagerly): "Phut is he—a jhanitor or a polaceman ?' A New ARISTOCRACY. —"You must be very careful to have nothing to do with those bacilli,' said a germ ',amma to her small daughter " Why, mamma?" " Because we belong to the very exclusive artificially propagated bacilli, while they can lay no claim whatever to culture." SMALL COMFORT--Little Dot: "Teacher says we needu t all learn to write the same hand." Mamma: "That pleases you, doesn't it?" Little Dot: "Why, no. Its Just as hard to learn to write one way as another. Now, if she'd only tell us we needn't all spell the same way, there'd be some comfort in it" Cormn SEE VERY WELL.—Mother (angrily) "Why didn't you come in when I called you?" Johnny : "Im comin'." "Yes, you're coming now; but I called and called, and you didn't budge till you saw me at the door with a switch in my hand." " Well, my earsight is sort o' weak to- day, but my eyesight is all right." AT a recent breach of prom se case, in which the plaintiff was a Miss Kyss (pronounced kiss), the plaintiffs counsel, Mr. Stuflgown, commenced his speech with, "My Lord Kyss, the plaintiff," when his lordship dryly interrupted him with :—"Oh, kiss her yourself, Mr. Stutlgown ; it would be unbecoming for one in my position." PRECOCIOUS YOUTH (smokiog furiously) ; "This is a smoking carriage, ma'am." Determined. looking Female: I know that, young gentleman, and I know exactly what you're smoking. It's a penny Pickwick; but you needn't put it out on my account. I keep a cigar shop." A LADY of charitable disposition asked a tramp if she could not assist him by mending his clothes. "Yes, madam," he replied, "I have a button, and if you would sew a shirt on to it you would greatly oblige me." ON A STEAMBOAT.—Nervous Passenger: "And are you sure there is no danger, officer ? " Officer: "Not a bit. The captain's just gone to take a nap because it's too foggy to see anything." IDLE Tim: "Phew 1 Never had sich a narrow escape in all the years I've been trampin'. These ere big cities is full o' pitfalls fer the unwary." Tattered Tom: "What happened ye?" Idle Tins: "I went into that big buildin' to tell the tale of woe, and where dye think I found meself ? It was an employment office—an' twenty different persons offered me work afore I could get out.' Mother-in-Law. (severely) : have you to understand that my daughter was raised with some regard to religion and morality 1 It is better you two should separate now, it the pleasures of the gaming-table appeal more to you than your home." Jack Potts "Well, mother, the ninety I won last night puts me some three hundred ahead of the game." Mother-in-Law (playfully): "You naughty, naughty boy 1" A NIGHT OF DANCING.—With the dances now in vogue, a lady taking part in every set will per- form in a single night a distance of 19 miles, hopping on her toes, and her body compressed in a corset, not unlike a cuirass for unyielding rigidity. If the same lady were required to be perform the same journey on foot, in suitable attire, and on the best roads she would declare most positively that it would be the death of her. Mits. DE TONE: "I want to have an asylum ap- pointed for my poor husband. His mind is failing." Attorney: "Have you any particular evidence of the decline of his mental facu.ties ?" Mrs. De Tone: "Yes, indeed. I asked him for £2 for anew bonnet this morning, and he said I had better take £5, so I thought I would come to see you." SOMETHING LINGERING. —A suburban chemist had been advertising his patent insect-powder far and wide. One day a man rushed into his shop and said: "Give me another half-pound of your pow- der, quick, please!" "Oh," remarked the chemist as he proceeded with the order, "I'm glad you like the powder. Good, isn't it?" "Yes,' replied the customer. "I have one cockroach very ill. If I give him another half-pound he'll die." ARTIST (to friend, who is looking round the studio): "Now, that's a favourite little bit of mine. I knocked it off in a couple of days—re- minds me of Turner." Friend: "Wh—what is it?" Artist: "A view of the Bristol Channel on a foggy day. What do you think of it, eh?" Friend (slowly) : "Well, I think it would have looked more —er—natural if the fog had been a little bit thicker, don't you know." Mu. Tnorrga (to himself) : "As I will be at the office until nine o'clock this evening, I think I'll send Alice a telegram explaining matters." As he enters the house at ten o'clock, his wife of a month throws herself into his arms. "Oh, Tom," she weeps, holding out a yellow envelope, "this horrid telegram came to-day, and I've been afraid to open it lest it should contain bad news What in the world has delayed you so?"</p>
        <p>OI'TBR DECRIVED.—Don't think the hair can he pr..- e,-ved without prop .r nouri .htnent ; all hair speeWlst s positively as•tert that pure crease is absolu'elv u,ceisary or the preservation of the hair and ROW LAN D.'S MACASSAR OIL is without donb the best. It nourishes the hilt.. pre- vents it falling off or turning grey, eradi ateA scurf, aud arrests baldness. Also in a golden colour tor fair hair. Bottles 3s. 75.. 10s 6d. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OiL.—Preserv( a the hair. ROWLAND'S MACAW 4 It OlL.—Prevents balcint AS. RowLAND'S MACASSAR M.—Prevents scurf. llowLAraig MACASSAR OlL.—Beautifies the hair. Ask anywhere for ROWLAND'S MACASSAN OIL, of 20, Hatton Garden and avoid cheap, worthless imitations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>FXAMINI4T, sATU_RDAY, OCTOBER 5, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE FXAMINI4T, sATU_RDAY, OCTOBER 5, 1895.</p>
        <p>It is estimated that New Yorkers spend ten thousand • pounds every day for flowers. Pierre Lorillard, the tobacco millionaire, dces not Use the weed in any form. Queen Amelie of Portugal is studying medicine, and goes into its intricacies with the zest and zeal of a pro- fessional man. There is a pupil of Beethoven still Irving, ar,cl he is touring in tile Far }list and wirming enthus,astic ap- plause from the Japanese. This is the veteran pianist Ghevalier de Kontski, now more than eighty-five years of age.</p>
        <p>As a general rule, it may be said that the lower the race the greater the disparity between male and temale &amp;ature. According to the traveller Campbell, the male ' Lipp is 10.94 inches taller than the female, while the difference is 8.7 inches among the Javans, according to Weiabach. The difference among most European nations does not amount to more than three or four inches. We invariably find that the offspring of animals, equally matched in size, is stronger and healthier than that of those unequallv matched. A curious custom prevails among Roumanian peasants. When a Roumanian girl is of a marriageable age tier • trousseau, which has been carefully woven, spun, and embroidered by her mother and herself, is placed in a • painted wooden box. When a young man thinks of ask- ing to be allowed to pay his attentions to the girl, he is at liberty first to open the box, which is always placed in a convenient position, and examine the trousseau. Ii he be satisfied with the quantity and quality of the dowry, he makes a formal application for the girl's hand; but, if not, he is quite at liberty to retire. A pupil of Thomas Carlyle has just died at Montreal. Jonathan Duncan Gleig Findlar was his name, and he used to delight his friends with anecdotes of the early days when Carlyle faithfully carried out the precept, " Spare the rod, spoil the child." " Oh, yes," said the old gentleman, " Carlyle was downright cruel. Re would help us very little and chastise us very much. His attention was not upon us at all but upon what ha was constantly writing at his desk. I think he hated teaching. Many readers will be surprised to be reminded that Mr. Sims Reeves's recent marriage is the third incideut of the same kind in the tenor's eventful career. His first wife VldB not in the profession, and died suddenly ; and a painful incident was the tenor being startled by the news of her death just after he had, in the panto- mime of " Ola Mother Goose," been singing— My wife's dead, there let her lie ; She's at rest, and so am I. His first:wife was much older than himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>DEAREST</p>
        <p>MT. • NELLIE, I don't like to think the summer has gone yet, so I shall tell you this week of a summer blouse, a garment which has, thanks to the delightful weather we have just been having, not yet entirely disappeared. The blouse I allude to was made of fine black alpaca. The lining is close-fitting, and the alpaca is set on in a number of tiny tucks, which form a yoke, and are repeated at the waist, leaving a very pretty full front, which is equally becoming to a slender or plump figure; indeed, it is perhaps most suitable to the latter, as the little fulness breaks the hard outline. The back is also tucked just in the centre, and the blouse hooks down the front in the ordinary way. Then there is a pretty dress of cream crdpon, ornamented with lace bib and insertions. There is a sash entwined round the waist in pale gold satin, and tied at the side into two loops and streamers. The expanded part of the sleeves is partly tucked and gaged to- wards the shoulders.</p>
        <p>So many ladies have mantles or trimmings of seal of caracul, that they will be delighted to hear how great a part these furs will play in winter fashions. The newest and smartest capes—cut full and rather long, so that they fall in rich folds, finished with a Medici collar which turns up or down—are in seal or caracul. Some are in both furs; because the added stimulus given to stripes this autumn has reacted on furs, and we have a selection of capes made in alternative stripes of seal and caracul. Although caracul is so prevalent, there is little or no astrachan to be seen. The grey furs are fashionable, especially silver fox and chinchilla; but the novely of the season (for those who can afford it) is the royal fur, ermine. Rich redingotes of silk, velvet, or face-cloth are lined with ermine for driving purposes. Theatre and evening wraps of broche, satin, or velvet are lined with ermine, and have great ermine hoods, collars, or p6lerines. Winter coats have ermine collars and revers, the muff to match being of the same fur. Sable, skunk, and all brown furs will be worn. The new sealskin capes look very handsome, and the Alaska seal is, I think, the most handsome. I was shown the other day a full circular cape in this material. It had epaulettes and rolled collar trimmed with sable tail, and was one of the most stylish-looking garments I have seen for some- time.</p>
        <p>Here is a capital smock for a little girl. It is made in dahlia-tinted velveteen, with the gathered yoke and cuffs of Indian silk to match. The lace frill might be simply adjusted for special occasions, and it should be of a handsome lace. Irish point is very suitable, and this can be purchased at a most reasonable price. The smock would look quite nice, however, without the frill, and the design might also be made in material, with yoke and cuffs of velveteen and silk. Four to five yards of velveteen, or two and a half yards of double. width stuff, would be required. As for hats, the newest felt ones (which are to be had in all colours) are really very pretty, with broad artistic brims, not too stiff, stitched at the edge to give them a certain firmness. They are trimmed with a profusion of feathers. In bonnets there is very little novelty. The shapes and styles of trimmings are wonderfully like the past season's, heavier materials, of course, being used. For instance, is one in brown, trimmed with rep bows and large white bird and osprey, forms a most stylish headgear.</p>
        <p>If more women realised that straining the eyes produces wrinkles, more would exercise a proper care of these valuable members. Reading by a dim or failing light, coming suddenly from a dark room to a light one, or from a light room to a dark one, overworking the eyes in any way, and last, but by no means least, wearing dotted and cross- barred veils—these, and more taxing of the eye- sight, are of valuable assistance in the wrinkle. making process. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>A Belfast cnarespondent states that the threatened dig- pute in the shipbuildirg trade has practically col- lapsed.</p>
        <p>! Tuttoxr IRRITATION AND COUGH. —Soreness and dryness, ' tickling and irritation, inducing cough and affecting the voice. For these symptoms use Epps's Glycerine Jujube. I lit contact with the glands at the moment they ere ttacited by the act of sucki-g, the Glycerine in these arts con- fections becomes actively healing. Sold only It( ev.es, 7id., tine is. lid., labelled "iireze EP? it Co.. Ltd., Homceo- pathic Chemists, London." Dr. AtiOnie, in his work on "Nose and Throat Diseases," sa."The Glycerine Jujubes prepared by James lit,ps am' are of undoubted service as a curative or palliative age:% while Dr. Gordon Holmes, Senior Physician to the MiMicipal Throat and Ear Infirmary, writes : "Alter art extended trial, I have found your Glycerine Jujubes or considerable benefit in almost all forms of 'throat disutse."—Advf.</p>
