      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>GLENALPINE'S OATH;</head>
        <p>GLENALPINE'S OATH;</p>
        <p>OE, THE MARK OF THE BLACK CROSS.</p>
        <p>,BY SYLVANUS COBB. I.</p>
        <p>CRAPTEP. XV. A DEADLY PURPOSE.</p>
        <p>On the morning following his return from the jungle Lord Birchmear went to the Government House. He went ostensibly to see the governor- general, but really he was anxious to see only Arthur. He made inquiries at the office of Colonel Tabor, and was informed that Captain Rainsford had not yet made his appearance. His lordship's next move was to the dwelling of Sir Jasper Wal- lace, and he found the old general walking alone in the garden. Sir Jasper wore a pained and jaded look, and he could not even smile as he extended his hand to his friend. Sir John, who had advanced with eager, buoyant step, caught the look, and was filled with sudden alarm. "Sir Jasper! What has gone wrong with you ?" "Have you seen Arthur ?" returned the general. The baron was startled with a nameless dread. "My boy 1 Hath evil befallen him ?" "Then you have not seen him ?" "No,—not this morning." Wallace took the baron's arm, and leading him away to a far part of the garden, he told him the story of the terrible ruin which had fallen upon the love of the young people. Sir John listnned as op,' might heed the passing of a fearful dream, and when the story had been told— " Alas! my poor boy !" he groaned, in painful accents. "0 ! this is cruel !"</p>
        <p>"Sir John !—you do not inean—" "Hush ! Do not think, my dear Wallace, that I could reflect upon HER. No, no. I thought of the cruel fate !" "A fate doubly cruel," said Sir Jasper. "My poor girl is utterly prostrate and heart-broken. I think there never was a love purer and stronger than the love which lay between those two hearts. Every impulse or Mary's noble and trusting nature waan, devoted and true, told her to love the youth- ful hero. He was handsome brave, frank, strong, and manly, and he had saved her from a calamity worse than death, and his heart was all her own. This morning, when I saw how utterly miserable and unhappy she was, and how the great agony threatened to eat away her young life, I ventured to hint that, after all, she might forget the dark story of the past, and regard it only as the creature of a troublous dream, and, so forgetting, join hands again with her true-hearted lover." " Well, and what did she say ?" asked the baron, eagerly. "Sir John," returned the old soldier, with solemn impressiveness, "can you, for the moment, imagine yourself in her place? Think of the one only brother—her companion in orphanage—the brother who had held her up when she was a weak child, and who had never, with all his faults, shown her aught but tenderest love and kindness. Ile was her mother's boy—her only pride in the youthful years—the one being left to her after her mother died whom she had loved from her earliest recollec- tion. Do you think of this, Sir John ?" "Yes ! yes !" IS Then could you, were you in her place, take to the most sacred place in your confidence, and esteem—lifting even above the dead brother's place —the man whose hand was red with that brother's blood? Would you not live in the continual presence of an avenging spirit—a spirit haunting you by day and by night—bidding you to look with horror upon the blood-red hand into which you had given your fate?" "it is true—alas I too true!" groaned the baron. "And yet," he added, after a pause, "how wicked, almost, seems the fate which could put it into the hands of Lady Birchmear and her son to work this evil I In their base hearts they will triumph over this, while others are stricken and palsied with grief." Sir Jasper laid his hand upon his friend's arm, and looked eagerly i...t0 his face. " Sir John," he said, in a trembling whisper, "Harold -Berwick bath a desire to bee our brave boy crushed? " The baron started and turned away his face. At length he returned, and put forth his hand beseech- ingly. " Sir Jasper, I must have a friend with whom I can share the fearful burden that oppresses me. I know I can trust you. I will tell you my story, but not yet must you breathe it to another. Listen." And thereupon Lord Birchmear told Wallace the story of the treachery of the present Lady and her son. He told all which he himself knew. He told how Arthur had been traduced, and how, finally, they had attempted to fasten the odium of a base robbery upon him. Sir Jasper listened to the end, and finally he said: "My dear Berwick, you give me hope. That duel was planned by Harold. The end was an- ticipated by him. If we could but prove that that fatal end was of his own contrivance—that he did it—" "Hush I Let us not hope too much, Sir Jasper." "By Heaven !" cried the old soldier, smiting his fist upon his breast, "while my poor girl is sudering even unto death, there is no such thing as hoping too much. Go you, Sir John, and look to Arthur. If you cannot bid him hope, you can give him strength and encouragement in the prospect of an earnest and useful future." "I will go, Wall .ce ; but while I seek my boy, do you keep an eye upon Lady Birchmear and Harold. I doubt if the day passes without their presenting themselves at your doors." The two friends walked together to the gate, and there they separated. Lord Birchmear returned to the Government House, and found Arthur waiting to see the governor-general. He was startled when he saw how pale and worn the youth looked. "Arthur—my dear boy !" Our hero took the outstretched hand and pressed it warmly; but he did not speak. Sir John drew him into a private apartment, and there told him that he had seen Sir Jasper, and that he had heard the sad story. "Then, my lord," said the stricken youth, "you know the worst." His voice was not the rich, mellow, musioal voice that had been wont to sound from his lips. It was hard and metallic, and its firmness was strained and unnatural. He was out- wardly calm and possessed, for he had spent nearly the whole of the long, dark night in doing battle with the terrible calamity that would lay its crush- ing hand upon him. "Arthur, you will not let this thing break you down. Look up, my son—" The poor boy put out his hand with an imploring gesture. "No more, my lord! Do not speak of it now. Let me rest." "But, my son, what will you do? Why are you here in the War office ? " "I will join my regiment, my lord." "No, no,—not yet. 0, no, Arthur. When Igo back to England you must go with me." "And until then," pursued the youth, "let me go with my regiment. There I shall be busy, and may find excitement to blunt the edge of this keen sorrow." -</p>
        <p>"Arthur, will you wait a few days?" " Wherefore, my lord 9 "To please me. Not to please Lord Birchmear, but to please—on FATHER ! " There wassomething so tender and so pathetic —something so truly loving and paternal in his look and the tone that Arthur's heart was touched, and, throwing his arms around the old man's neck, he burst into tears. -</p>
        <p>"You may command me, my father." "Then, my boy, wait for me. We will say no more now. I have—" The speech was interrupted by the opening of the door, and upon looking up the baron beheld Tony Piper, Harold's valet, who stood in the en- trance with his bat in his hand. "How now, sir?" demanded Sir John, with a stamp of the foot, lie was angry not only at having been interrupted in the midst of such a scene, but the more at having been intruded upon by the con- fidential attendant of his intriguing step-son. "What do you seek here ?" "Pardon, ray lord, but her ladyship sent me. Lord Blatchford is at your house." " Blatchford i" repeated the baron, with sur- prise. " When did he arrive ?" "He came up in one of the company's yachts last night. He arrived in a troop-ship which was forced to cast anchor in Diamond Harbour." " Ah I then the troop-ship has come safely to port ?" "Yes, my lord, and it is said she has a full regiment on board. They will be up to-morrow." "Well, get thee gone, and tell Lady Birchmear that I will return presently." "The governor will be glad to know of this arrival," the baron continued, after Tony had gone. "We need troops very much." "Then." ventured Arthur, with a faint smile,</p>
        <p>" the more need of my joining my regiment" "Not so much need now, my boy, as there was before. Here we have a thousand new men. But remember your promise. You will make no decisive movement without consulting me." Arthur promised, and Sir John shortly after wards left the palace. At his house he found Lord 'Ratchford, whom he had last seen in London, and who had just arrived by the Balderic troop- ship. While the two lords were talking over their wine, Lady Birchmear called far her carriage and ordered the coachman to drive to the house of Sir Jasper Wallace. She alighted at the door and entered the drawing-room. She desired to see Miss Wallace. Word was brought to her that Miss Wallace was too unwell to receive company. ," Will you tell her it is Lady Birchmear who would see her ?" "She knows that your ladyship is here." Her ladyship was for the moment vanquished, but she presently renewed the attack. "I surmised that your dear mistress was not well, and I much wished to see her. Will she per- mit the Honourable Harold Berwick to take her out upon the Esplanade this afternoon in his new phaeton? The weather promises to be delightful; the phaeton is one of the moss charming vehicles, and Mr. Berwick would be really happy." Elsie Dolan, who served Mary Wallace as wait- ing-maid, and whose bright, quick eyes and pretty face gave token of both wit a, d intelligence, seemed inclined to answer her ladyship upon her own responsibility ; but, upon second thought, she retired with the message. "My mistress will not iide to-day," she an- nounced upon her return. "Perhaps to-morrow ?" Elsie's eyes snapped and her pretty lip cured. " Miss Wallace's compliments" she said, "and when she wishes to ride she will let Ludy Birch- mear know." "She will see Mr. Harold this evening?' Will you ask her ?" "She does not wish to see him at all," replied the maid, flatly and emphatically. " And perhaps," suggested her ladyship,. "she does not wish to see me?" " Your ladyship is blessed with excellent pene- tration." Lady Birchmear was not the woman to expose her chagrin to a menial. As quickly as possible she re-entered her carriage and was driven back to her own house, wnere in her boudoir she met her son. "My mother, you look flurried." And you," retorted her ladyship, sinking into a chair and t.nnitni, herself, "are not particularly cheerful of expression. What have you dim- covered ?" "Tell me first if you have seen Mary Wallace." "I have not seen her." "They told me you had gone to Sir Jasper's." " Aye ; I went to the house. I was admitted to the drawing-room by a servant, and I was dismissed with the comforting assurance that Miss Wallace would let us know when she wished to see us." The fan was applied with desperate energy. "Did you mention my name ?" "I did; and your invitation was carried to the lady." "And what was her reply ?" "In one brief word, Harold, she does not wish to see you at all." Harold Berwick clenched his hands and ground his teeth with rage. "Remember, my son," said her ladyship, sooth- ingly, "we have our revenge in advance. The blow we have dealt has touched the girl far more deeply than I had imagined." " Bah !" cried the son, with a furious stamp of his foot upon the floor, "she may yet recover Lorn that. If she spurns me, we gain nothing by the development." "Spurn you, Harold ?" " Aye ; I know whereof I speak. I met Sir Jasper this morning, and he turned his head away Irons me. It was a cut, deliberate and direct I" " Harold !" "But, my dear mother, this is as a kitten's play compared with the other thing I have to tell you. I met a servant of Colonel Harry Seaton last even- ing. He had just come with his master from Jungee- poor. Sir Jasper Wallace and Arthur Rainsford were in their company. They met Lord Birchrnear on the road—met him in the great Nagdapoor Jungle—where he was beset be a score of murderous Ghurkas and Arthur delivered him. Arthur literally saved his life 1" "Harold I—you dream!" "I wish I did, my mother; but I cannot so easily throw off the danger. But this is only the beginning. Listen further: When Tony Piper went to the Government House in quest of Lord Birchmear, he found him closeted with Arthur, and they were actually weeping in each other's arms. His lordship called Arthur sox; and Arthur called him FATHER " Lady Birchmear dropped her fan and sank back aghast. She first called for her salts, and then asked for a glass of wine; and when she had somewhat recovered, her son gave her a more par- ticular account of the transactions that had come to his knowledge. She heard him to the end, and the great needs of the occasion gave her strength. "Harold, what will you do ? " The question was not asked shrinkingly, but with fierce and deadly purpose asked as though she would measure the depth and the strength of her son's will. Harold took a turn across the room, then stopped and poured out a goblet of wine and drank it, and then came and stood before his mother. "The end has come," he said, slowly and dis- tinctly, "and there is but one thing for me to do, And, mark you, I have been prepared for it ever since I set foot upon these shores.' "Harold I" The woman arose to her feet and laid her hand upon his arm. "Dare you strike the blow with your own hand ? " "Aye I Think you I would trust such work to another? No, no; I have gone too far to hesitate at this point. I know Arthur's habits. He will walk alone upon the Esplanade to-night. To- morrow I will. be Lord Birchmear's only living heir !" "But, my son, remember that a dash of Sir John's pen can ruin you. He must not suspect—" " fallow I He suspects nothing. I have net shown my hand yet. With Arthur out of the way we can take time for soothing the old man's feel- ings. He can never dream whence the blow came. While Arthur lives there is a tie that binds him to the memory of his first wife." Lady Birchmear clenched her white hands, and ground her heel into the carpet. "If your hand is strong enough and steady enough, strike 1" "]'ear not, my mother, that my hand will fail me. I strike for a barony 1" At that moment there came a rap upon the door. Harold opened it, and found Tony Piper standing there with a newspaper in his hand. "What is it, Tony ?" "I thought your honour might like to se - e that." said the valet, pointing with his finger to a para- graph upon one of the printed pages. "It's about the arrival of the troop-ship Ba/dric." Tony retired, and Harold re-entered the bou- doir. " What is it, my son?" Harold Berwick stamped his foot upon the floor with a fearful oath. "Look at that! Read it I" he whispered, quivering at every joint. The lady read, and started from the printed paragraph aghast. "It is Colonel Thorpe's regiment !" she said. "Aye," returned her son, with fierce energy; "but Arthur Rainsford shall not live to hear their story I"</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Be Humane to the Flies.</head>
        <p>Be Humane to the Flies.</p>
        <p>It is somewhat late now to talk of flies, as the nuis- ance is almost abated at this time of the year. But the special correspondent of the" Chemist and Druggist" tells us that the Leipzig amiety for the Prevention 01 Cruelty to Animals IldS circularised the druggists of the city, pointing out the euffering caused to flies by the use of sticky fly-papers,and suggesting that the sales of these articles sii,uld be iiiscoutinued. 'roe Society at the same time"suggests, as a more " humane " means of keeping off flies, the placing about the apartments of small,open vessels flied with cod-liver oil. As flies greatly dislike the odour of that drug,they may be trusted promptly to betake themselves next door. This remedy, however, might not prove over p!easant.</p>
        <p>Cuban Coffee-making. The most delicious coffee obtainable is V.) be found in Cuba, and the secret lies in the peculiar way it is made. It is prepared by first hall-filling a course flannel bag with finely pulverised coffee, and suspending it from a hook over the pot or other vessel. Cold water is p9ured on the bags at intervals, until the entire mass is well saturated ; then the first drippings which have fallen in- to the receptacle are poured again over the bag until the liquid almost becomes thick and very black One teaspoonful of this strong extracted liquid placed in a cup nf boiling water will yield a draught of coffee that is simply delicious. In Cuba this flannel bag hangs day aud night on the wall, the process of pouring on the cold water and allowing it to drip being almost cease- less in its operation.</p>
        <p>May we Eat Fresh Bread.</p>
        <p>Many people lik fresh bread better than stale ; others prefer stale. This diff-rence of taste is mainly a mattet of habit. We generally like best that which vin. become accustomed to whether it is better for us or not. it we investigate fresh bread carefully, we find it is soft, doughy, sticky and glutinous ; that by mastication it is with great difficulty separated and reduced to smaller parts, and is less under the influence of the saliva and gastric juices. It consequently forms itself into masses by careless and hasty mastication and deglutition, and in this state enters the stomach. In the case of weak stomachs, when the digestive juice is weak and scanty, it is unable- to penetrate and dissolve this sticks, mass and scarcely able even to act ur on the surface of it ;it frequently remains in this organ unchanged, and, like a foreign body,irritates and incommodes it,inducing every species of indigestion and all its long train of symptoms, Of course, if the person is strong and exercises a great deal out of doors, new bread is finally digested, and per- haps without much inconvenience, but the tax on the ty,cly is greater than it should be. Such persons are less clear-headed, less happy, and accomplish less than they otherwise might. Stale bread,on the other hand, is easily broken up in the mouth,easily mixes with the saliva and gastric juice and easily digested. The argument, then, Li in its favour rather than in favour of new bread. Mothers would do well to look to this subject in the pre- paration of food for their tables ; and form tastes which favour health rather than the reverse in their children and all the members of their family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Value of Sunshine and Fresh Air.</head>
        <p>The Value of Sunshine and Fresh Air.</p>
        <p>Which is of the greatest importance, furniture or health? A sunbeam has a power to fade the carpets and curtains, to rot the blinds, and for this reason some people carefully exclude the sunshine. What is the re- sult ? The family is always ailing ; the young girls have a waxen white skin and a weary, pinched expres- sion of countenance. Their appetites fail ; they fall into such a bad state of health that the doctor is called in. In olden days he would have shaken his head, per- haps, and friends would have whispered that dreaded word, " decline !" Nowadays, he notes the pale gums and waxen skin, and says, " ancemia :" prescribes iron and milk,fresh air and exercise, and often a change. If he knows nothing about the darkened rooms, he will be puzzled as to why no permanent improvement manifes itself, and possibly the patient will seek other advice.Sl</p>
        <p>It has been held by hygienists that the wrinkles on a woman's lace are often due to impure air, a cause of which is not infrequently the exclusion of snrishine. The skin owes its beauty to the nerves which control the fine blood-vessels of the surface, whose work lends glow and clearness to the fare. The nerves,in turn,owe their sensitiveness to the air, which is our chief nutriment,in- haled by gallons hourly, and should be pure and invigo- rating. When the nerves are deadened by close air the fine muscles lose their tone,the tissue of the face shrinks, and these shrinkages become wrinkles. So, let the sun- shine and air into the house, even at the expense of carpets and furniture.</p>
        <p>THANKFUL THOUSANDS All over the world testify to the peculiar curative powers of Flood's Sarsaparilla. Its remarkable suc- cess is one of the wonders of the day. Everywhere you go you will find someone who has been benefited by this great medmine. It is the household word in many communities, and in hundreds of homes it is THE ONLY MEDICINE and the only physician that is ever employed. Thousands of men, women, and children are living to-day as illustrations of the health-giving powers of Hood's Sarsaparilld,and in scores of cases they freely admit that they would not be alive tc-day but for Hood's Sarsaparilla. SLEEPLESS NIGHTS. Rheumatism and Pains in Head Cause the Trouble. Relief and Cure Follow- Taking Hood's Sarsiparilla. " Gentlemen,—l suffered for three or four years with rheumatism and pains in my head. I lost all appetite and could not sleep at night. I had been under the treatment of several doctors, but could not get any permanent relief. About six months ago I had my attention attracted by an ADVERTISEMENT OF HOOD'S</p>
        <p>Sarsaparilla, I tried so many different remedies that I had got tired of them, but my husband in- duced me to try a bottle of Hood's Sarsaparilla. I was relieved a little after the first, but after taking the second and third bottles, I recovered my appe- tite, and could sleep splendidly all night, and the</p>
