      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>"UAJA " critA r.</head>
        <p>"UAJA " critA r.</p>
        <p>many &amp;Dish taxpayers are aware that the en :"'vernment, among its other industrial enterprises ga d • le go In the manufacture of cocoa? The fact th not generally known, I should think, except in f 5 44e Admiralty Department. Yet the cocoa mann- " has been in existence since 1822. It is situ- th'e' Deptford, and makes cocoa for the Navy, se, "Thual output oeing sufficient to supply about wia.rietY thousand sailors and marines with the, soale beverage. It is fairly good cocoa, this. tb7erotnent production, it appears, as,according to Ad'iridence given before the Committee on tht .eoc Iteration of Food, it contains fifty per cent. uf taki°,a, and fifty per cent. of sugar and arrowroot, taken as a standard by private mantainc- tuers, TherA • --- the -18 no longer any room to doubt that ofa continued resignations of Voltanteer hieeeta are likely to lead to a serious crisis, in the eferY of the force. The position of the retiring etitertl was made perfectly clear at the Volunteer, 80n51lis8ion. They are unwilling to meet the per- eonses which attach to a commission, and instloti. intt is contended, should, in the majority of the awneeB, be borne by the War Office. tieveral of vt "ar Office authorities are in harmemy with this</p>
