        <p>1311r.glar5 and thieves are in a state of abnormal activ t • I yin :London this autumn, and they have ,ttlllosen'the south-east quarter of the metropolis as et,°,l,cading scene of their operations. Richmond Wimbledon have been specially favoured ivy the fraternity, who have paid no fewer than 30 success- 4111domiciliary visits to the houses of residents in e course of the last seven or eight days. To these add the theft of Mrs. Langtry' s jewels and that of the •. . itrtg silver ingots, and it will be seen that money Agfribeen made in other places as well as in the Can mining market. At Wimbledon several elri4e mansions were broken into and a great deal Te„dalllage done, iind at Richmond the midnight anders were in several cases successful in carry- off a goodly share of spoil. Besides these tinny ecessful cases there have been during the same week te_ not ,tar short of a dozen unsuccessful at- Where the burglars became alarmed before evecring their purpose. Even the letter-boxes have " escaped their attentions. MrConway,h„ ' t' the Congregational minister of Wel- ‘beTrillatove, who is the latest victim to the prevalent lef that no one is ever to old too ride a bicycle, 'wee e ne who owned none of the culture and learn- eltrZ -vhich he possessed—and it was much— to ‘1)01.1,1Y advantages The classics were to him a closed ,e °Ft at an age when meri with better chances are it°ll3Derent scholars. But Mr. Conway had too keen 11aense Of the impossibility, even by nv,st strenuons 3Dlicatloo. of wholly redeeming lost time, to let ? 8 Childretybe in the oaMA VOsition. Hit son, now - rcfessor of ;Latin at Cardiff, was, when still quite and acceptable and competent Cambridge ,i;OT, and Mr. Conway leaves at least one accorn- ensiled daughter who has done excellent work.</p>
        <p>_wa ft slack ti _ inshe abnormal heat in London has changed what rucl among the doctors to a busy one. imedical men hitherto with little to do have uni- versally found themselves with a large number of l'ilatients.on their han'cif,. The report of the Registrar en ; 1., eral is a far more definite guide than common [ , 47,P_ Ft. In the corresponding week of last year the .074,h-rate from iliarrhcea was 44 below the average. t'aes latest report from :-orn,raet House shows that tuoll Year it is 27 above, or an increase of 71 deaths, 4reSay nothing of 27 gastro-enteric eases. These last ta °6 tIY in the East-end, and no doubt are due 1 eatingth,, unsound fruit. On the whole, however, 4,;"kleath-rate is only 2 per 1 000 over the usual therage, being HOW 16 per 1,000, and last year for ' elqiulc week 14.5 per 1,000. lir ,r, am • • . 1- ' T. r Tanels Rivers, the well-known horticu •,;,,,,,',l2iit. Who is also a practical farmer at Sandbridge- ins'ttl, propounds an ingenious scheme for remedy- „ R the agricultural depression. He would farm the 'R(lokilnties which, under exiting circumstances, are Dknnig out of cultivation, by means of grand em- ail,. ". Landowners and practical men, Mr. Rivers tha(cßeets, should be the directors, tenant farmers ' tl: raulaLiers, and under such a system he thinks '• ,i a labourers would have regular employment and CA eni.erfillar rate of wages. The capital he would base value of the acreage of the land, and he be- t' ditives that if the companies undertook all the in- ,'' iiooB,tries. fruit and forestry as well as corn and thew would pay an ordinary interest. He AteB(l Maintains that great companies of this char- Could not be trifled with by any Government, that they would make their influence felt in ing l&amp;gislation for the benefit' of agriculture. "NW ,• 4moTTIFRS.—Mrs. WI f lows Soothing Syrup has been 14 over fifty years kv millions of mothers for theirchlidren sw. taethtng I t with perfect ansevss. It will relieve the poo