        <p>WA RRINOTO :sr TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The in,7mtlily meting or this Council was held in the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Councillor James Fairclsugh) presid••d, and there were also prese.,t —Aldermen Harris .n, Sutton, Wright, Hutchinson, Blackhurst ; Councillors H. T.Fairclough,Roberts,Green, Cannel, Hill, Smethurst, Shilloock, Skelton, Wood, 1,7 e, Greening, Evans, Bennett, Seaman, Shaw,Garner,i3aird, Plaimpton, Wilkinson, Muir, Monks, Idepherd, Flaaton •, Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Longdin (boroiNh surveyor), Fairhurst (borough treasurer), and Stanley Wilson (assistact town clerk).</p>
        <p>THE GIFT OF PARR HALL I VOTE OP VVIANKS TO M. PARR.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in opi,nin, the business of the Counch, said that sIOCR the Council summons was sent out, Mr. Parr had formally presented the Parr Hall to the town. He thought they ought to talm the opportunity at their first meeting tilnae the event of recording Mr. Parr's muni- ficence on their minutes, and he therefore proposal that the following record he madewon the minutes of Grinnell: —" Mr. J.Ctiariton Parr having on the 26th September, 1895, handed to the Mayor the deeds of Parr Hall, and forma ly presented the hail to the borough, resolvel,that the Council desire to recor theie warm appreciation of Mr. Parr's munificence and to offer to him on behalf of the town the heartiest thanks of this meeting for his most .generous gilt." ( ffear,.hear, and applause)...</p>
        <p>Alderman Sterrox seconded the motion, and said there was no doubt Thursday last would be regarded as a red letter day in the history of Warrington. The inter- esting ceremony in which they were happily engaged on that day was an important event, one of the most important events which would be recorded in the history of the bqroug,h. (Hear, hear.) The name of Parr had always been honoured and respected in Warrington, and it would te handed down to posterity as tkit of one of its greatest ben factors. (Hear. hear.) . . . .</p>
        <p>- Alderman fifiRRISON observed that he must indeed be very ungrateful who could treat a gift like that of Parr Hall with indifference. Ho did not think Mr. Parr could have given them anything of which they stood in greater need than such a building, for it was almost dis- creditable that a town of upwards of 50,000 inhabitants should have no place of its own in which to hold meet- ings or to entertain anyone who 'came to the town. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Saw GREEN supported the motion, and re- marked that aithougn everythisg was done at the pub- lic assembly in the ball to inakethe vote of thanks from the whole town perfect,they as a municipality ought to take special notice of the matter. fie did not think Mr. Parr's gift was unappreciated by anybody, and he be- lieved that the whole town looked upon the gift with thankfulness. The gift was a precedent which he hoped would be acted upon. Warrington had been unfortu- nate in many respects. It was an old town,witli all the responsibilities of new streets to be made and old streets to be reinade,and they had need of institutions similar to the hall to make the town what they would like it to he. A magnificent opportunity was now given to wealthy townsmen to emulate the noble example which had been set by Mr. Parr. (Applause.) The motion was then put and heartily agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>COUNCIL AND THE LATE POSTMASTER.</head>
        <p>THE COUNCIL AND THE LATE POSTMASTER.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said the C aincil were aware that Mr. Kerry, the postinaster.had been apoeinted postmaster of Stoke-on-Trent. Fle did not think they ought to allow Mr. Kerry to leave without expressing their acknow- ledgments of the good work he had done, for , the town and the coavenience of its inhabitants. The postal arrangements of the t wn were never so perfect as at the present thne,and he thought they would agree with him that lie had done right in sending Mr. Kerry the following letter.—' Dear Mr. Kerry,—The Town Clerk ioforms me that you have now left Warrington to take up your new duties as Postmaster of Stoke-on- Trent. It wris thought that you wemld not be leaving for a few days longer; otherwise I should have taken an earlier opportunity of writing to you to express on behalf of myself, the Town Council and the inhabitants at large their sense of indebtedness which they feel to- wards you for the greatly improved facilities in the 'postal arrangements of the borough wh'ch you have madeouring your stay with us. Moreover,the kind arid courteous manner in which you have acted as postmaster of this town has wont)r you the respect and este,,m of all. We regret exceedingly that you have lat Warring- ton, but are pleased to hear of your well-earned advance- ment by your appointment as postmaster of a much larger district, and we wish you long life and happiness in your career of so mach usefulness to those for whom you discharge the important duties of noAmaster.—Be- lieve me, dear Mr .Kerry, yours very faithfully, ..lAmss FAIRCLOOOH. (Hear, hear.) Alderman HARRISON quite agreed with what the Mayor had said. It had, he said, been the town's good fortune to avail itself of Mr. Kerry's good services which had been directed in all respects for the interests of Warring- ton. ,(Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MINUTES OF COMMITTEES.</head>
        <p>MINUTES OF COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Watch, Finance and Estate,Street Improvement, Health and Sanitary, Museum and Libr- ary, Cemetery, Town Rail, Park and Baths, Markets and Water Committees were adopted without debate. The most important matters in the minutes were given in last week's " Examiner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WOOD PAVEMENT FOR MUSEUM-ST.</head>
        <p>WOOD PAVEMENT FOR MUSEUM-ST.</p>
        <p>On the minutes ot the Paving and Sewerage C.onemit- tee, Councillor GREENING aknl if anything had been done with regard to placing wood pavement in Museum. at., near trt the Museum, and he referred to the incon- venience by the noise of vehicles over the stone pave- ment. . .</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGHT sympathised with the complaint, and said he would bring the matter before the Com- mittee,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.-THE APPOINTMENT OF</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.-THE APPOINTMENT OF</p>
        <p>INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>The average attendance at the public elementary school during August was reported to be 9,101. corres- ponding period last year 8,950. The minutes also relate to the appointment of chief arid assistant school atten- dance inspectors. Councillor Straw-GREEN moved, and Councillor SHILLCOOK seconded the adoption of the minutes. Alderman Sorrosr said he had no doubt the Committee acted wisely in appointing the gentleman named in the minutes as ,chief school attendance officer, but it was very extraordinary that they should advertise for a suc- cessor to'Mr. 'Riley, as no fewer than 73 persons were put to the trouble and expense of applying for the vacant situation when there was not the slightest &amp;panne of a stranger being appointed. He looked upon the pro- ceedings as a farce. Councillor WOODS said great consideration was given to the matter by the committee. The whole of the ap- plications were carefully considered, two hours and three-quarterabeing spent in the work. He did not see that any great hardship had been inflicted upon the applicants, as they would not have been put to much expense. He contended that if they could promote the j officers who were already serving them it was their duty as a committee to do so. (Hear, hear.) Councillor llonEnTs said that no doubt the Council would approve of subordinate officers being promoted if they were qualified, and if any mistake had been made' it was that the committee did not consider the p^ssi bility there was of appointing Mr. Jones before the advertise- ments were issued. (Hear, hear.) There was Fomething to be said on behalf of the 73 gentlemen who applied for the post. Councillor SHAW GREEN replied that he had heard of these complaints outside the Council Chamber, but na mistake whatever had been made. The committee were placed in a very awkward predicament. The school examinations were just coming on, and they had been for SOME, time without an officer. They were sorry for the applicants, and for the amount of labour they had , gone through, but Mr. Jones had previous to his applica- tion for the post, tad no opportunity of showing what was capable of doing. He was second man to Mr. Riley, who would not allow any man to help him more than was necessary. The usual advertisement was , issued, and the whole of the applications were treated on their merits. In the meantime Mr. Jones developed the ability which they thought was latent in him, and the oommittee were satisfied that the best appointment had teen made. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were approved. "TUNING OFT THE GAS", prom the minutes of the Gas Committee it appeared that it had been decided that a new vertical boiler be purchased at the estimated cost of £4.0. Councillor RIC:HARD FAIRCLODDR moved, and Council- lor fiIIPHERD seconded, the passing of the minutes. Councillor Monts wished to ask a question of some urgency, seeing that the lives of a good many of the inhabitants had been placed in 'je)pardy. He was told that the gas went out at most of the houses in Wilsna- Patten-great between twelve and one o'clock in the morning. It was most important that the inhabitants should have been warned, because at some houses the gas would be kept on all night. Alderman WRIGHT confirmed the statement. Councillor Wows said the same thing happened in Sankey-street. • Councillor R. FAIRCLOUGH said he happened to he in Wilson-Patten-street on the night in question, when he found a strong escape of gas near Mr. Hotacrts's offices. He menticned the matter at the Gas Works, and perhaps that had something to do with the matter. However, inquiries should be made into it. The minutes were agreed to. FIRE ENGINE COMMITTRE.—A WANT OF WATER.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Fire Engine Committee coming up for adoption, Councillor BENNETT said there Was one fire not mentioned on the minutes. How was it there was no report of the fire alleged to have taken place at the Town Hall on the Tuesday previous. (Laughter.) There was to be an experiment of the Fire Brigade, a gentleman from Stoke was to witness their process of extinguishing a fire, but judge of his (C,uncillor Ben- nett's) consternation and vexation when he found that</p>
