        <p>all the experimcrital display beca'ne a complete ii Tl.ere wa; rl water, and once a.i iii tiny had ti complete coiltapso of the Fire Hr . a He did rot haiiik the Fire Vrt,iiiie Cammittee were iis;fle for this r- Ocular ahllapse. Someone wss to blame—the Fire EngiCommitten or the Water Cointnittse. The blame oughtto be put on the right shoulders. (Laughter.) l'tte MAYOR : This is not on tam minutes. (Linghter.) Oommiillor BENNETT : My point is, why is it Llt ct the minutes ? • Councillor SMETHURST : He has made his speech. ;ought to have stopped him at the beginning. The MAYOR: I don't think it is necessary to reply. You all know the circumstances. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>You</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF TRELAIWAYS. rnlnutes of the General Purposes Committee hav- ing been moved, Councillor ROBERTS asked if the General Purposes Committee would be the proper Corn- rnitee oonsider the question of municipal tramways. It was quite time the subject was discussed. Alderman HARRISON : It is not on the minutes. (Laughter.) Thm [OWN CLERK repliei that the matter was before the Town Council some years ago, when it was referred to the General Purposes Committee, and they appointed a committee called the Tramways Sub-Committee. That would be the best course to adopt again. Cmoc,llor ROBERTS said he should act upon that sug- gestion.</p>
