      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>TII E EX A INI IN 14R., SATURDAY , OCTOIII4II 5 , 18!-:.)5.</head>
        <p>TII E EX A INI IN 14R., SATURDAY , OCTOIII4II 5 , 18!-:.)5.</p>
        <p>TITLED EINWPEANS AND AMEILICA.N</p>
        <p>HEIRESSES.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of American heiresses who are wedded to titled Europeans : Lady Abinger was Miss Helen Magruder, daughter of the late Commander Magruder, U.S. navy. She was married in 1868 to Leiutenant General Lord Atinger, C. 8., a Crimean officer. 1 The Marchioness of Anglesey was Miss Mary Living- ston King, daugh,ter of J. P. King of Sandhills. U.S. 'She is the widow of the Hon Henry Wodehouse, of Eng- land, and was married in 1880 to Henry Paget, fourth Marquis of Anglesey. The Marchioness of Anglesey took 1E50,000 to England. I The Countess Casa de Agreda was the widow of George Lorillard, and took £200,000 aboard with her. Lady Aylmer was Miss Ann Reid, the daughter of T. Douglass 11,4 d, of New York. Lady Aylmer took to ! England a quarter of a million. Princess Salvatore Brancac?.io, wife of an Italian prince of the house of Savoy, was Elizabeth Field, of New York. She carried a fortune of £200,000 to her Italian home. Mrs. William George Cavendish Bentinck was Miss Elizabeth Livingston, of New York. In 1866 she was married to the Hon. Cavendish Bentinck, M.P., great- grandson of the Duke of Portland. Mrs. Cavendish Ben- tinck t )ok to England £300.000. Lady Terence Blackwood was Miss Flora Davis,of New York. She was married in 1893 to Lord Terence John remple Blackwood, second son of the Earl of Dufferin and the British Ambassador to Paris. Lady Blackwood carried abroad a fortune of £40,000. .</p>
        <p>Lady Arthur Butler was Miss Ellen Stager,of Chicago. She was married in 1887 to Llrd James Arthur Welling- ton Foley Butler, second son of the second Marquis of Ormonde. Lady Butler took £200,000 to England. Duchess de Camposelice inherited £1,000,000 from her first husband, Isaac M. Singer of sewing machine fame. All of this enormous sum she carried abroad with her upon her marriage to the Duke de Camposelice. The Hon. Mrs. William Carrington was Miss Juliet Warden, of New York. She was married in 1871 to Colonel the Hon. William H. Peregrine Carrington, second son of the second Lord Carriegton. Mrs. Car- rington took to England £40,000. Countess de Castellane was Miss Anna Gould,daughter of Jay Gould. Her fortune, most of which will be spent in France, amounted to £3,000,000. Lady Randolph Churchill was Mks Jennie Jerome, daughter of Leonard Jerome, of New York. Lady Ran- dolph Churchill took £40,000 to England with her. Mrs. Charles Pelham-Clinton was Miss Lizzie Zertga, of New York. She was married in 1886 to Charles Napoleon Pelham-Clinton, son of the late Lord Charles Pelham-Clinton, and grandson of the Duke of New- castle. The Princess of Galatio Colonna and Stigliano was Miss Eva Julia Bryant Mackay, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John W. Mackay, of New York and London. The Prin- cess took abroad with her the income from £1,000,000. The Duchess de Cacez was Miss Isabella Singer,daugh- ter of Isaac M. Sieger. She carried abroad with her £400,000. The Countess of Craven was Miss Cornelia Martin, of New York. The Countess carried £200,000 to England.</p>
        <p>Lady Gordon Cumming was Miss Florence Garner. She brought £200,000 to England. Mrs. Ernest W. Beckett-Dennison was Miss Lucy Traces, Lee, of New York. She brought to England £lOO,OOO. Countess Devonne was Miss Florence Audenmel, of Washington. She carried £40,000 abroad. Viscountess Deerhurst was Miss Virgina Bonynge, of California. She had £400,000. The Countess of Essex was Miss Adele Grant of New York. When she married the Earl of Essex several years ago she brought with her a fortune of £200,000. Countess de Frankenstein was Miss Brewster, of New York. She was married in 1894 to Count Henri is Frankenstein, now of Rome. but a Russian by birth. Her fortune amounted to £BO,OOO. Lady Graham of Esk was Miss Eliza Jane Burn, of New York. Lady Grantlev was Mrs. Catherine McVicker, of New York. Lady Grey-Egerton was Miss May Cuyler, of Norris- town, U.S. Princess flatzfeld was Miss Huntington, daughter of Collis P. Huntington. She carried £1,000,000 with her abroad. Baroness Efalkett was Miss Sarah Phelps Stokes, of New York. She carried £200,000 abroad. Lady Vernon Harcourt was Miss Elizabeth Motley, daughter of the Hon. J. L. Motley,the historian,and the widow of J. P. Ives. In 1876 she married Sir William, taking to England £400,000. Lady Fermor-Flesketh was Miss Florence Emily Sharon, daughter of the late senator William Sharon, of Nevada. She took £400,000. Lady Edmund Flornby was Miss Emily Augusta Roberts, of New York. She carried away £20,000. Lady Lister-Kaye was Miss Natica Yznaga del Valle, of Cuba and Louisonia. Her fortune was £lO,OOO. Lady Charles Keith Kortright was Miss Martha Ella Richardson, of Philadelphia. The Duchess of Lante-Monfeltio della Rovere was Miss Matilde Davis, daughter of Thomas Davis, of New York. She took abroad £600,000. The Countess Langier-Villars was Miss Carola Living- ston of New York. She took abroad £lOO,OOO. Countess Eberhard von Lindon was Miss Isabella An- drews. daught of Loring Andrews. She carried £200,000 abroad. The Duchess of Marlboroligh was Miss Lilian Price, of Troy. She was married in 1888 to George Charles Spen- cer Churchill, eighth Duke of Marlborough. The Duchess brought the income from £1,400,000 to England. The Marchioness de Mores was Miss Medora Marie Hoffman, daughter of J. Hoffman, the New York banker. She took abroad a round million sterling. Lady Musgrave Was Miss Jennie Lueinda Field. She brought to England £120,000. The Hon. Mrs. Northcote was Miss Edith Livingston Fish, of New York. She married Sir Arthur Paget.' Lady Paget brought £lOO,OOO to England. Countess Pappenheim WAS Miss Wheeler, of Philadel- phia. She carried £200,000 to Europe. Lady Playfair, wife of Sir Lyon Playfair, was Miss Edith Russell, of Boston. Lady Plunkett was Miss May Tevis Morgan ,of Phila- delphia. She brought away £300,000. The Duchess de la Rochefoucauld was Miss Mattie Mitchell, of Oregon. She took with her £60,000. Mrs. nerke-Roche was Miss Fanny Work, of New York. She carried to England £lOO,OOO. Baroness de Selliere was Miss O'Brien, daughter of the New York banker. She carried £400,000 to France. Countess Sierstoepff was Miss May Knownon,of Brook- lyn. The Countess carried abroad £200.000. Princess Scey-Montbeliard was Miss Winneretta Singer, dsuehter of the late Isaac M. Singer. She carried £400,000 abroad. Lady Vernon, daughter of Francis Lawrence, of New York, rook £200,000 to England. Lady Wolseley, wife of Sir Charles Michael Wolseley, was Miss Anita Theresa Murphy,,f San Francisco. Lady Wolseley brought £500,000 to England. Baroness de Vriere was Miss Annie Cutting, of New York. The Baroness to dr abroad £200,000. The Countess of Waldersee was Miss Mary Ester Lee. daughter of a New York grocer of small fortune. She married, in 1864, old Prince Frederick of Schleswig- Holstein, died six months later, leaving her a great for- tune. In 1866 she married Count Waldersee, and his rise in the political world is due largely to his wife. Countess Waldersee is considered the most remarkable woman in Germany, and,next to the Empress,is thought to be the most powerful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MOTION BY THU MAYOR:</head>
        <p>MOTION BY THU MAYOR:</p>
        <p>the MAYOR moved "That the regulations with respect to dairies, cowsheds, and milk-shops made by this Couriml on the sth day of March, 1895, be, and the same are hereby confirmed." This was seconded by Councillor R.T.FAlamonaa,mid agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>'CORPORATION AND THE. SHIP CANAL..</head>
        <p>THE 'CORPORATION AND THE. SHIP CANAL..</p>
        <p>COURCiIIOr SHAWGREEN said it had been stated that the difference between the Corporation the Traders and the Canal Company had been settled. He expected that something would h tve been said that night to en- able them to go away with their minds satisfied that the long Immding difficulty was at an end. The Towx CLERK : It was settled on the day the Council summons went out. .</p>
        <p>COUBCMOT ROBERTS :It will be sufficient to say that it is a fact that the matters in dispute have been settled, and that they have received the approval of the traders' representatives, so that we have got through the whole matter with the support and approval of the traders as well as the Corporatton. (Bear, hear.) This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WHAT DO YOU SAY IT IS?</head>
        <p>WHAT DO YOU SAY IT IS?</p>
        <p>What do you say it is that tires and exhausts us most? Hard work? Oh, no. When a man, or woman either, is well fed and healthy, hard work is a good thing. The day's tug being over you eat your supper—sauced with appetite—and by-and-by go to bed and sleep like a top till morning. What's better than that? Why, nothing this side of heaven.</p>
        <p>I (the writer) have a cast-iron habit in this matter. I turn in at exactly eleven and out at exactly seven. Never wake between those hours except when there is a bark- ing dog scmew here near. Then I look to that dog and his owner next day. I say a man who can sleep that way has drawn the first prize in Life's lottery. Oh, no; work does't exhaust anybody save for his good. It about half empties his jug for one day, and then Nature holds him under the spout (sound asleep) and fills him up again. . _</p>
        <p>But, oh, the pity of a thing like this! Any of you who feel the Angel's fingers touch your eye-lids every night for eight hours of blet.sed blindness, fancy, if you can, the case of a woman who says, " I often awoke at nightie: great dread and fear, with perspiration running from me in streams." Such a condition is awful—terrible. Mere pain were a thousand times preferable to it. And that, under- stand, without any noise to wake and.frighten her. One such night is more wearing than a week of hard work. " Wearing," do I say ? It is killing that's what it is. What canoed it ? Let us pick up the facts one after another, and perhaps we may find out. She says : "My hands, arms, and legs had b•come numb and stiff, and I lost the proper use of them. As I I walked I was not sure of my footsteps ; I had a feeling as if I should fall down. I was much swollen around the laxly, and troubled with flatulency. At times I was taken with faintness, and a at feeling came over me I.as ,if I were about to die. I not fearfully low and nervous, and was afraid to fe left alone. For days and days I never touched *any kind of food, and for twelve months ate scarcely anything." Now all this is sad and bad as can be, and our hearts are sore for the poor lady ; yet as far we don't seem to get hold of the outside end of the thread.. Suppose we look for it amongst the first sentences of her letter. " In the early part of March, ISM she says, " I appeared suddenly to fall into a low, depressed condition, every- thing being a toil and a trouble to me. My appetite failed, my mouth tasted badly, my tongue was coated; and the least morsel of food gave me pain and tightness across the chest and around the sides, so I could not bear the weight and pressure of my corset. My skin was sallow, eyes tinted with yellow, and there was a dull pain at my right side. I was constantly sick, and a sour sort of fluid came up and nearly choked me. A* for food, I could not bear the sight of it ,it made me sick to look at it."</p>
        <p>The case is plain enough now. She was seized with acute indigestion,which in a few months developed into chronic inflammatory dyspepsia. All her early ailments were symptoms of this common yet deceptive disease, Always the same deadly thing, it wears more disguises than a hired murderer. She saw a doctor, of course, who dithwhat he could ; but his medicines were like candles in a London fog—without effect. It was simply a wonder that she lived until the date which she names now.</p>
        <p>"I got so weak and low," she says--and who can doubt it ?—" that I thought I never should recover, when one day in July, 1891, we received a book telling of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. My husband read the book and persuaded me tot ry this medicine. He got me a bottle from Mr. J. H. Shaw, chemist, 120, New- townards Road. A few doses relieved me. I kept on with it and soon relished and digested food. Thus en- couraged I continued with the Syrup, and am now well I and strong. I can eat anything, and work with plea- sure. Mother Seigel saved my life, and you can publish the fact if you wish to. I will gladly answer inquiries. (Signed) Mrs. M. McGregor, 10, Greenville Avenue, Bloomfield ,'Belfa st, January 18th, 1893." We are glad of this result, and congratulate Mrs.Mc- Gregor. We are sure that her once shattered nerves— poisoned and starved by indigestion and dyspepsia—will now permit her to sleep in peace.</p>
        <p>But how many other suffering women await the help of Mother Seigel P Alas ! thousands. We hope some of them may see and read this. Happy for them if they do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>;REVISION eOUILT.</head>
        <p>;REVISION eOUILT.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY_ VOTERS..-RRVISION OF LISTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. T. Foard, revising barri, ter, was present at the , Warrington Borough Court,on Wednesday evening, last week to revise the lists of county voters in the Warring- ton division. Mr. Renshaw and Mr. Clayton appeared on behalf of the Literals, and Mr. Holland Owen and Mr. Mawdesley on behalf of the Conservatives. There was a considerable number of claims,but very few of general interest. The Liberals objected to the name of Mr. Robert Davies being kept on the list in respect of certain freehold land situated in Orford, in the occupation of William Hunt and other tenants. Mr. Fowler. who represented Mr. navies. contended that the property still retained its freehold nature,although Mr. Davies had leased all of it to different persons for long terms. He stated that Mr. Davies had reserved the power of distress and the right to re-enter upon the land, in Case of non- payment of the ground rent within a certain number of days after it was due. Mr. F mler contended that the etotind rent was not included in any of the rents men- tioned in the Act of 1884 which prohibited the holders of certain rents from being entitled to vote.—Mr. Renshaw contended that Mr. Davies iiad parted with the land and that it had changed hands, and that the claimant did not hold a yard of land in the parish of Orford.—The Revisit'," Barrister after some consideration struck the name off the list. On the application of Mr. Owen, the Revising Barrister agreed to grand a case for appeal to alhigher court.</p>
      </div>
