      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>COLTTERY ACCIDENT AT TYLDESLEY.</head>
        <p>COLTTERY ACCIDENT AT TYLDESLEY.</p>
        <p>FIVE MEN KILLED.</p>
        <p>An accident happened on Tuesday in the Wellington Colliery, Tyldealey,owned by Messrs. Ramsden and Sons by which the underground manager, Mr. John Kay, and two firemen and two datallers were killed. Some of the 'miners who were working In the Trencherbone five-foot seam were during the morning turned hack by gas, and the manager, accompanied by the four assistants, went to the place to investigate the matter They did not re- turn, and it was feared that a slight explosion of gas had taken place, as a strong smell of after-damp penetrated the working at some distance from the place to which they had gone. A search party was consequently orga- nised. At first they were driven back by the gas, hut late in the afternoon it was found possible to make a search. It was then discovered that a local explosion bad occured, and that all five men had perished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE. SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE. SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>THE CORPORATION AND THE TRADERS.</p>
        <p>We understand that a settlement has now been come to between the Corporation and the traders of War- rington upon the proposals recently agreed upon be- tween the Corporation and the Ship Canal Company. Very slight modifications have been made upon those terms, and the Company have met the wishes of the traders to some extent. They have agreed to include the Cheshire side of the river within the borough for the purpose ef the dredging of wharves and to dredge all ne, wharves which may be constructed within the next five years. The Company further undertake to dredge the river about Howley Weir if at any time it is found neces- eary to enlarge the locks at Howley and rwentystep Bridge to enable larger boats to get to Howley. The Company will not forego the charge for accumulated tolls since the Ist October last, but have consented to reduce them to the revised rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SHOW THIS TO SOME WOMAN.</head>
        <p>SHOW THIS TO SOME WOMAN.</p>
        <p>I will send a valuable. instructive, and highly interestine benk which eve-y woman would be glad to have Contains important and confidential advice and tells both married and single how to keep healthy and avoid the nxtelles so dletresßinc to the sex. Sent free, securely ealed, for one stamp to cover postage. Ard&gt;fttaa : Professor L LISALLE, 17, Eastbourne Terraee Paddington, London, W,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</p>
        <p>ADLEV.3 CONOLID IrED MINING AND LAND CORPORIuoN, LTD.</p>
        <p>Adler's Consolidated Mining and Land Corporation Limited, is a Company formed on the same lines as the Consolidated Goldfields of South Africa and kindred undertakings, to acquire and develop the interests of Mr. W. H. Adler, one of the best known men on the , Rand. The advantages of these powerful organisations with an experienced Directorate aad large capital re- sources must be obvious. Companies like the Consolid- ated Goldfields and Barnato's Consolidated are, in fact, , becoming more and, more indispensable in the develop- ment of the mineral riches of South Africa. That the investing public are awake to this fact,and eager to ta...e advantage is proved by the price which the Shares of these great organisations command in the market. Alder's Consolidated is formed t...) operate on the same lines, and the character of its assets is such that it will have interests almost identical in many respects with those o Barnato's Consolidated Company; the £1 Shares in which now stand at over £5. The assets consist of two-fifths interest in Gray's Mynpacht, of 249 claims on Farm Witpoortje (of the pre- sent market value of £60,000), and also interests in the iollowing properties, the balance of which are included 1, in the assets of the Barnato Consolidated Company, ! viz. : (a) A 11± per cent, undivided interest in 143 claims on Langiaate, immediately south of the New Croesus Gold Mining Company. (6) A fifteen per cent. undivided interest in 67 claims adjoining the above. (c) A one-tenth undivided interest in 283 claimson Vogelstruisfontein adjoining the Steynstate. (d) A fif- teen per cent, interest in 63 1-3 claim on Klippoortje. The value ot the Adler interest in these assets can be best determined by ascertaining the value of the interest held by the Barnato Consolidated Company. The capital of Adlers Consolidated is £500.000 in £1 shares and the nreser.t issue is limited to 250,000 shares of which 107,500 are for working capital and 142,500 are to be paid as the full purchase price of the properties enumerated above. The extreme moderateness of the capitalisation is shown by comparing it with the present markot value of the assets, which is about £668,750 or actually £418,750 in excess of the issued capital of the Corporation exclusive of 107,500 shares set aside for ordinary working capital. A reference to any map of the Rand will show that the Company's claims on Langlaate constitute the deep levels of the New Croesus which that Company will eventually have to acquire from Adler's Consolidated and Barna to's Consolidated. .</p>
        <p>Nor must it be supposed that tthese joint holders are, or are likely to he, in any sense, antagonistic; they have the same interests and what benefits one will benefit , both. The reefs runs right through Gray's Mynpacht, and essays have give 21 ors. to the ton, and the block, moreover, is a good shape for w rking. The Blippoortje property forms the deep levels of the East Rand Com- panies, and the value of these will, in the not very dis- tant future, be greatly enhanced. From this brief survey of the properties held by Adler's Consolidated and their future possibilities, it will be seen that they constitute assets of the greatest import- ance, and it should be observed that the terms upon which they have been acquired give the shares an actual market value of £5. It is intended by the exceeding 'strong Board of Directors associated with Mr. Adler in the management to form subsidiary companies after the manner of the Rand Mines Company and East Rand Proprietary. The position as sketched out is an exceed- ingly strong one, and as Mr. Adler will shortly return to the Transvaal to take the entire charge of the interests of the Company there is every justification for believing that its future will be an unqualified success and that the shares will reach a high level.—(Extract from " Financial News" of Sept. 27th, 1895).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>TRIUMPH (HAURAKI).</head>
        <p>TRIUMPH (HAURAKI).</p>
        <p>Triumph (Hauraki). Triumph shares have been actively dealt with, and the market has been the stronger by reason of the fact that large orders have come from New Zealand for the acquisition of these securities where their value is w, ll known. It appears that the popularity of the undertaking has extended to Paris, as one hundred thousand "Triumph were being acquired by an eminent firm of French financiers at 7s. eafth. The property covers 30 acres, which are inter- sected by reefs, the main lode which has been cut being from 10 to 50 feet in width, and from which at once can be won about a million tons of ore. Mr. Thom as, the engineer, reports that 53 tons of general quartz with 381ozs. of selected specimens gave a yield of 7ozg. of melted gold to the ton. Triumph shares now stand at 7s. and should advance to double that price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S ITIFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the seven days ended Friday, Soptember 27, 150 sea-going vessels (exclusive of plssonger steamers), with a total net registered tosaage of 40,116 tons, ar- rived at and sailed from the ‘.y.,rt of Manchester, which includes all parts of the Ship C Anal. The arrivals were over the average in number, but many were small coast- ing schooners and steamers, consequently the aggregate tonnage is somewhat below the average for the last few weeks. The number of steamers and sailing vessels (ex- clusive of pleasure steamers and barges) to the port at the end of the week was 104, of which 28 were in the Manchester and Salford Docks, 39 at Weston Point, 27 at Runcorn, five at Ellesmere Port, three at Saltport,and one each at Eastbam and Ice. Amongst the full car- goes imported during the week were fifteen of china clay and stone, eight of pig iron, six of limestone and macadam, six of wood pulp, five of flints,two of apple , one of currants, one of fustic, one of iron ore, and one of scrap iron ; whilst several vessels cleared towards with coal and salt, and one with pitch. Arrivals and Departures for the week ending September 27. Inwards. Ships. Net tonnage. Coastwise Foreign</p>
        <p>Total Outwards. __</p>
        <p>Coastwise Foreign</p>
        <p>Total Total Inwards and Outwards. Coastwise Foreign</p>
        <p>Total Several additional vessels have been fixed for the canal from foreign ports during the past week, amongst them the Parhlands (s), 1,627 tons, with general cargo from New York ; the Merannis (s), 628 tens, with iron ore from Huelva ; the Jargoon (s), 188 tons,wit' wood pulp from Christiania ; and the following sailir g vessels : Carl Frederick,9l3 tons, from Miramichi ; J. D. Everett, 1.957 tons,from West Bay, Nava Scotia ; and Emigrant, 579 tons, from Halifax, Nova Scotia (all with cargoes of timber) The Gulf of Siam (s), 2,126 tens net, and 3,446 tons gross register,has been fixed from Melbourne to Manchester direct, with general cargo, November loading. RE-ARRANGEMENTS ON THE CANAL</p>
        <p>The Bridgewater Trustees are, it is stated, contem- plating making a number of important changes at their Worsley yard. They purpose removing the forge and mechanics' shop from W orsley to Walkden, and it is pro- bable that they will also establish the depot which was at Woralev in the days of the old Duke of Bridgwater. The Manchester Ship Canal Company, it is expected, will utilise Worsley yard with a view to the relief of Runcorn. It is also reported that the trustees intend opening a foundry at Walkden, in which event employ- ment could be found for a cumber of the colliers who have been discharged from the trustees' pit. The Ship Canal Company are erecting an engine house for hydrau- lic purposes on the north-east side of Mode Wheel locks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MANCHESTER AS A COTTON PORT.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER AS A COTTON PORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. W. Macara, president of the Manchester Cotton Association, made an important announcement at a meeting of the Association held on Tuesday afternoon. Re stated that the port of Manchester had been avail- able for direct importation of cotton since the first day of last year, but although the spinners of Egyptian cot- ton had been importing a fair proportion of their con- sumption via the Manchester Ship Canal, only an infini- tesimal prowirtion of the cotton consumed by spinners of American cotton had been brought direct to Man- chester, owing to the difficulty of obtaining freight at all times on a reasonable basis. Now, however, so far as the two great shipping ports of New Orleans and Gal- veston were concerned, all such difficulties had been completely overcome. Arrangements had been made by which cotton could be shipped from those places direct to Manchester at the same price as to Liverpool. In this manner, he said, a way had been cleared for united Reim on the part of the trade. The members of the Association, of whom there was a large attendance, un- animously pledged themselves to insiAt that all their im- portations from New Orleans and Galveston should be shipped by steamer direct to Manchester, acd to do everything in their power to induce all the other spin- ners of the district to give the same pledge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LIVERPOOL WALTER POLO LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL WALTER POLO LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>THE FINAL TIE.</p>
        <p>WAILED:GTO/I T. final tie in the Livorponl and District Water Polo League was played at St. Helens on Tuesday night, and St. Helens were again returned champions. The St. Flelens team, as leaders of the A division, had defeated Sefton, the run- ners up of the B division, and Warringt,in, who were at the head o' the latter division, beat Liverpool Gymna- sium. On Tuesday night Warrington had a full team, while St. Helens lacked the services of Ripley, their county player. Mr. A. E. Birch,Southport,was referee ; Mr. F. D. Nevin, Liverpool, timekeeper ; and Messrl. Lott and Thompson goal judgeh. Warrington opened the sz..oring, early on, hut Andera almost immediately equalised, and then Sherwood, M`Kay (2), and Sharrock added goals before half time, when the score was—St. Helene 5 goals, Warrington 1 goal. In the second half play was very slow, and it was some time before a score was recorded, M‘Kay scoring his third goal. From a pass by Locker,Turner defeated Aspinall. Near the close Sharrock cleverly evaded Turner, and shot past Dixon. The final result was—St. Helens 7 goals, Warrington 2 goals.</p>
        <p>'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>TEMPERANCE MEETING AT</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE MEETING AT</p>
        <p>_ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting under the auspices of the National Temperance League, was held at the Co-operative Hall. on Tuesday evening. The Rev. " J. J. Langharn, vicar of Padgate, presided,and there were ale° present, Revs. B. Perci val, W. J. Kirkland, J. Cooil, J. Davies, E. T. Reed, 11. R. Hartwright, Messrs. W. Roberts,C. George, J. Drury,J. C. Dalton, F. R. Roberts, M.A., L.L 8., IL Taylor, P.C.M., J. Lewis Fenn, Dunnachie, tlrs. Hind Smith, Mrs. Yonge,Nlrs. H. Roberts,Mrs. Walker,Nlrs. R.Garnett, Mrs. John Fairclough, etc. The CH&amp;IRMAN said that considering the speakers which were to follow, he did not intend to speak at any great length. It was, however, incumbent upon him to say a few words explanatory of the circumstances which had led to the convening of that meeting. As many would be aware, during the present week the National Temperance Congress was being held at Chester. That, of course, was held under the auspices of the National Temperance League, the head quarters of which were held in London, and the president of which was the Bishop of London. The Congress Was held at various centres and its object wes educational, a very simple, a very necessary, and a very important object, for what- ever their individual views might be as to the temper- ance question, of one thing he was certain, that no reform that might be introduced by the legislation would be a practical one, unless concurrently with its introduc- tion the people of this country were fully agreed and in sympathy with it. It was impossible for them to march in advance of public opinion,thereforc the educa- tionel aspect of the temperance question was one of, he might almost say, the most important aspects with which they had to deal. The temperance question touched their social life at almost every conceivable point. There were six sections and a mere recapitula- tion of the subjects discussed by the Congress sufficed to bring home in a remarkable manner the importance of that great question. That night he understood the sub- ject was the moral aspect of temperance. Of the moral aspect of temperance, they all knew some- thing. They might realise at all events that intemper- ance was amonget the hindrances to morality, but he believed that they could go still further and that it would be brought home to them that not only was intemperance a hindrance to morality, but that even what was con- sidered as moderate drinking was very often a hindrance to morality—(applause)—because in order that a min might remain mend, he must have absolute control of himself, and it was well-known that there was many a man who did not take drink to excess, who would at- tempt to do what he would never have done in his sober senses. It was the skipper who was just three sheets in the 'wind who was liable to put on a little bit too much sail. This educational question was a most important one, and he hoped that the Congress would bring home to the people the fact that no educa- tion would be complete which did not inculcate the duty of self control. Of course as the Congress was under the presidency of Sir B. W. Richardson, the scientific aspect was a point upon which much would be said, and what the nation required Was to know what experts had to say about alcohol. It was not a question about which individual doctors had to be asked,who while they might be excellent men, in regard to the drink question were half a century behind the times. (Applause.) The nation wanted to know what the modern scientist had to say about alcohol, what had those experts who had devoted most of their lives to the consideration of alcohol had to r say about its effect upon the human bode ,both in health and in disease. Then there was the question of drink in relation to the community. They knew that there were many children who were born into the world more or less handicapped in the race of life—children who were born of drunken parents—children who so to speek had been brought up from their birth in intoxicating liquor ' —children with a hereditary taint. It was a most seri- ous question to Englishmen that the children of to-day became the rulers of to-morrow. Could nothing be done for those children ? There ought to be a wholesome in- fluence which could be exerted upon them in their own homes. (Applause.) There were also the industrial, commercial, and statistical aspects of temperance. Those were points which would specially appeal to those who lived in such manufacturing towns as Warrington. He supposed that there was no one who did not realise the immense commercial loss that accrued from exces- sive drinking. (Applause.) Many of the people in that place would know many cases of those who kept what was known as " Saint Monday." That was a loss not only to the labourer from the way in which he squan- dered his money, but it was also a loss to the employer, because the labourer could not take his place owing to his having taken intoxicating drink in excess. These who had at heart the commercial wel- fare of their country, would feel the question worthy of study. Then there was the legislative ques- tion which would be discussed, and of which there I would be contrary and diverse opinions from the many points of view of temperance reformers, There were questions which they would be agreed upon, such as the limiting of the sale of intoxicating liquors to very young children (applause), the question of the registration of clubs (hear, hear), the question of what was to be done to habitual drunkards. (Hear, hear.) It was recog- nised that a drunkard became in time diseased, and temperance reformers thought that those who had lost the power of self control, should for a shcrt time be compulsorily confined for medical treatment. All those points should be considered by every earnest temperance reformer, and by every earnest Englishman. They needed education upon the matters which deserved care- ful and earnest study. He hoped that they would be benefitted that night by the speakers and would form their own ideas upon this most important question. (Applause.) The Chairman further announced that he had received letters of apology for non-attendance from the Rev, J. Yonge, the Rev. W. H. Drummond, Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., and Mr. S. B. Stringer. Mrs. BIND SMITH, president of the Y.W.C.A. Total Abstinence Union, urged the great influence of total abstinence in the homes of the people. It would be a great comfort for parents to feel that parental influence had heel all on the right side, and that no child could quote its parents as having been the means of causing it to take intoxicants. She was president of the Y.W.C.A. Total Abstinence Union, and she hoped that the branch in Warrington would do some good work. Good work could only be the result of prepara- tion. They should keep in touch with the people and try to influence them in their every day homes. They should hold meetings so as to let the people know that they were still alive. They were told that because the Local Ve'o Bill was defeated that they were done with. The chairman had told then that they could not legislate ahead of public opin- ion, and they must try to educate the people up to the level of the Local Veto Bill. She was sorry that she did not see the blue ribbon there that night. She look upon it as daring to make their purpose known. Everyone knew the blue ribbon and what it meant. She also asked them not to be deceived by doctors. She did not believe that strong drink was good even as a medicine. (Ap- plause.) Mr. Taos Hutment's of the Scottish Temperance League said that wes the first time he had spoken before an English audience on the Temperance question. Be had spoken in Scotland upwards of 2,000 times, but had never had the honour of crossing the borders to speak on that subject. He was a total abstainer of 85 years' standing, and he reu,,nbered very well when the tem- perance movement began, and when his father and mother and he and two brothers became associated with it in 1830. The speaker went on to speak in an interest- ing and amusing strain on the med;cal aspect of the tem- perance question, and before con4ludlog again expressed the pleasure it gave !dm to come and speak there, Mr. Fasne,naval agent of the National Temperance League, said that the National Temperance League was one of the oldest of their British Temperance Associa- tions. Its work had been educational, and it had sought to educate all sorts and conditions of men and women. It had tried, and successfully too, to educate minister and clergyman. It had something to do with the formation of the Church of Eng- land Temperance Society, and was a parent of the Baptist and Congregational Total Abstinence Associations. (Applause.) It had been for more than a quarter of a century hard at work among the doctors. Every year, wherever the Modica4 Congress was held, they put in an appearance, and it was one of his (the speaker's) pleasant duties to buttonhole one of the doc- tors and have a chat with him. They attributed to a very large extent the change in the tone of medical men to their endeavours,and that able scientist,the President of the Chester Congrese that week, was one of their converts—(applause)—and if they had done nothing else but set to work the string which brought him into their work the League would have been deserving of the , thanks of temperance reformers of all time. (Applause.) The speaker then proceeded to speak of the temperance work being carried on in the navy, and in the conclud- ing passage of his address said that the way to regene- rate Warrington was not to get up and look about for some great thing to do, but to do these things which Were nearest to thein and to do them well.</p>
        <p>During the course of the eveni.'g glees were rendered by Mrs. Joseph Monks, jun.. Miss Ashler. and Messrs. S. Millington and S. Astle. The latter also sang "Lskes of Killarney," in an efficient manner. Formal votes of thanks were dispensed with, hut the Chairman thanked the speakers from Chester for their attendance.</p>
        <p>MORE GREAT CURES of torturing, disfiguring skin, scalp, and blood humours are daily made by CUTICURA remedies than by all ether skin Nnd blood remedies com- bined. The best /ohs sicians endorse and prescribe them. Chemists everywhere recommeud them. They may be, safely used from the moment of birth, and are guaran- teed absolutely pure by the highest medical autivaities whose certificates accompany every package. They are sweet, gentle, and effective, and especially appeal to mothers, nurses, and children, as wtql as to the refined 4nd cultivated everywhere. They cleanse the system by internal and external medication af every eruption and impurity, ant' constitute the most effective skin cures, , ' blood purifiers, and humour remedies of modern times.</p>
        <p>TRE LOYAL ADMIRAL NELSON LODGE OF ODDFELLOWS (MANCHESTER UNITY).</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF PAST OFFICERS' CERTIFICATES.</p>
        <p>IThere was a largely attended nr..--7;,4 of Oddfellowe of the Warrington DL'tnc at th, Lodge meeting of the Loyal Admiral Nelson Lodge,held on Monday evening,to witness the presentation by D.G.M. Samuel Turner, of Bury,on behalf of the Dodie,of Pos:. Odicers' Certifi3aces to P.P.Ci.lit's W. Richardson, G, Webster, J. T. Perry, Jahn Ellison, George Perry, Frank Webster, and P.G.F. J. Emson. Proy. G.M. Albin tbyle occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>_ . The Cuatamix submitted the toast of the " Queen and the Royal Family," whicn was received rig= ['roc, C.S. JOHN EMSON proposed the toast of the Manchester Unity and the Board of Directors, and coupled with it the name of the Grand Master. The speaker also referred in eulogistic terms to the work of the D.G.M.in the Bury district in raising various lodges from a state of insolvency to one of solvency. D.G.M. SAMUEL TURNER said it always gave him pleasure to be amongst 0 idfellows, and particularly Lancashire Oddrellovcs, and to find the good feeling that existed amongst them, as exempl,fied by presentations similar to those that were to be made that evening. His Grand Master, whose name had been coupled with the toast, was entitled to every word of praise that had been spoken of him. He was a persevering and painstaking man, and one who had the unity at heart. (flear,hear.) They had reason to feel proud of the uaity. It had been founded in their county about 85 years ago, and it had gone through the dark ages of its existence successfully. He mentioned the names ot Mr. Henry Radcliffe, of Chowbent, I..P.G.M.Hardy,and P P.G M. Samuel Doan, of Norwich, who had done yeoman service for the order in those dark days in various ways. Theirs was the largest society in the world,and he urged them to endea- vour to still increase its membership by trying t ) per- suade othors to join. The increase in m anb •rship during 1894 was 16,724, and the total woollier of adult members on the 14 January, 1895, 739,449. They were, therefore, progressing. Daring the past 30 years, the increase in memliership had bean 365,940. In Lancashire there had been a &amp;crease in membership during the last twelve years of 1,554. This was not very satisfactory, but they had had a hard up-hill battle Is fight. There was, years s silver lining, for taking the last four there had been an increase of 2.568 members. He was glad to know that in Warrington they were endeavour_ Rig to show them how to " turn the corner "in this res- pect. In that ledge they were anxious to get themselves in line ready for action, to solve the great question that would-soon have to be solved. He asked them as practi- cal men of business not to be led away with castles in the air, but to stick to their own work, and to tell those who would endeavour to lead them a way " Mind your own business. lam at present very busy." (Applause.) Let them keep a neutral platform, and not bother one party or the other. The juvenile branch of the society was of great benefit to the order in increasing its mem- bership. The funds of the society amounted to over 84 million pounds. Their order was a monument of skill and industry, and they were anxious that others should come in and share their benefits. (Applause.) He asked them to use every endeavour to keep it to the front,and to build up a society that would still be more useful in the future than it had ever been in the past. (Loud applause.) The DEPUTY GRAND MASTER then made the presenta- tion of the certificates to the members mentioned, and in doing so said it was a pleasing duty, and he trusted that every member in the district would do all in his power to push forward the work of Oicifellowship. RICHARDSON replied. . .•</p>
        <p>PG., W. HOLBROOK proposed the toast of the Warring- ton District, to which Prov. G.M. ALBAN BOYLE res- ponded. Bro. G. MATER submitted the Admiral Nelson Lodge. P P.G.M., FRAN% WEBSTER responded. Other toasts tollowed.</p>
        <p>Songs were given during the evening by N.G. Baldry, P. G. Ashton, V. G. Barnwell, Bros. Peter Bate and W. W. Whittle, and P.G. Jackson. Bro. Bate presided at the piano in an efficient marner. N.G. Scorr proposed a comprehensive vote of thanks to the D.G.M., the Chairman, singers, Arc., and an en- joyable gathering was brought to a conclusion by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>DESTRUCTIVE STORIf ON THE WEST</head>
        <p>DESTRUCTIVE STORIf ON THE WEST</p>
        <p>COAST.</p>
        <p>HEROIC ATTEMPTS TO SAVE LIFE.</p>
        <p>Early on Monday morning a violent north-westerly gale sprang up, causing great destruction of shipping and considerable loss of life on the west and north•west coast of Er.gland. The ketch Arabella was observed ira distress off Pfracombe, and a father and son natird Souch put off in a boat and rescued the crew of four. ITheir boat was, however, in turn swamped, and all six nin were drowned. A Penzance schooner was swamped off Bull Point, Ilfracombe, and five men were drowned. The captain put on a lifebelt, and after being tossed about in the sea for hours was cast ashore on the Lee Bay rcks. A party of coastguardsmen and others made strenuous endeavours to rescue him from his position, and one of the party descended a cliff 150 feet high by means of a rope and administered restoratives ; hut the unfortunate man was too exhausted to be re- moved by the rocks,and after three hours' work it was found necessary to summon the services of the lifeboat. The man was ultimately-landed at Ilfracombe, but only survived a few minutes. The storm was felt with great violence on the Lancashire coast, and several fishing boats, a Preston pilot boat, and a Russian barque were wrecked. The crews were saved except in one instance. A boat which it is believed had on board two men was swamped near the end of the Central Pier extension at Blackpoc,l and before any assistance could be rendered the men were drowned. The Russian barque was wrecked on the Horse Bank near Southport. The South- port and the Lytham lifeboats were launched, and tha former succeeded in taking off the crew. TM IFlnylake lifeboat rescued two men. Several Lancashire fishing boats were reported missing on Monday night, and the fate of their crews is not known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WARRINGTONIANS 1N AMERICA,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTONIANS 1N AMERICA,</p>
        <p>A COMING OF AGE CELEBRA I 10'..;1.</p>
        <p>We extract the following item of news from "The' Brooklyn Citizen" of September 6th z—The home of Mr. and Mrs. S. Thompson, was, on Wednesday evening, the 4th inst., the scene of a very pleasant celebration of the coming of age of Mr. Thomas P. Throssell, who came over to this country from Warrington, England, some two and a half years ago, and has secured the good will and esteem of a large circle of friends and acquaintances. That which gave the most interest to the occasion, per- haps, was the fact that Mr. and Mrs. Throsqell and their young son, Stanley, respectively the father, mother and brother of Mr. Throssell, had made a journey from their home on the other side of the Atlantic to be present,and to manifest the affection- ate regard in which they holt their son and brother. A most excellent supper was privided, and full justice was done to it Over the walnut, and wine a number of toasts were proposed and heartily drunk, due respect being paid to the President of the United States, and also to the Queen of Great Britain, under whose aegis a large proportion of the friend., gathered together either were or had been at one time or another. The health of Mr. T. P. Throssell and of his parents was proposed, and suitable and feeling replies made to that, as to the other toasts customary on such occasions. With music and song a most enjoyable gathering was kept up until an early hour.</p>
        <p>Thc Chinese Government, according to a telegram. frmm St.Peteriburg,has decided to reoteanise the navy. Both /thins and material are to be ordered abroad.</p>
