        <p>TB E EXA MINER, SATURDAY OCTOBER 5, 1895.</p>
        <p>SAVE 25 PER -CENT. FURNISH. AT WORSLET'S. "THE PANTHEON. GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER. ESTABLISHED 51, YEARS. SHOWROOMS COVERING NEATILY ONE MILLION SQUARE FEIII</p>
        <p>THE MOST COLOSSAL FURNISHING ESTAIILI NMSNP</p>
        <p>IN THE NORTH. SPECIALITE: HIGH-CLASS SECOND-HAND FURNITURE. FURNITURE by "JAMES LAMB, HENRY OGDEN and SONS. GOODALLS; GILLOWS of Lancaster, GARNETTS of Warrington, CUTTER of Liverpool, SHOOL BRED of London, STARKEY &amp; MELD of Altrincham, CHAMBERLAIN of Birmingham, and other High-clsss lio,ises. TERMS CASH OR DEFERRED PAYMENTS. All goods are advertised at cutting prices, being in most cases 50 per cent, below ordinary city prices for cash. If by deferred payments, no differ nce is made in the invoice price. but a slight per centage is charged according to the length of credit desired As compared with the hire system this saves from 103 to 2.0 percent.</p>
        <p>WrBBHLY CHAT.—SECOND-HAND FURNITURF.— Benjamin Franklin said a penny saved is a r enny made. This is an obvious truism, and ought to be borne in mind by all, rich and poor. But at" The Pantheon" it is not only a question of saving pennies, but ,1 pounds. House Furnishing Is one of the important epochs in every family, well deserving careful thought. If you can economise with- out detriment to quslity and stability, then by all means do so, and you cannot practice this theory better than by paying a visit to' The Pantheon," which will cost nothing, but profit you much. One of the most interesting sights in the Fur- nishing World is • • The Pantlio.n." a busy hive of indastry, containing without tear of contradiction, th es to four times the stock of any two of the largest furnishing houses in Man- chester. The bulk of this stock is High-class Second-hand Ferniture '3y high-class makers, at quarter the price of the poor miserable, veneered orange hex furniture from the Bast , End of London, now so largely retailed by drapers. Beware of purchasing London Furniture ; it lacks stability and fiuish. A Good Second-hand Article is worth a hundred of spurious imitations of furniture made in East London. Worsley's can supply you with all you require in furnishing, by such makers as Lambs. Gillows, or Garnet, at prices simply pul- verising competition. For instance, as a simple bargAin you can obtain a Fine Sideboard by Dovestons which cost EB5, for £l6 ; what chance has the competition of new furniture against bargains like these, If you go to an auction room and buy you take the risk yourself as to whether your pur- chases are sound and durable, but at Worsley's you have no risk, Worsley's absolutely taking all risk, guarantee every article for ten years. and will take any article or articles back not giving satisfaction. It will cost you nothing to call at "The Pantheon,"and see for yourselves ; you can inspect wit hoot feeling compelled to purchase; they will be delighted to show you round whether you buy or not. Under these circumstances you will be guilty of criminal, wilful neglig- ence of your own interests if you neglect visiting Worsiey's before deciding where ti Furnish.</p>
      </div>
