        <head>EXA M iNEIt</head>
        <p>THE EXA M iNEIt</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER sth, 1895,</p>
        <p>—.------r-----1 The Temperance m la Congress. Temperance Congress he'-übt Chester this week will have been THE proceedings at the Isratioe.a. followed with close attention by tui% - who are interested in the great drink cluest'; The subject of temperance has been dealt wl a , practically in all its varied aspects by Bien an ; women who are thoroughly entitled to b a ut probably the emphatic pronouncement° 4.be eminent doctors will on the whole attract '' v i most attention. But the doctors did not tY goll'a means monopolise the whole of the tiolo•tor in , n faca ~ 1 Tuesday, for instance, "The .Press as temperance work" was dealt with atio Mr. J. R. DIGGLE the late Chaiirrc ::ff ` ' of the London School Board, rebbea some old scores against the editors and proPo tors of newspapers—of the daily newspapers I particular. Mr. DIGGLE was not comPlirae,rl„,tgargl i. heariabg; to the Press, yet the Press with its lona' nanimity gave the fullest publicity to his criticishleat . and imputations. Mr. DIGGLE is of ePini°ll tf a ' I the newspapers do not take much notice °coin g '. , movement till it has reached a reat and a ; mending stage. "The writer," he added, "hd P's soul of his own. He had to write up to the soul,and the editor to that of the proPriet°r',-heof for the proprietor himself he chiefly th°ll"-this what would pay." It may be gathered from es, , deliverance that Mr. DIGGLE is one of those sggr,nd , sive personages who have a weakness for Cr. other people's business while perhaPs IlOtf 00 mindful of their own. When Mr. Die°l- to chairman of the London School Board he „ilts, "faithfully "dealt with, according to b his e 1,,„" ojt y the metropolitan press, but he is not gra' • a Or. i rand now that he is relieved of the onerous --u to 1 appertaining to the chairmanship °f or important public body he seems, as We have nreS- gested, to be bent upon rubbing off eld sc-sved The Rev. S. WEavax, of Liverpool, who fohllore Mr. DIGGLE in the debate, took a mtle-f news' sensible view of the position and duties 04 -press papers when he remarked that if the to see "excluded many things they should Po, excluded, most of their newspapers Would tically die, and they should make in that vi,uY the sporting journal the mightiest PaPer 'Allay&amp; country. Whether that would be for the 4'lo be age of the minds and morals of the PeZ pot would leave that meeting to judge. il° ,71, for at present think it was a practical Pel'," they newspapers to exclude some things wh!°: 01 gave publicity to. Newspapers must ilv".4801 they must in some sense meet the current '- of the time." rl neat Turning to the other business of the --° kable no one can help being struck by the vow° f the medical testimony that was given in faouf.:9oo 1 temperate use of, or rather the entire ab°l3-00,1, ' from, alcoholic drinks. Sir B. W. B, isot who speaks from the experience of a life4l/33- rage only condemned the use of alcohol as a bevede 01 but even as a drug in the hands of 11;100 men. He had, he said, witnessed the trea'd be of diseases with alcohol and without it, anotit ', was bound to say that the results of the witht oof ~ had been unquestionably the better. ge 1,1°,4MA ~ maintained that alcohol was never neetift" 7c „go . as a drug, but that even amongst drugs 1 jot . more hurtful than many others, such us.t,, „lessor chloroform, quinine and ammonia. “- WILLIAM CARTER, of Liverpool, ia a Pas ora 0 44 "The practical benefits of abstinence 'I. or ' physician's point of view" was even veuruspsofi• . phatic in his condemnation than Dr. BO° f cielv .. He claimed that from the medical Point °atoll ' three broad general facts had now been je0 ,bo ` established, namely, first, that those Poo° ,get ' 1 ' entirely abstain from alcohol than live A that gen 039 1 those who use it even moderately; 0011.9 abstainers work longer and harder "170°11 ' moderate drinkers; and, third, that int°, was es energy could be as well sustained and sorrii great without as with alcohol. Stni" ted 10 ; and Dr. JOHNSON are not usually tlu° witty or ' t Prdeis support of temperance arguments, ye ..„ _ CARTER used them both very effectively. 17 „ Det ' clergyman once wrote to LADY HOLLA-2")bStal v .---. •131tja me state some of the good arising from a _, sleep from all fermented liquors. First, Bweeb</p>
