      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>!laying never known what sweet sleep was, I sleep like a baby, or ploughboy. If I wake, no needless terrors, no black visions of life, hut pleasing hopes and pleasing recollectious ; Holland House, past 411(1 to come! If I dream, it is not of lions and tigers but of Easter dues and tithes. Secondly, I °an take longer walks and make greater exertions without fatigue. My understanding is improved, aud I comprehend Political Economy. • • . 4341 Y one evil ensues [rein it. lam in such extra- /agant spirits that I must lose blood or look out for someone who will bore and depress me." Dr. Jonxsor's declaration in favour of temperance was made with epigrammatic force and conciseness. 4A man may choose whether he will have ahstem- kilaness and knowledge or claret and ignorance," 411 d) again," This is one of the disadvantages of ,swine,it makes a man mistake words for :rofessor CARTER also gave some very instructive formation upon the results of increased facilities far drinking. There are people who argue that the I:.liminishing of the number of public-houses or of the hours which these houses may remain open has 1140 effect upon the drunkenness of a community, lit Professor CARTER'S researches have led him to directly opposite conclusion. He has found that 'under every variety of conditions, this general asult had invariably followed —that exactly in t2'loPUrtioll as the facilities for obtaining drink had that eau increased so had drunkenness increased, and in exact proportion as drunkenness aci increased had there been an in- ease in poverty, disease, crime, and death." !lid, further, "a curtailment of the time of drink ?3' a single hour led to a considerable curtailment 14 the number of broken heads, disfigured faces, 4ad other casualties of a similar kind." Amongst the other medical men who have read papers at v ,a° Congress are Dr. VACTIER, medical officer of aealth for Cheshire, who declared that the "old lief that alcohol gave strength or cot:served it „„,cilind no place in scientific teaching"; and Dr. "Atatst.nit, medical superintendent of the Metro- P_ olitan District Idiot Asylum, whose statements °Lit the subject of drunkenness and lunacy should have the gravest consideration. In London alone 13_ 4aPer lunatics are increasing at the rate of 500 a 7ear,thus making it necessary to erect a new asylum every four years at the cost of half a million, 4141, d, according to Dr. WALMSLEY, "alcohol is .IrectlY, or indirectly, responsible for more mental "Ild physical incapacity and suffering than any ether single known cause of disease."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>A PROTRACTED MEETING.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Board-room at the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening. Mr. Pritchard occupied the chair, and there were also present :—Revs. Canon Wood, and Dr. Gastaldi, Dr. McDougall, Mr. E. Imisou, J.P., Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., and Mr. Moss (clerk.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LATE MR. HANDLEY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. HANDLEY.</p>
        <p>The Chairman,- la-eKie-i-he;C-ommencement of the pro- ceedings, said he desired to refer to the loss the Board and town generally had sustained in the death of Mr. Thomas Handley,who held several public positions in his native town. Ile inight state that he had known Mr. Handley for many years, even from his boyhood,and his sterling character and conduct heel been such as to gain the esteem of all who knew !ern. It was unnecessary for him to refer to the manner in which Mr. Handley dis- charged his public duties in other places ; it was suffi- cient to say that he was held in the highest respect by his fellow townsmen as was manifested by the universal expression of regret at the time of his removal by death. But it was his melancholy duty to speak of him as a col- league of theirs and the way in which he took part in the progress of edu,•ation of the young people of Run- , corn. He was constant in his attendance, was a usetul member at their meetings,and took a deep interest in the work of the Board. He was a man of sound judgment and of straightforward conduct, and always supported measures that were of a progressivecharacter,and which in his opinion were for the welfare of th young. He formed an independent judgment and was true to his convictions, but was always courteous and kind to those who differed from him. When a valuable and useful life was thus cut short it created a sad vacancy and he was sure the deep and sincere sympathy of the members of the Board would be extended to the wife and family in the dark cloud of sorrow that bad befallen them. He moved that the Board desired to express its deep sorrow at the loss the Board had sustained by the death of one of its members, the late Mr. T, Handley, J.P., who by his unfailing urbanity and kindness had secured the highest esteem and affection of every member, and that the Clerk be instructed to convey to Mrs. Handley the sincere sympathy of the members with her and her family in their great bereavement.—The Rev. Canon Wood said he would like to second that resolution. It was unnecessary for him after what had been said to add anything in the nature of a eulogy, but in-so-much as the late Mr.Handley was found on the opposite side to himself he could say that whilst they constantly differed in opinion, he never failed to recognise in him a sincere and most courteous and pleasant opponent whilst a member of that Board.—Dr. McDougall said he felt sure it would be very difficult to fill the place which Mr. Handley so admirably occupied, or to find one so well qualified to fill the vacancy. He knew Mr. Handley as a personal friend,and became acquainted with him when he (Dr. McDougall) first came to Runcorn. His death came as a painful surprise, although not altogether unex- pected by him (Dr. McDougall). No one he knew carried a better balanced mind than Mr. Handley, who was always ready to credit others with sincerity in their efforts.—Mr. Imison said no one felt mote keenly the loss of Mr. Handley than himself, for they lived under the same roof for four years. He could speak of the sincere desire Mr. Handley had to promote the public good, and however muck they differed from him in opinion, the 7 were bound to admit that he acted with pure motives. The town had lost by his death a very valuable servant, and they a very valuable colleague.—Mr. Taylor endorsed all that had been said that night with refer- ence to the late Mr. Handley. He was always courte- ous, and always agreed to differ with those who held other opinions than himself. He was sure he gained the respect of all who knew him.—The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi said he had much pleasure in corroborating all that had been said of the loss the Board had sustained in the death of Mr. Handley, and also to express his deep sym- pathy with the family in their bereavement. Mr. Handley was a man whom he had known in his capacity as member of that Board for many years and he could honestly say that he was a sincere, good, and virtuous gentleman, and always ready to do a good action to anyone in need.—The resolution was passed unani- mously. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BCHOOL A.TTENDANCE -ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>BCHOOL A.TTENDANCE -ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the School Attendance Committee stated that 32 persons were summoned before the Com- mittee, and 27 appeared. Proceedings before the m igis- trates were ordered to be taken against six persons for failing to send their children regularly to school.—The annual report of the Committee showed the work done under the direction of the Committee during the past year, also the state of the attendance at tht. public elementary schools as compared with the preceding year. Warning notices served by visitors numbered 4,009 as compared with 3.992 during the proceding year. Parents summoned before the Committee totalled 552 against 526. There were 98 prosecutions before the magistrates as compared with 122. The average number on the rolls was 3,440, in the preceding year 3,536, decrease 96, and the average attendance 2,787 against 3,038 during the previous year, being a decrease of 251. The per- centage of attendance was 81, preceding year 83, decrease 2. The foregoing figures showed a general falling off in the numbers of children attending school during the past year. The deerease in the average number on the rolls was caused by removal of families from the town. The' decrease in the average attendance was attributable to the distress which prevailed amongst the working classes during the winter, and which brought down the attendance considerably, and the recent epidemic of measles had a disastrous effect on the attendance of infants, bringing down the average attendance for July to 520 less than that for May. With regard to the number of persona summoneel,the committee were reluctant to send parents to the police court. The percentage of attendance at the whole of the schools during the year was 81 against 83 last year. Boys made 864 per cent., girls, 798, and infants 76.4. The latter was five per cent, below last year, it being the infants mainly who were affected by the epidemic of measles. No children had been sent to industrial schools during the past year, but one girl had been sent to the Liverpool School for the Blind. The Board was now responsible for two boys at the Bishop Brown Industrial School, Stockport, two girls and one boy at the Liverpool school for the blind and one boy at the school foi the deaf, Boston Spa.</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of the report, Canon Wood re- ferred to the decrease in the number of children attend- ing school,and stated that it cenld only be expected that there would be a falling off, considering the nurni4er of people who had been obliged to find work elsewhere. He thought it showed the wisdom of the Board in deciding not to extend their school accommodation when some suggested that it should be. Ile believed at the present time there were places for 600 more children than were wanted. With regard to the reduced number of prose- cutions, the committee thought it inadvisable to prose- cute people when they knew they had no money to pay the fines. Regarding the decrease in the percentage, he did not think it was to be attributed to want of effort or attention on the part of the officers, but that it was due mainly to the epidemic of measles, and partly to poverty. Considering all the circumstances, the aver- age had been very well sustained. There was one other point he would like to mention as that was the last annual report before the dissolution of the Board, and that was with reference to the working of the School Attendance Committee. At present the committee was working in two divisons ; one-half met one week, and the other the next. The work was therefore not carried on continuously, and he thought it would be better if the work was left to two or three persons to undertake it regularly.—The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi said the Board had not taken proper steps to , prevent children from absenting themselves from school lon Mondays and Friday afternoons. He thought they should adopt some means to remedy this evil. If they did so they would have a great increase in the average attendance.—The Chairman said there seemed to be one or two items in the report which were not quite satis- sactory, but he thought the explanations given were sufficient. They bad had an exceptional year. With regard to the objection of Dr. Gastaldi, he was of the opinion that it was a matter which should be largely taken into consideration by the head teacher of the re- spective schools. He thought when the visitors obtained eight attendances out of ten, it was for the teachers to endeavour to work up that deficiency. If they did so it would help considerably in the averages.—Mr. Imison thought teachers did the best they could to make the scholars attend, but there was a point beyond which they could not go, and in Runcorn they appeared to have reached it.—The report was then adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SCEOOL MANAGEMENT. COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCEOOL MANAGEMENT. COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The School Mangement Committee reported that at their last meeting the Clerk presented the annual report of the Wesleyan school for the year ending June 30'h last. The total amount of the grant earned was £490 17s. 63. from which amount the Board was to hand over to the late managers the total amount of their net outstanding Habil ties on current account.—The minutes were passed un the motion of Mr. Imison, seconded by the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SCIENCE AND _ART CLASSES.</head>
        <p>THE SCIENCE AND _ART CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual report of the Science and Art Com- mittee was submitted. It stated that the students at- tending the classes numbered 152 against 163 in the preceding session. Although there had been a small diminution in the total number of students on the regis- ter it might still be said that the classes had on the whole been well maintained and done good work throughout the session. The result of the examination of the Science and Art Department were given. The pro- portion of success obtained in science and art was not as high as could be wished, but it compared favourably with last year. This work of the Committee was now practically at an end as in the ensuing session the classes were transferred to the Technical Institute under the management of the Runcorn Urban District Council. The whole of the models and scientific apparatus pur- chased by the Committee had been handed over to the Technical Instruction Committee. There now remained only the financial business to wind up. The grant from London had to be received, and the salaries to teachers, and ether small items to be paid. A further subsidy would be required from the Council but it would be well to defer applying for it until the exact amount required had had been ascertained. The income including £lBO from the rates was £259 17s. sd. The balance at present owing was £39 15s.—Dr. McDougall in moving the adop- tion of the report commented on the cv•rk which had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>~.,.,'..i:'r'4,-i'.:'.•',.i.,;••:,.1'1...'.`;'''..',..• ',.'l',' 7!'.:-',.....;..!.•-',...•,i,::!:,;::;,1.4:;','„,,.,.;t7,•ai:7,.:;,.'''':i'!-.,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
