        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>EXAMINER SATUR Y, OCTOI3BR 5, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATUR Y, OCTOI3BR 5, 1895.</p>
        <p>been done. He trusted that the work at the Tec.'hnical Institute would he more successfully carried on there, as ' no doubt it would be, because the students had an in- centive there that probably they might have thought they had not at the Board's cia,ses.—Dr. Gastaldi seconded the adoption of the report.—The Chairman said the science and art classes were no part of the duties of the Board. It was a voluntary work on their part, but he thougnt that the four yeara they conducted them,. might be regarded as of a satisfactory nature. They had laid the foundation of the Technical Institute, and those who attended their classes, he understood, had largely availed themselves of the increased facilities which were to be found in the Technical Institute—Mr. Imison said it was rather unfortunate that Dr. McDlu- gall's position under the Urban Council deprived him from attending on the Technical Instruction Committee. He had taken a great interest in such education, and no, small amount of the success ot the science classes was owing to Dr. McDougall. He hoped that although not on the Committee his interest would be maintained, and that at some future date he would have an opportunity of distributing the prizes.—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>A LITTLE BREEZE. Dr. McDougall introduced the subject of the proposed extensions of Greenway-road Bard School, stating that he was desitceis that the public should become acquainted with the project. He was about to explain the plans when several members objected to the subject being pro- ceeded with.—Mr. Taylor stated that when the plans were last before the Committee they decided that on the next oecasion they were considered the architect should be present.—Dr. McDougall con- tended that he had a right to bring the matter before the attention of the Board, as it had been placed on the agenda.—Mr. Imison said that in order to set the Board right he would move that the plans be considered in committee.—Dr. McDougall having asked the Clerk to produce the plans, again rose, and in a heated manner said the whole question had been before the members since early in 1893. It was not competent for the Board to take such an extreme course as to move the closure because there was a diverse opinion respecting the scheme.—Several of the members having risen from their seats and about to leave the room, when the Chairman said there was other business to be transacted, and declined to hear Dr. McDougall furthe•.. If he de‘er-d he could introduce the subject when the ocher business had been transacted.—At the conclusion of the business,some time afterwards, the Chairman saia it was now open for Dr. McDougall to reopen the subj,ct,but the Dr. declined stating that he would take another course to make his extension scheme known to the public. He did not know anyone who had been more hostile to les views than t,he Chairman.—Mr. Taylor said if any good would come out of a discussion of the plans he would not object to sitting there another hour but knowing the feeling of four of the members pre sent he did not think it advis- able. It would be better to communicate with the architect, in order that he might attend to their next meeting.—Dr. McDongall said the matter had been before them so long that it was time something definite was accomplished. They were no further forward beyond getting the transfer of the Wesleyan schoolsthan in 1893. —The Chairman said he quite agreed that it was an important matter, but he was sure the plans had not been brought before the Board before.—Dr. McDougall : As a matter of fact the plans had been before the Board, and you will find you were present en that occasion.— Arrangements were made for a meeting to be held next Tuesday afternoon for the consideration of the plans,the architect to be present.</p>
        <p>baroain.—Mr.Aaron seconded the proposition,and it was carried.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywood moved the adoption of the Finance Committee's report, stating that after the cheques sub- mitted for salaries and various accounts had been paid, there would he a balance in hand of £,3663.—Mr.Wood- ward seconded, and the report was adopted. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>corn ? (Hear, hear.) Mr. 11. H. Posnett seconded the resolution. —Mr. IViewa opposed the motion, and said Dr. Gastaldi had made so cock-sure about the matter that it was hardly necessary to have a committee formed A committee was appointed to deal with this question in January, 1893. That committee sat and carefully studied the question, arid it was afterwards decided not to go further with the matter. The proposition reminded him of the saying that "fools rush in where angels fear to tread." (Laughter.) He did not wish, however, to say that the rev, gentleman came within that catagory. (bauguter.)—Rev. Dr. Gastaldi : Shall I answer him.. (Laughter.)—The Chairman : You will have an opportunity presently. (Laughter.) —Mr. Wiswall costinuing said he objected to their time be- in g taken up by the consideration of such a project on such a bald staterntut. There was really nothing to work upon; it was sitnply a Mere assertion with nothing to back it up. It •WAS brought before them by what he might say were " amateurs," and he hoped that on the next occasion such a motion was brought before them, it would be done in a more practical manner —Mr.Tic lor said he was not going to oppose the motion of appointing a committee.fle could not go so tar as that. He was sure if it could be shown the Council that it would be an advantage to the ratepayers to have the electric light it would he adopted. The matter had been thoroughly threshed out I hree years ago, hut he was not going to say that something more might not come of it now.—Mr. Bisbrown thought they should treat Dr. Gastaldi with respect and appoint a committee to con- sider the 'matter. It was no new question for the Coun- cil, but the members would be more satisfied, and the public would be satisfied, even if nothing came of the deliberations of the committee. As already stated, a committee had gone into the matter very fully, but ultimately when the matter was brought before the Commissions, it was defeated by eight votes to four. It was considered that the time was not opportune, but he was in favour of another committee being appointed in order that the matter could be gene into exhaustively.— Mr. Wilson said the mover of the motion had stated deliberately that a saving of sd. in the £ could be affected and other advantages gained. He thought the matter really deserved consideration, and in the interests of all, they should certainly agree to the appointment of a committee, and do their best to get to the bottom of the subject. There were other illuminants besides electricity which were coming to the fore, and he hoped they would try, if possible, to obtain some relief for the ratepayers. —The Chairman said tie bad allowed a good deal of irrelevant matter to be introduced, for the reason that he did not want to be taunted that he was a share- holder of the Gas Committee. He did not want to appear before them under false colours, and he thought they would admit that they had had fair play. He proposed the following amendment : " That a Committee consisting of the gentlemen who were recently selected to interview the directors of the Runcorn Gas Company be appointed to carefully con- sider the advisability of introducing or suppling electrio lighting or other method of artificial illumination except- ing gas within the district under the control of the Council, the Committee not to incur any costs chargeable to the district rate in connection with this matter."— The reason he asked them to accept it was very much for the reason sugeested by Mr. Wilson. Th.,re were other illuminants which were supposed to be more to the advantage of the community which adopted them. There was a new illuminant called acetylele which was sup- posed to possess wonderfol advantages. It had the advantage of h-ine dividable, and sold in small quanti- ties to householders. Its cost was about 6d. per thousand, and as an illuminant, it was supposed to be superior to electric light. He thought they were approaching a time when there would be an ex- haustive series of experiments in artificial illumination. It would be a waste of time to confine the members of the proposed committee to consider electric lighting only. He thought they would recognise the reasonable- ness of the amendment —Mr. Griefs said he was a little interested in the Gas Company, and was therefere in a similar position to the Chairman. But he thought it was their duty to study the question of electric lighting or any other illuminant.—Mr. Dean said the way the Liverpool Corporation were taking up electricity did not seem as if they anticipated hotter illuminants within any reasonable time.—Dr. Gastaldi said he had no ob- jection to accept the amendment, but he was anxious that the committee appointed about the gas should he increased. He then replied to the statements made against his motion, and on it being put to the vote, as arnendei,it was carried.—Dr. Gastaldi then moved that the following compose the committee :—Messrs. Dean, Wilson, Kirkham, Hewitt, Coliier, Gastaldi, Imison, Taylor, Bisbrown, and Sweetman.—A discussion ensued, in which several of the members suggested on the com- mittee, declined to be put on, hut ultimitely the com- mittee as suggested by Dr. Gastaldi was appointed. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>PRECEPT.</head>
        <p>THE PRECEPT.</p>
        <p>It was decided that a precept be made on the over- seers for £9OO for the next six months, compared with one of £6OO for the same period last year.—The Chair- man moved the resolution, and in seconding it, Canon Wood said thot it WAS only an example of the liabilities they incurred in taking over the ruanagementof schools. They could not possibly avoid it. When they had the rates to fall back upou, he was afraid :that the public must expect, if the School Board took over more schools they would have to draw more and more largely from them. It was absolutely impossible to avoid it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EVENING SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE EVENING SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that about 80 males and 50 females had joined these schools this season. This was in excess of the number who joined last year. It was a very good start for the year. Vie business of the Board occupied two hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>IUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>IUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council met at D,ltton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. J. Brandreth occupied the chair, and amongst those present were Admiral Parry, Messrs. E. Bratherton, J. M. Williams, J. Stokes, T. Wright, W. Bradford, C. Mounfield, J. W. Ba..:uley, W. Pickerinc,,T. Darlington, C. Roylanco, F. ?Ming, J. Whitlow, T. Bax- ter, C.C., J. Parr, J. Darbyshire, S. Woodward, H. E. Heywoon, S. Horton, J. Weir, J. H. Chadwick. C. W. Davenport, and Mr. J. Ashton (clerk). Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Messrs. Diggle and Swinton (surveyors), and Mr. J. Farrington (nuisance inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</head>
        <p>THE PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>Under date September 26th, the Local Government Board wrote,stating that the Board's inspecter Dr. Barry, would attend on Tuesday, the 15th October, at noon, at the Mayor's parlour, Town Hill, Manchester, to confer with representatives of the several councils, whose dis- tricts were riparian to the Port of Manchester, on the subject of establishing a joint Board to act as Port Sani- tary Authority for the customs port of Manchester.—A further communication from the Town Clerk of Liver- pool, stating that it having been found more convenient for the majority of the authorities concerned the conference would be held at Manchester instead of Liverpool.—Mr. Williams referred to the inconvenient time fixed for the conference—the date of their next meeting. They knew that important business was to he brought forward at the next meeting. It was very desirable for the members to be present, and the Clerk's attendance was indispensable. He did not think the Council was being treated fairly in the matter, and he proposed that the Clerk write stating that it would be extremely inconvenient for the deputation from the Council to attend the conference on the date fixed ; and to add that as the Runcorn Union occupied 15 miles of seaboard on the Canal it was a very important matter with them.—Mr. Darbyshire thought it was absolutely necessary that someone should attend from the Counctl, and he /948 of the opinion that they might defer some of their business in order to allow the Clerk and the depu- tation to attend—Mr. Heywood proposed that the Coun- cil meeting be postponed until the following Thursday. —Mr. Aaron seconded.—After some discussion it was decided that the meeting be held as usual, and that the Gerk with Messrs. Williams and Chadwick attend the conference on behalf of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ISOLATION HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Writing with regard to the isolation hospital, the Local Government Board stated that if it was intended to seek the Board's sanction to a loan to defray the cost of a site for the hospital or of the hospital buildings it would be necessary that a local enquiry should be held before the matter could be considered. It wis for the Council to select the individual site, and in doinv, so they should have regard to the facalities for drainage and water supply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ROADS AT WALTON INFRRIOR.</head>
        <p>ROADS AT WALTON INFRRIOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter moved that the Co .nty Council for Cheshire be requested ta take over so much of the new roads in the township of Walton Inferior, extending from the main road to the foot of the Ship Canal swing bridge, being about 80 yards in length, and also so much of the said road extending from the swing bridge on the north side of the canal to the fixed bridge over the river Mersey ; being about 200 yards in length, so as to con- stitute these lengths, main roads repairable by the County Council. In doing so, he stated that the Counts, Council had prepared an estimate for an improvement which had been suggested to them,at tba entrance of the road which extended from the main road to the foot of the Ship Canal swine bridge at Walton In- ferior, The estimated cost was £l4O, and towards this, the Counts' Council asked the Runcorn Council to con- tribute £5O. The Manchester Ship Canal Company had paid the Runcorn Council a sum of money to keep the road in question in repair,but if they could transfer the road to the County Council on satisfactory terms he thought it would be better to do so, because it was in reality, a main road.—Mr. Woodward seconded the pro- position.—Mr. Darlington moved an amendment that the matter be deferred until the Warrington Extension scheme had been settled. If that Bill passed, ono of the roads would come under the control of the Warrington Corporation.—Mr. Darbyshire seconded, and after some discussion, the amendment was carried. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported by telegramln—Consequonce of having missed the train. that one case of scarlatina had occurred at Sutton, and one at Stockton Heath. He had visited both cases.—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY OPIIALTON.</p>
        <p>The Nuisance Inspector (Mr. Farrington), reported that he had submitted two samples of water from Hal- ton for analysis. One was returned as not satisfactorv. This had been taken from the reservoir.—Mr. Wright said he noticed that about SO yards from the reservoir there was a piggery. He would like an examination made to see whether anything from the piggery perco- lated into the reservoir. If not, he would propose that the township be supplied with the Vynwry water, as several diseases were traceable to the water suyply.</p>
        <p>THEI NUIS&amp;NCE /WSPECTOR'S REIPORT.</p>
        <p>Several analyses of water were submitted bp Mr. Far- rington. All with one exception were returned as satis- factory, and the exception was from Appleton on the Arley estate. He recommended that the report of analy- sis be forwarded to the agent.—This was agreed to. He further reported that five houses at Moore were without a water supply, and in accordance with his recommen- dation notices were ordered to be served on the owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ROAD IMPROTHMHNT AT WESTON.</head>
        <p>ROAD IMPROTHMHNT AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the committee appointed to consider the road question at Weston Point,demited him in company with tha Surveyor to see Mr. Clarke with reference to the purchase of land and the taking down of a wall for an improvement to a road. Mr. Clarke ad- hered to his original proposition that he must be paid £2O for the land, and have the material for the taking down of the wall. The committee had met. and recom- mended that those termS be accepted.—Mr. Wright in moving the adoption of the committee's recommenda- Coo, said be thought the Council had made a very good</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF ELEcnuc MOOTING</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Thursday morning,the monthly meeting of this Council was held. Mr. E. ItniBoll, J.P., occuused the chair, and there were also present, Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. F. Wiswall, S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., H. J. Grice. J. Wilson, J. Jones, O. H. Kirk- ham, T. Dean, M. Sweetman, D. Di:thrown, R. H. Posnott, G. Hewitt, and Mr. F: A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. E. Marshall (secretary), Dr. McDougall (medical officer), and Mr. M. Darker (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MEDICAL .OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL .OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall presented his monthly report in which he stated that there had been 39 deaths cluriyg the month. Three were inquest cases, and of the immainiNg 36, 13 were due to diarrhoea, 2 to 'natural decay, 2 , phthisis, 2 convulsions, a, mesenteric disease, and one each to infantile jaundice, scarlatina, diabetes, debility, dropsy, kidney disease, ulcers, embolism, coma, disease of liver, cancer, bronchitis, croup, disease of lung and to incestine origin. The 39 deaths showed an average death rate for the twelve months of 241; and exclusive of the inquest cases of 22/ of the estimated population. Diarrttet was very prevalent during the month amongst infants and young children and accounts for the. increased death rate as compared with the corresponding month of last year. 47 births were registered during the month.—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>ITIGHWATS AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE. Recommended by this Committee that the lamp in Zion Place be moved to the upper end of Sewell-street and that one be fixed at the bottom end of Jackson-lane, That th 6 crossing 5 t the bottom of Ellesmere-street be re- constructed with rimlingden setts ; that the sewer in prn-road be ex rended 45 yards or thereabouts, that an inlet be inserted near the Curve of t e road, that an additional manhole be constructed and a flushing eye be fixed at the extreme end of the sewer; that the amended plan of Messrs. Hallding and Warburton shewing one cesspool for each house instead of one down the field for the two houses be approved, subject to proper ventila- tion being provided. That the plan of Mr. J. Branch be disapproved on the ground that it does not comply with the building bve-laws.—These minutes were passed on the motion of Mr. S. Taylor. seconded by Mr. Wilson. HEALTH COMMITTER. _</p>
        <p>This Committee—re-commended that the tender of Mr. Sam lel Crosby for the supply of provender for the en- suing @ix months be accepted.—The minutes were adopted.</p>
        <p>TEE WATER RATE.</p>
        <p>The Water Committee r-e-c7;nri;;nd.ed, and it was ap- proved of, that the sum of £95 15. 81. for empties ttc. in respect of the water rate for thc4 quarter ended Michaelmas day be written off. TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.-THE CARETAKER'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Technical Instruction Committee recommended that the chairman of the committee arid the secretary having reported that they had carried nu% the instruc- tion of the committee and had appointed a temporary caretaker at 15i. per week it is recommended that t arrangement made by them be continued until the end of the session of 1885.96 (end of April next).—The minutes were passed without comment on the motion of Mr. Wiswall, seconded by Mr. Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee reported that the following matter which was referred to them by the Council for consider- ation and that the report had been discussed by them, viz. :—" That a Memerial from this Council be sent to the directors of the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Co., urging the desirability in the interest of the said Company, the travelling public and above all of the working classes, of a reduction in the existing transit per the footbridge and ferry ; and further to reduce the railway are bet- ween Runcorn, Ditton and Widnes."—The Committee while of course being of opinion that it is desirable to have the footbridge toll reduced as soon as possible, do not consider it expedient that the directors of the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Co. be approached on the matter at pre- sent.—Approved on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi,seconded by Mr. Grice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CEMETERY_ WALL,</head>
        <p>THE CEMETERY_ WALL,</p>
        <p>Mr. Grice moved, on behalf of the Markets and Ceme- tery Ccommittee, that the tender of Messrs. Thomas Kirkham ano Co., Runcorn, for the raising of the Ceme- tery boundary wall, be accepted.—Mr. Kirkham seconded, and it was agreed to.</p>
        <p>LETTER PROM SIR JORNMRIINNER, BART., M.P.</p>
        <p>Sir John T. Brunner writing from the Schweizerhlf, Lucern..., on the 17th September, to the Chairman, said his wife joined with him most heartily in rendering thanks to Mr. lmison and his colleagues, and to all who joined in the kind and hospitable reception accorded to them in Runcorn last Sat rday. They retained a vivid and pleasurable recollection of the day, whi..th they should long cherish. The brilliance of the weather, the gaiety of the decorations, the friendly greetings, and the noble character of the work in which all seemed to be taking a happy interest,combined to make it for them a delightful occasion. He begged also to thank Mr.Wiswall and all concerned in its preparation for the exquisitely beautiful illuminated address which he presented to him in such graceful and well chosen wards. The address and the silver key with which he opened the door of the Technical Institute, formed now a permanent part of ttuir house- hold treasures.—The Chairman moved that the secretary be instructed to acknowledge the receipt of the letter and acquaint Mr. J. T. Brunner with the success which had attended the classes, for he was sure it was a sub- ject Sir lihn Brunner took a great interest in.—Mr. Wig- wall said he seconded the proposition with very great pleasure. He thought the fact that they had already 430 students taking advantage of the institution was the crowning success of the whole proceedings. The insti- tution was in such a position that he felt it would now work its own way into public favour. He thought if they informed Sir John Brunner sif the number of students it would be the best way of showing him that the town appreciated what he had done in that direction. The propositien was carried unanimously. SHALL RUNCORN HAYS TRH HIECTRIC LIGHT?</p>
        <p>In accordance with notice given, Dr. Gastaldi moved that it is in the interest of the ratepayers ot Runcorn that electric lighting be introduced in this town and that a committee be appointed to carefully consider the subject.—ln doing so he said electricity, although in its infancy at the latter end of the nineteenth century, had, nevertheless, made such rapid progress as to call the attention of all scientific men to the utilization of it for the greater comfort of mankind. Staffordshire in that direction was well abreast of the times,and the Corpora- tion electric supply of Stafford, was already inaugurated, and that of Walsall coincided with it. A more remarkable portent of the times was that the small county town of Cheadle and the still more remote district of Alsager on the northern boundary of the county, had a scheme under consideration, whilst the Rugeley Urban Council had announced their intention of taking into consideration the lighting of their streets with electricity. If places second in importanca to Runcorn considered that elec- tric lighting would be a boon to them, why should not Runcorn consider the question in the interest of the ratepayers? His reasons for its introduction were (1) that the town would be better lighted than at present; (2) that the town lent itself in a remarkable manner to provide electricity; ; (4 yahy3lt)hat in the ultimate end it would be h that it would be a source of great revenue to the ratepayers. No one could gainsay his assertion that the town would be better lighted. Dur- ing July and August, no gaslight was used no matter how dark the night might be, and the inconvenience waa greatly felt by the ratepayers who paid dearly for light they did not receive. That anomaly •would be absolutely removed if they adopted the electric light because it would be the property of the town. With regard to his second point as to Runcorn lending itself to provide electricity, he might say that at the water works they had got three boilers capable of producing any quantity of steam for the purpose of generating the motive power, capable of revolving the most powerful dynamos. They had there ample apace for placing the necessary machinery to distribute electricity. Further, the Council was at the present time studying how to get rid of the nightsoil an account of the great cost involved in disposing of it, and the question of provid- ing a destructor had occurred often to them. The diffi- culty would have to be met, and his idea was that if a destructor was erected it would provide nearly sufficient fuel to provide the motor power for the dynamo, and thus generate the light of the town out of the town's refuse. Then they had water near them—the Vrynwy (Liverpool) supply, from which they could purchase additional power,which with the destructor would pro- duce a powerful motor for the supply of electricity. In support of the third argument he would urge the dear- ness of gas. They paid now for the lighting of the streets nearly £9OO a year. If they capitulated that amount it would give them at 3 per cent. £30,000 of borrowed capital, and with that amount they could easily put up all the needed machinery and plant, and supposing that in 30 years they repaid the borrowed capital by means of a sinking fund, in 30 years the elec- tric light would be a source of great revenue to the town as they would cease to pay rates for the lighting of the public streets,and have the revenue from private customers to go to the lessening of the rates. He asked them to carefully consider the question and for that pur- pose to appoint a committee to go into the matter care- fully and diligently and starry on investigations on all points. If the committee decided it was inopportune or that the scheme was impracticable he would rest content, feeling that his endeavour would receive due consideration, but if on the contrary the committee were favourable to his views,and it was proved that Runcorn could have electric light with advantage, it would be for the Council to take the necessary steps, and ho (Dr. Gastaldi) would he pleased that he had to some degree contributed to the well-being of the town, which during the last two vesrs had witnessed such reverses of fortune and who should say that the inauguration of the electric light in the town might not be as omen and harbinger of better times and greater prosperity of the people of Run-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>'...,';.'::::'''.1:..:,7,i,!7,:'....2.i.i.'.:..;•......:.:,!',.;.'''':.'',:-. ~''..'.:.i.''!•.l;'-1..:.,..;:.4.;;,1k,. ..,. ....,..!.,,,j..!....,:.:;:,..:.': .:-.,:;..7.;,..,,i.4..!.5::::.i..!...:',',e .-.,..:..-.:,'..-"!`..,., ,::!...F;1:..;:::...i'..:!..,,E':::.'.,...,..'.:,1„.;,,,::</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>HONOUR FOR A RUNCORN SCHOOLMASTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Crosby, headmaster of Brunswick-st. schools, Runcorn, has been honoured by having the distinction conferred upon him at the recent annual statutory meet- ing of the E iucational Institute of Scotland, the fellow- ship of the Institute. Six other English teachers had a similar honour conferred upon them. This distinction is only given to those occupying prominent positions in the profession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>At this Court on Saturday before Mr. F. J. Norman (chairman), and Mr. E. Imison, a man of no fixed resid- ence named William Tickle was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Brunswick-street, on the prev- ious night. The charge of drunkenness was proved by P.S. Gill and P.C. Gregory, and Elizabeth Barker spolvi to the prisoner accosting her, and then knocking her down. Having been previously convicted, and a stlte- ment being madu as to his general conduct, he was ipiit to gaol for a month without the option of a fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Sr. MICHAEL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>Sr. MICHAEL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services at this church were continued on Sunday. The Rev. P. J. Woodcock, M.A., vicar rf St. Paul's, Chester, preached. Special music was rendered by the choir. In the morning Jackson's To Deum and the anthem "Praise the Lord,o Zion" (Simp- son) were given. At the evening service Simper's Magnificat and Nunn Dimittis (in which the solos were taken by Masters H. Hill and H. Stubbs),and the anthem "0 God, who is like unto Thee ?" (the soloist being Mr. Charles Pownall) were rendered. The singing of the varisius items reflected credit upon the hon. conductor, Mr. Pownall. Mr. Timmins, jun., presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>FUNERAL OF M. E. SEDCOVIC• IC OF RETFORD. _ _</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF M. E. SEDCOVIC• IC OF RETFORD. _ _</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Cemetery on Monday afternoon, the re- mains of Mrs. Elizabeth Sedgwick, of Retford. Notting- hamshire, were interred. The deceased bad attained the age of 82 years, and her remains were conveyed to Run- corn by train. The Rev. H. C. Moyle was the officiating clergyman. The deceased was the relict of the late Mr. Isaac Sedgwick, at one time a linen merchant at Barns- ley, and later still a wine and spirit merchant at Ret- tord, where he held several public positions. He was interred in the same grave about 23 years ago. Her son came to Runcorn and joined in partnership with Mr. Day, solicitor, some years ago. The partnership, how- ever, was a very brief one, Mr. Sedgwick, jun., being taken ill, and dying at the early age of 24 years. He was interred in the grave where now rest his father and mother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Sr. PAUL'S BIBLE CLASS-"OPSN METING."</head>
        <p>Sr. PAUL'S BIBLE CLASS-"OPSN METING."</p>
        <p>In connection with this class an "span meeting " was held on Saturday, when a large congregation assembled. Mr. Kniveton presided. A very instructive and interes:- ing address was given by Mr. Wilding, on " Christ's Transfiguration." The musical part of the service was open with Mendelssohn's " I waited for the Lord." the duet in which was sung by Miss Lily Johnson and Miss Florrie Bradshaw. the quartettes by Miss E. Rigbv, Miss L. Rigby, Messrs. Cleave and Atherton. This was fol- lowed by Miss Smith playing a violin ;solo by Handel. Another excerpt of Handel's,the duet, "0 lovely peace," was then rendered by Mrs. A. Caygill and Miss Simkin. Mr. A. Shingler followed with a solo " The Volunteer Organist." The " Gloria "from Mozart's 12th Mass was ' performed on the organ by Mr. William Cooke, after which Miss Simkin sang " Creation Hymn," by Beet- hoven. Mr. C. Atherton sang Gunned's " Nazareth," which was appropriately followed by another solo by the same composer, 'There is a green hill tar away," sung by Miss Lily Johnson. Mr. William Cooke acted as ac- companist.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of this circuit was held in Brunswick vestries on Monday. At three o'clock the ' local preachers meeting was held when the usual routine business was gone through, The representatives par- ! took of tea at five o'clock, and this was succeeded by a ' meeting at which representatives were present from all parts of the circuit. The Superintendent of the circuit (Rev. T. Brackenbury) presided. and the Rev. W. George ' was also present.—The Chairman at the commencement of the proceedings expressed regret at the unavoidable absence of the newly appointed minister (Rev. C. De Putron Gliddon) who should have received an official welcome at that gathering. lie had, however,been called away through the serious illness of a relative. Regret was also expressed at the enforced absence through sidkness of the senior circuit steward—Mr. Robert Row- linson,—and a vote ef sympathy was ordered to be sent him in his affliction. Mr. F. .I. Norman, J.P., presentnd the financial report. which was considered highly satisfactory,especially considering the heavy expenses incurred by the change of pastor.—The numerical report of the superintendent showed a slight decrease which was accounted for by several deaths during the quarter, and a number of removals to other circuits. On the whole,the report was considered satis- factory. An interesting conversation ensued respecting the state of Christian work in the circuit, and the opinion was expressed that some special effort should be made to strengthen village Methodism in the locality. ' After some discussion it was unanimously decided to obtain the services of a lay missionary to work at Wes- ton and Weston Point for a short period. This concluded the business transacted.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSMNS. The Rev. J. D. Sutcliffe, late missionary of the West Coast of Africa, will preach two sermons on behalf of the Wesleyan Foreign Missions, in Halton-road Wesleyan Church, on Sunday next. The collections taken will be in support of the missions. Further particulars will be found in our advertising columns.</p>
        <p>EfanyEsT FESTIVAL AT THE PARISH CHURCH, RUNCORN. The annual harvest thanksgiving services in connec- tion with the Parish Church were commenced on Thurs- day evening. There was a large attendance. The church had been most artistically decorated tor the occa- sion, and presented a fine appearance. The Rector of Warrington (the Rev. Canon Willis, M.A.), was the preacher. THE BENEFIT CONCERT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Committee who had in hand the arrangements for the benefit concert for Mr. Harrison met on Monday. The chair was occupied by the Rev. A. H. Bartram. The Secretary (Mr. G. W. Yates) sub- mitted the financial statenaent from which it appeared that the receipts amounted to over £2O, and alter meet- ing all expenses, the balance in hand was £l5 149. 2]. It was decid(d that this amount should be handed over Ito Mr. Harrison. A letter was read from Mr. Harrison thanking all ho had taken part. NATIONAL DAY SCHOOLS, RUNCORN. A meeting of the subscribers to these elementary schools was held on Monday under the presidency of the Rev. Canon Wood. Amongst those present were:— Messrs. E. unison, T. P., J. Johnson, W. Mellor, James Littler, John Littler, Edward Key, and J. Yates. The Chairman spoke of the committee's work during the past twelve months which was considered satisfac- tory. The following gentlemen were appointed on the committee :—Rev. Canon Wood, Rev. H. B. Firth Messrs. W. Whittaker, E. Imison, W. Mellor, H. White, E. Gregory, and J. Johnson. On the motion of Mr. Mel- lor, the Vicar was thanked for the trouble taken in ob- taining the money for the extension and improvement of the schools. He was also thanked for presiding.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. Harvest thanksgiving services were held in this church on Sunday. Large congregations assembled at both morning and evening services. The church had been artistically decorated for the occasion, and presented a most beautiful appearance. The work had been carried out by the following ladies who had bestowed consider- able time upon it :—Mesdames Forrester, Lacy, Lur,t. and Davies ; Misses Robb, Efeatherington, Ainsworth, Cringhall, Richardson, Barrow, Jessie Stone, Mr. and N. Wright, T. and F. Temple, and Entwistle. They were assisted by a number of ladies and gentlemen. Within the communion rails were numerous plants, end on tables were flowers, fruit, vegetables, miniature stacks, and a model of a farm, with implernents,&amp;c., forwarded by Mr. Price, of Weston Point. At the end opposite to the pulpit were shields containing the words " The earth is the Lord's," " And the Mine's thereof," the letters being in straw on a crimscn ground. In the morning the anthem " Thine, 0 Lord, is the greatness " was set rendered by the choir; and in the evening they gave " 0 Lord, how manifold," and " God said, let the earth." Mr. C. C. Le Couteur occupied his accustomed place at the organ. The preacher at both services was the pastor, the Rev. J. Simp,on, who in the evening based his discourse on Amos viii. 2, " A basket of sum- mer fruit," and Galatians v. 22 and 23.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. The teachers and elder scholars of Bethesda Sunday School were on Wednesday evening entertained by Mr. W. H. Chadwick, the superintendent of the school, to a tea and social gathering. After a most enjoyable tea,Mr. Chadwick gave a short address, in which he expressed his earnest'hope that at some not very distant time 1 many of the young people who were at present in a state of indecision and were not extensively identified with the active work of the church, would feel it incum- bent upon them to make themselvs most useful in each I branch of work connected with it, and thus ensure it be- I coming:prosperous and far reaching. Mr.H.Taylor then passed a few remarks impressing upon all present the need for earnestness and unswerving steadfastness if any real work was to be accomplished in their midst. A short musical program:ne was then given, the various items being :—" The Anchor's Weighed," Mr. W. Lacy "Rocked in the Cradle of the Deep," the Rev.". Sanp- son (encore) ; "On the Banks of Allan Water," " TIIO Flight of Agee," Miss Wilson; " Come Lasses and Lads," Miss Heatherington (encore) ' • " I couldn't say no, now could I ?" Mr. C. Le Couteur. Games were then indulged in and heartily enjoyed by all,until the close of the even- ing. During the evening the thanks of all were conveyed to Mr. Chadwick for his hospitality, which had been kindly apprcciated by all present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>" THE 'MAID; OF ORLEANS."</head>
        <p>" THE 'MAID; OF ORLEANS."</p>
        <p>_ At a meeting of the Brunswick Mutual Improvement 'Society on Wednesday evening, a capital lecture was given by the Rev. C. De Putron Gliddon, the newly ap- pointed Wesleyan Minister of the circuit, on " Joan of Arc, her voices and victories." The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Simiater, president of the class. The lecturer introduced his subject by referring to historical critic- isms having laid ruthless hand on the heroes of their boyhood. But they still had Joan of Arc. After sketch- ing her early career in her humble surroundings at Dom- remi,in Lorraine, he proceeded to speak of the "voices." bidding her to deliver her country. Speaking of Shaxes- peare's caricature of her in " Henry VI." he said he had cruelly insulted her by describing her as a witch. In dilating on the reality or otherwise of these voices which she declared were from Heaven, the rev. gentleman,said , there was no doubt that they were realities to Joan of Arc. To her, they were voices from Heaven commission- ing her to do definite and almost super-human tasks. It was necessary for them in considering this epoch in her life to take into consideration the a ge,(early in the fifteenth century),the religious system then believed,and her patrinti m and passion for the deliverance of her country. If the voices were not sent direct from Heaven who would say that she was not sent by Heaven ? Why should they reject Joan's voices P Every argument used to disprove the reality of them was used to disprove the miracles related in the Bible. If she succeeded in her work it would prove her mission,which was to drive the English from Orleans, and to take Charles to Rheims for his coronation. The lecturer then proceeded in a most interesting manner to refer to Joan of Arc's exploits in the fulfilment of her mission, and to the complete suc- cess which attended her eff n.ts. Her imprisonment and martyrdom at the early age of 20 were afterwards told in befitting language, and the rev, gentleman concluded an impressive and instructive lecture by deducing from the lifework of Joan of Arc a lesson and a call to his hearers,to take their part in removing the evils at present existing.—A vote of thanks to the lecturer,on the motion of Mr. J. D. Jones, seconded by Mr. Millington, and supported by Captain Le Couteur, concluded the meet- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>INTERESTING WEDDING. RUS H TON-EVANS.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING WEDDING. RUS H TON-EVANS.</p>
        <p>On Monday at Unity Chapel, Bristol, a marriage I was Celebrated between Frank Edwin, youngest son ' of Mr. William Rushton, of Runcorn, and Florence Elizabeth, youngest daughter of Mr. F. Evans, of Milton Park, Redfield, Bristol. The ceremony was performed by the pastor, Rev. W. J. Morgan, amidst many friends of the happy pair. The bride was attired in an electric blue faced cloth dress, trimmed with silk, bonnet of gold and chenile, and carried a magnificent bouquet, presented by Mr. Walker, superintendent of the Sunday school, with which both bride and bridegroom have been connected. The bridesmaids were, Miss Evans, sister of the bride, who wore a fawn satin cloth dress and jet bonnet, and Miss May Alexander (niece of the bridel,who was attired in a cream serge dress,leghorn ' hat,with ostrich feather,and carrying basket of flower&amp; The bride was given away by her father, and the groom was accompanied by his brother, Ti. W. Rushton, as best man. The happy pair left the chapel amidst showers of rice and surrounded by a vast throng of friends,for their hemp in Staffordshire. The following is a list of some of the presents :—Mr. and Mrs. Evans, silver cruet, &amp;c. ; Miss Evans, silver tea pat ; Mr, and Mrs. Alexander, silver spoons ; Miss Alexander, picture ; Master Alexander, dinner mats ; Bridegroom to Bride' cheque ; Mr. and Mrs. Rushton, cheque. Sze. ; Mr. A. W. Rushton, silver forks and cut- lery ; Miss Rushton, antis ; Miss 11. B. Rushton, steel and carvers ; Miss A. Rushten, crumb brush, tray and duchess mats ; Mr. and Mrs. J. A. Rushton, antis. ; Mr. and Mrs. Stanway (Cleckheaton) coal vase and lamp ; Miss Stanway (Cleckheaton), ornaments ; Master Stan- way (Cleckheaton). bedroom lamp ; Mr. Walter Pea- cock (Runcorn), breakfast cutlery ; Mr. Charles Littler (Runcorn), egg cups and stand ; Mrs. Thomas Bowyer (Runcorn), teapot ; Mr. Dyer (Runcorn), ironmongery ; Mr. and Mrs. Walker end family (Bristol), brass gipsy kettle and stand ; bride's Sunday school class, biscuit barrel ; Mr.. Mrs., and Miss Fox, Crown Derby teapot and breakfast cruet ; Mr. and Mrs. Dames' silver coffee pot ; Miss Dennes, preserve jar ; Mr. and Mrs. King, silver butter dish, salts and spopos ; Mr, Short, Mrs. Short, and Mrs. J. W. Orchard. silver sugar bowl, cream j',g and nut crackers ; Mr. Lambert, painted hand screens and mirror ; Miss Crocker, pair brackets; Mr. and Mrs. F. Orchard, cheese dish ; Miss Morgan set of damask doyleys ; Mr. and Mrs. Heaven, oil painting and motto ; Miss Willey, oil paint- ing mounted on plush; Mrs. Parsons, silver butter knife; Miss Payne, tea cosy; Misses Ashfcrd and Best, silver bread fork; Miss Sinclair, hand painted plaque ; Miss Small, glass vases; Mrs. jPethard, chinese teapot; Mrs. Johnson (Staffordshire) mats and curtain bands.</p>
        <p>_ THE DISTRICT NURSE.</p>
        <p>The monthly report from August 25th to September 25th, of the Runcorn District Nurse, states that there' has been 29 cases on the books, 3 of which were chronic, I 14 medical, 10 surgical, and 2 typhoid. Of these six are convalescent, 4 are dead, and one in the Workhouse In- firmary. The number of visits paid to cases was 206, and occasional visits 72. HOUSEHOLD LINEas, hemmed and marked free of charge.— and W. Dutton, Sankey-et., Warrington.—Adat. The Macclesfield Town Council on Thursday decided to memorialise the Cheshire County Council and the Chancellor of the Exchequer in favour of a five-shilling yearly tax on cycles, the money to go to urban and rural district councils towards the maintenance of the roads.</p>
