      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BEFOIiE MA6I6TEA.TES.</head>
        <p>BEFOIiE MA6I6TEA.TES.</p>
        <p>SHOUTING IN THE NTARICET.</p>
        <p>At the WarringTon-iii;ough Police Court yesterday. Joseph lever, 25, Bishop et., Hightown. Manches' er ; George Lever, 21, Ball-road, Oldham ; Laur-nce I) ate. 13, Portland-street; William Johnson 40. King-st.; William Lowe. 149, Church- it. ; William :huffieton, 3. Cheshyres-yard ; Thomas Shovel- ton, senr ,and I homas Show. Iton, jun., 1, Cheshyree-yard ; and Samuel Henshall, 53, Liverpool road, St. Helens, were each charged with crying fish for sale in the Warrington mar- ket, on September 21. contrary to the by-laws.—P.C. Hol- lingswot th said he had warned the defendants several times, but they dii nothing to ab,te the nuisance and abused him by using some fear ully bad lan..uage. He was at length obliged to report them and they the:, made a worse noise than eve .—Similar evidence nas given by P.C. Delahay.— Inspector Place said that he cautioned Borne ot the defendants but later on in the. same evening, he was sent for by the police. While the police officers were taking the de- fendants' names, the defendant Joseph Lever said " We are not going to be summoned for nothing," and his brother George corn me red to shout at the top of his voice. Joieph also commenced learner, lately at terwards, ancrthe market was like a bedlam. All the deendants but tienshall wt re em- ployed bv the Levers who had more men than thee needed, and as they had no work to do, they mu ,t be shoming.—Bvi- deuce was further given by assistant Inspectors Wallington and Topham. —The Chief Constable said that the Town Clerk was unable to be present that morning, or he would have asked for the full penalt,. The polioe were e ,ristant y defied —systematically defied- by Mr. Lever who tried to make capi- tal by appearing as though he was persecuted —Mr. Lever denied that he had more men than he iieeded, and said that he was obliged to get a living —'the Bench decided to fine the two Levers 10s. and costs aini dismissed the others who were In their employ. Henshall was fined Is. and costs. THEFT FROM A NEWSPAPER OFFICE.</p>
        <p>Three news boys, Joseph Sutton, John Sutton, and John Gibbons, were summoned at the Warrington ' Borough Court, on Monday, for stealing on Saturday from the office of the " Evening News," Bewsey-street, 15s. 61. in cash, the property of Alfred Downes. They were also charged with stealing 4s. on Friday. The Chief Constable said that Mr. D )wnes had charge of the " Evening News" cffice. The Suttons lived at 7, Rodney-st., and Gibbons at 58, Turner-st. For some time Mr. Downes had missed cash from the drawer in his office at Bewsev-st. At four o'clock on Saturday. Mr. Downes put into a bag in the drawer a half sovereign and a ss. Shorly afterwards he missed the money and recollected that the three defendants were the only boys who had been in the office. He in- formed Detective Monaghan who arrested the three prisoners. They then admitted that they had taken the money and changed the half sovereign at the Bridge Inn and had bought a football. On Fri +ay afternoon, Mr. Downes had missed a 4s. piece and when questioned about this the defendants admitted taking it. During the last few weeks £4 or £5 had been missed. us. 71. only had been recovered. Mr. Downe4 said that he locked the drawer,but he had since found that when the drawer was locked the back went down a little so that a boy with a small hand could get the money out of the drawer—the Bench ordered the boys to receive twelve strokes of the birch rod. taOBSTRUCTING THE FOOTPATH AT CENTRAL STATION. A number of p•srsons were summoned at the Warring- ton Borough Court on Monday for obstructing the free passage of Winwick-st near Central Station on S.pterba- her 20th. Only two. Peter Scully and John Griffia ap- peared in answer to the summons. The ether defendants were John Bartley, Patrick Dooney, Patrick Carroll, John Bamfoot. and William Coyne. It was decided to hear the case in the absence of the defendants. P.C. Lewis and P.C. Monks each gave evidence to the effect that they had repeatedly requested the defendants to keep off the footpath at Central Stat' 'on but as soon as their backs were turned they returned again. They were a source of great annoyance to the railway autho- rities who fr-quentiv complained about them. The Bench decided to fine Scully and Griffin ss. and costs or seven days, and the rest who had not appeared, were fined 40s. and costs or one months' imprisonment. MISTAKEN IDENTITY.</p>
        <p>Walter Hartley was charged at the Warrinoton Borough Court on Tuesday, with being a deserter from ! H.M. Royal Artillery since February 2.—Detective Monaghan said that from information he re,eived, he went to the house of defendant's father in Knutstord- road and informed him that his son had deserted from the Ruyal ArtilierF, and that it wa discovered that he was working at Loughborough. The .on who was in- formed of this came over, and denied that he had ever been in the army, but that it must be his br-ther who had enlisted in his name, and was very like him in ap- pearance. The father told his son thar ho had better go to the police station, and inform the police of this and he did so. The enlisting sergeant however sill that the defendant was the man wanted.—The father of the de- fendant said that he had one son in the army, but he had never known that Walter had enlisted. The other brother however was very like him arid could easily be taken for him.—Several letters had been received from Walter since he went away from home, hut they had always had the postmailc of Lmehborntwh, or Rugby.— The defendant still adhered to his statement that he had never been in the army, and the Chi-f Constable then said that he would withdraw the charge, and the de- fendant was liberated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</p>
        <p>TIIIIRSDAT.—Before the Mayor and Alderman Sutton. , USING THREAT:I.—George Maguire was summoned for having on the 24th inst. made use of threats towards hia wife.—The complainant said the defendant threatened to take away her life on the day in question. He also threw himself on a bed on which a child was dying. and she had to remove it. He said the deed at the " Bang Up would be nothing to what would be committed in that house." Complainant had to fasten herQelf in a bed room,as she was afraid of her hushand,who Was not now following any employment.—PC. Sarkev deposed to finding the defendant lying drunk on the hed.—Com- plainant : Grant me a separation order.-- The Clerk said that could not be done at present. C.• nipl.ti riant : We cannot keen him from the alehowie door. He is a good husband when he keeps away from the TKO:Ain:house.— Defondant : I will sign the pledge if you will let me off this time.—Th, Mayor : The pledge he hanged !—The complainant attain pre4sed for a Qeparstion order, hut was again told by the Clerk that ir was impoesit;io to grant one—Alderman Sutton said that was the defend- ant's 25th appearance.—Th. tli-lendant, who admitted that he was under the influence of drink on the day in question, tion wits htund over t 3 keep the peace. In default of finding sureties he will have to go to gaol for three</p>
        <p>months. DRl7NlC.—Fitelan was fined 2. 131. ingindinz costs for having boon drunk arid disorderly in Mori-U.. at. on the previous afternoon. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chahlnan), Dr.Gornall, Alderman Wright Councillor Roberta, and Messrs. T. Birehali and S Wright. USUAL FRID&amp;Y CASES.—Jon Lythgoe Snit Daniel Lytegoe were bound over for six months for s=autting Francis Tog- gins on Saturday night: Mr aohur Browne prtoecutei and said that the two detendants without any prim ealou what- ever, str.,ek Teggins in the eye, and also turd him in the nose —Emma Forti. 28, Gee-st.' was fined 54 aroi costs for assaulting Mary Russell. 38, Gee-st., on September 22. Richard Hassall, 9, Rostoek-st,. was hound over for six months for naine, threats towards his wife on S. prember 25. The throat was th tt he would • • BP iv Green" h, r.— Mary Ann- Wilkinson, 15, Haydock-.t., was bound over for six months for using threats towards Kate Hardman, 15, Hay- ; .dock-st., on September 24.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Mr. F. Monks, Alderman Harrison, and Alderman Wright. DRUNKENNESS.—Fr this offence Margaret Dolan, 5, Berry's-yard, was fined 55.; William Cunningham, 38, James-st., ss. and costs ; John Kelly, 9, &amp;-one-yard, ss. and costs ; Thomas Stockdale, Melton, ordered to leave the town ; Frederick Spencer, 35, To .via Hill, 2 •. 61. Doe %%groom, Licoxso.—Charles Jackson, 51, Eldon St., was fined 2s. 61. for having a dog without a license. STONE THROWING. —.Thomas Phillips and Joseph Brain- hail were each fined is. for throwing stones in Cock- hedge-lane on September 30. P.C. Barrett proved the case.</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Councillor Henry Roberts (chair- man), and Dr. T. Starkey Smith. ABSENTEE FRODI TRAINING. —Joseph Birtwistle was charged with beina an absentee from the ttaining of the 3rd Liverpool Minlia on June 21. P.C. Corcoran proved the case. Prisoner said that he had been told that he I need not come again, and that he was in prison at the time the training took place. An officer present denied that the prisoner had been told not to go again tor training. Prisoner was handed over to the military authorities.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), Mr. John Geddes. and Mr. J. H. Fleming. DRUNICENNESS.—WiIIiam Lythgoe was fined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Sharpe-st., at two o'clack on Tuesday afternoon.—Kate Hinds was fined ss. and costs for committing a similar offence in Buttermarket-st. on Tuesday afternoon. TsrE rHEFT OF MATCHES.—The boy Peter Hankinson, who was brought before the magistrates with his sister on Wednesday of last week for stealing a quantity of matches and firewood, and who was remanded to find a home for him to be sent to, was again brought before the magistrates and ordered to be sent to Father Nugent's Home until he was 16 years of age. The girl was sent to a ref ,rmatory for five years. A letter from Father Nugent was produced in court, in which he said that he would receive any boys from Warrington. THURSDAY.—Before the Mayor (Councillor James Fail dough) wad Alderman Sutton. . IRREGULAR SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. —Mr. W. Jones pro- secuted in the following cases of irregular school attend- ance :—Robert Kennedy, 14 Wakefield-st. fined 2s. 61.; Matthew Carley,l4 Pierpoint-5t.,55.; Charles Higginson, 16 Ellesmere-at., adjourned ; Peter Tickle, 11 Garden Raw,adjourned for four weeks,and if his son does not at- tend regularly in that time, he is to go to the "Clio." ALLEGED THREATENING LANGUAGE. --Margaret Wag- staffs, 113 Sharpe-st,was charged with using threats to- wards Mary McKay on October Ist. The complainant said that since she appeared as a witness in a case at the Police Court some time ago, the defendant never left her alone, but was always using abusive language. She threatened on the day in question to put a knife through her, and also called her a Fenian. A neighbour was brought as a witness in support of the above statement. —Mrs. Wagstaffe danied using any threats,and said that Mrs. McKay abused her.—Several witnesses were called but the magistrates believed that the case was proved, and bound defendant over for six months, and ordered her to pay the costs. ASSAULT ON A POLICEMAN.—John Jones. 21,IAcester- street, was charged with being drunk and:disorderly. in Scotland-road, on Welnesday night, and also :with assaulting P.C. Frank Cooper. The officer said he saw the prisoner lying on the footpath in Scotland-road and acting as though he was mad. Two men were holding him and on witness going to him, prisoner kicked him twelve or fourteen times on the legs and body. Witness held him until P.C. Barnett came,and he was then taken to the police station. He smelled of whiskey " some- thing awful." The prisoner presented a pitiable sight, and had a swollen eye, and a larae lump behind the head. This, the officer explained, was caused by a, bother the prisoner had near Plinston'a lodging-house, where he had been assaulted.—P.C. Barnett gave cor- roborative evidence.—The priscmer said that he was so dazed with his injuries that ha did not know what he doing. Nothing was previously known against prisoner whobel, mged to Warrington, and the Magistrate dis- charged him on payment of the costs.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. —l3,fOre_ Captain .Withington (chairm</p>
        <p>- and Mr. Ziba Armitage. DRUNKENNESS.—Jamrs Birchall was charged with ing drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cart at Poul- ton on September 19th. P.S. Barnes and P.C. Heath proved the case. Defendant was fined 2s. 6.1. and costa. —William Burgess and John Williamson were fined 53. and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Sankey on Sept. 6. P.C. Thorley proved the case. AN UNMUZZLED DOG—Joseph Swift was fined 54. for allowing his dog to be at large unmuzzled in Penketh on September 23rd. RIDING WITHOUT RElNS—Robert Larch was fined 2s. 63. and costs for driving a horse attached to a cart and having no reins, on Sept. 25:11, in S SHOOTING A RACING PIGEON AT CUERDLEY.--Elenry Wil- kinson was charged with unlawfully wounding a pigeon, the property of James Lightfoot, 11 Moss-st., Mos3bank, Widnes, on Sept. 18, in Cuerdley. Mr. Knowles who represented the complainant said that on the above date Lightfoot sent a man named Fenlon, who was his brother•in-law, with some pigeons, the pigeon complained of among them, to set them up in Cuerdley. That pigeon was what was known as a short distance racer, which kept near to the ground, never rising more than six or seven feet above the ground. On the day in question Fenton had tossed off the pigeon and a short distance away was Wilkinson, who was seen by Ferslun andseveral other witnesses to shoot the pigeon. Several men followed Wilkinson, and the latter hid himself in a sluice on Cuerdley Marsh. Two men kept watch and Lightfoot was sent for. They then went to Wilkinson who refused to give him his name, and it was not until a policeman was met, and defendant threatened with being locked up that he gave his name. The penalty of this offence was 403. over and above the value of the pigeon. The value of this pigeon was laid at £5. A short time ago, it and its nest mate took part in a "Derby "and its mace won the first prizl of £5, amd this bird won the third priz: of 30i. Slice then it hal been trained, and the owner had every confidence that it would win the first prize in a " Derby" which vas shortly to take p!ace.—Evidence in support of Mr. Knowles' statement was given by James Lightfoot, Richaid May, Martin Rime;, Thomas Cullen, Daniel Plunket,—Mr. Browne said that his client wis of excel- lent character. Under the section upon which the de- fendant was summoned, the magistrates most he satis- fied that the shooting Was malicious. The defendant absolutely denied shooting the pigeon at all. On the day in question, he took 21 cartridges out with him, but as he found nothire, to shoot, he sat down near the river side and the first thing he heard of the pigeon being shot was when the witnesses who had been called by Mr. Knowles came to him. He at once offered to too to the police station to show the police IHS atilt so that it could be seen whether it had recently been fired.—P.C. Martin of the Widnes police force said that he examined thv defendant's gun on the day in question, and it was quite clean.—The Bench decided to dismiss the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATI3 RDAY. —Before Mr:.T. J.Gillespie, and Mr. C. B.F.</p>
        <p>Borron.</p>
        <p>THE JURY Ll+TS.—The-j,i-r-y lists for the township for the petty sessimlal diet rics 'NMo submitted by the 1/Ani- ons overseers, and anproved. DRUNILENNEgi.—WiIIiam Chambetiain, of Earlesto was fined 2s. 6d. and costs for this rffence ; Jane Hughes, Newton, 2s. 6L and costs: Abel Pimblett, Golborne. 2s. 6.1. ; Heaton, Havdock,ss. ; William Jones, Flavdock, 24. 61.; Samuel durrans, Golhorne, 2+. 6d.; Thomas Mandesly. Haydock, 24. 6.1. ; William Hughes, Gclborne, 2s. 6].; JPremiah James; Newton, as. 61 A SLEEPY DRlVER.—Benjamin Howorth was fined 54. and costs for having ridden asleep ors a trap while heiriz in charge on September .11, in ligh-stree, Newton, at five o'clock in the evening. STR9NGE CONDI/CT.—Susannah Richards. summoned William Southern for assaulting her on S.mtem*.r Defendant did not Annear. Tile evidence was that. (1.- feridant came into Mrs. Richard's house and tw,,an to abuse her. She asked him to ;,,o out hut he refused an I she would make him go our. and made ad tsli towards the poker. He then ran at her, knocked h...r down and tried to put her on the fire, and in other ways to assault her. Defendant was fined ss. and costs. . . .</p>
        <p>... THBEATENING LANGUAoe..—Margaret Ann Baxencl Ile summoned Esther Piikington for using threAteuirg i languaae towards her on September 24d. The threaten- ' ing language complained of was that Mrs. P lkiogton I said she would knock Mrs. Baxendale's brains out. 1 Several witnesses were called,ne of whom said that one i side was as bad as the other. The 13,lich ordeNd Mrs. , Pilkin.ton to be hound over for three months. IFRIGHTENED OF HIS SICIN.—MAry and John Corfield, Haydock, were summoned by Thomas Craghan, Hay- dock, for Rasa ultin, and beating him on S^ptem ber 9th. , Thomas Craghan said that on the above date Mary and ! John Corfield came into his house and John cau.tht him , by the throat and nulled him to the ground, While . down on the ground, both John and Mary kicked him , and made him unconscious. At th- time the assault took place, he was having his dinner, and there was i,I another man also getting his dinner but who dared not , come to his assistance. Another lodger who was untairs, . however, came rushing down on hearing the n ise, and , I pull.d the defendants off him. Ile did not know what Iwas the cause of the assault.—Cross-exatnined by Mr. I Stuart who defended: Be got Mr Corfield,rhe Itut•hind of ! Marc. and father of John to co hail with him wh-n the , bailiffs came into his house, and Mrs. Corfield had als, collected 5Y,111e money to bury a child. The tr-mble, however, d'cl not take place because the money was not paid back, nor did it arise nut of that matt, r at all._ Dmnis Craghan„ a lodger, said that although the same name as the complainant, he was no relation. Be saw John Corfield come into the hou..e and seize the corn- ' plainant he the neck and pull him to the floor, and also saw him kicked.—Mr. Stuart :And why did you not try to stop him ?—Witness : Bedad. I would rather have anc/hodl injured hut myself. (Laughter.)_mr. Gill.„. pie ; Do you mean to sac that you are such a coward as to let a man he strangled under your eyes T—Wirness : Yes. I would rather let anybody be strangled but my- self. I did not want to interfere as I didn't know !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EXAATTNEP, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 5,- 1895</head>
        <p>THE EXAATTNEP, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 5,- 1895</p>
        <p>what might happen to me (Renewed laughter.) Evi- dence was also given by Mrs. Grr,glian--Mr. Stuart denied that any assault toJk place on the part of the defendant. He said that the real case was that John Cor- field went to ask Craghan for the money for which his father was bail,and Craghan struck him, and a daughter of Craghan hit him with the poker and sent him out of the house.. Evidence to this effect was given by Win. and Alice Corfield, and the Bench de&amp;ded to dismiss the case. A PALTRY CHARCE.—Thomas Chadwick was fined 2.5. 6d. for leaving his horse at Golborne on Sept. 17th, without having any control over it. The evidence showed that the man was away two or three minutes at a house near by. LETTING OFF FIREWORKS IN THE STREET.—Patrick Bryan was fined 24. 6J. and costs for letting off some fireworks in High-st., Golborne, on Sept. 13th:</p>
        <p>earlier stages. The tfirwards worked hard to a man, whilst the backs handled the ball with mire accuracy. They now had much the best of matters, and compelled Runcorn to defend for some Faulkner got away and passed to one of his opponents, but Williamson took the ball and sent it well into the visitors' half. Time was called directly after with the result—Manniugham two goals, one try-13 points; Runcorn, three tries— nine points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>MoNDAT.—Betore Mr. James Handley (chairman) Mr. F. J. Norman, and Mr. S. Taylor. JURY LISTS.—The overseers produced the list of the ' persons qualified to serve on juries for the various town- ships in the Petty Sessional division. There were no ob- jections raised, and the lists as published, were duly signed. . SC m HOCL ATTENDANCE CASES.—For not causing en. children to attend school regularly the undermentioned persons were fined 6). and 4s. 6,1. costs. Mr. T. Bowyer prosecuted.—Richard Shaw, Jackson's-lane ; Thomas Elodgetts, rfirunswick-street ; Joseph Wakefield, Loch street ; Bernard Flannigan. Loch-street; William Helsby, Stonehills ;John Weaver, Shaw-street; Thomas Oates, Heath-road. -</p>
        <p>THREATENING A SCHOOL BOARD OFFICER.—James Rue- bottom, labourer, 8, Taylor's-row, was summoned to answer the complaint of George Howard, that on the 16th September at Runcorn he did use certain threats towards him for which he was afraid he would do him some bodily harm.—Mr. G. Howard, school attendance officer, stated that on the date named he was passing along Heath-road, going towards Mill Brow schools when the defendant who was coming in an opposite direction, stopped him. The defendant had been sum- moned the same morning for non-payment of a mainten- ance order. After using some bad language, he struck at witness with a piece of timber and threatened to strike his--head off. Witness got out of his way, or he would have received serious injury. He gave defendant no provocation.—Mr. F. Whalley stated that he was walk- ing in the direction of Delph Bridge on the afternoon in question when he saw the defendant and Mr. Howard talking together. He heard the defendant use the threat mentioned and also saw him strike at Howard with a stick he had in his hand, which was between three and four feet long, and about two inches thick. The defendant was under the influence of drink at the' , time. The defendant admitted that he was under the influence of drink, but pleaded that Howard had been insulting his mother. —The magistrates bound him over in two sureties.himself in £5. and another of £5, and to pay the costs, er in default 14 days' imprisonment. He went to prison. A WARRINGTON HAWKER FINED Martin Lyons, hawker, of Warrington,was charged with having on the 17th August at Runcorn, furiously driven a pony and cart to the danger of passengers.—P.C. Gregory gave evidence in support of the charge, stating that when he shouted to the defendant to go slower, he urged his pony on, and used an oath.—Defendant denied that it was going furiously, and stated that he would give it to anyone who could drive it more than three miles an hour. He did not,see the officer. Evidence for the defence was given by John McElroy,also of Warrington.—The magis- trates imposed a fine of 103. and costs, or 14 days' im- prisonment. DRIVING WITHOUT REINS.—Luke Quinn, teamsman for Mr. Baguley, of Norton, was summoned for having on the 21st ult. driven a cart without the use of reins. Defendant pleaded guilty, and was fleed 103. and costs. OBSTRUCTING THE FOOTPATH —Robert Bennion and William Ashbro. of Runcorn, appeared to answer the , information of the pence that they obstructed the foot- I way at Runcorn, on the 220 d ult., by loitering thereon to the annoyance of passengers. P.C.Richardson stated that on the date named the defendants with three others were standing at Mr. Price's window in Green way-road. , They were smoking and chewing and spitting across the parapet,—The cle'enfiants were Ordered to pay the , Costs. DOG OWNERS IN TROUBLE Anhans.—The following per- sons were fined for allowing their dogs to be at large unmuzzled :—Ellis Clarke, 14, Wirern Place • Henry Miller, tobacconist,Church-st. ; and William B idon, Sea. lane, Halton. A WEDDING DAY EVENT.—Margaret Farrell and Annie Dyer, both of Cooper-st., were summoned by Mary Ann Feeney on a charge of assaulting her on the 24th ult. The evidence given in support of the charge was to the effect that on the date named a wedding party was held at the house of the complainant, and at 10.30 p.m., as some of the party were going home, a dispute occurred outside the house. Mrs. Feeney went outside. She was struck by someone, and the two defendants pulled her by the hair of her head into the middl a of the street. Whilst on the ground, Mrs. Dyer threatened to take her life. The complainant denied that she was under ths influence of drink. Mrs. Crank and Mrs. Eliza. Ather- ton, neighbours, gave evidence in addition to the cons- plainant.—fhe defendants contended that they had re- ceived provocation for the assault, and that it was ow- ing to she complainant and others being drunk.that the " holier " commenced.—Etch defendant was fined is. and 111. 81. costs. _</p>
        <p>_ DRUNKENNESS.—M try Lyn., Mersey Square, was sum- moned for heiug drunk and isorderly in Mersey at., on the 19th ult. She did not appear and a wart. ,nt was isaued.—Goorge Moores (13th appearAnce), was charged with being drunk and disirderly in findge-st, on the prevlow Saturday. flis wife apwared in court and stated that he had been away for tw4 rears and she kid tnen ohl.ged to support her,elf. She applied for a separation. The magistrates stated that ti:ey could not ntrit a separation, but the defendant would he com- mitted to gaol for one month a ithout the option of a fine.—Parrick Degg,an, of Sutton, for being drunk in Fryer-st.,Runcaru, was fined Si. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>COSTLIEST MEMORIAL WINDOW IN</head>
        <p>THE COSTLIEST MEMORIAL WINDOW IN</p>
        <p>TEE WORLD</p>
        <p>cannot be apprcciat,d except under favourable circum- stances. Looking at it from the outside, embedded in its huge frame,rne gets no idea of its beauties, hut merely sees the faint outline obscured by the deeper darkness within, and overshadowed by the building in which it rests. But go into the quaint old church ; stand where the SU descending ray is softly sifted through the colonred,srtistically enamslled ancl arranged prisms of 21aS5, and th-li you can see blended in one urand whole the beauties of art in a harmony pleasing to the dullest eye. One of the finest memorial tablets in the world is that recently erected by the Rev. James Thomas LlanwYd- delan Rectory, Newtown, Montgomeryshire. A tablet which meniqrisps his droll/fall:WS% one to which others may look with both confidence and admiration, and one which will stand the te-t for years. The words on this tablet are deeply cut in momenta of direst agony and. pain, arid every letter, every character,st ands out boldly carrsing invaluable iiwsnns to Ile all. The text of this rAblet-, is :—"For over four years I suffered severely from indigestion and dizziness, and dir•ing that time I had to abstain from taking many articles of food. I took several different medicines. but received no permanent beneti% At Ilie end of 1893 I resolved not to take any more medicine, but made op mc mind to bear my Sufferings as we I as I mild ; in fac,, was under the im- pressinn that I was dying, arid actually bade farewell to my family. It is bnpossitale to fully describe my suff-- ings. Owing to the extreme pain I suffered in the lark of the head, tits* sight was also serioudy affrcted. In , January, this year, a bottle of Warnet'a Safe Cure was sent me, and the send-r begged of ire, to try it. I did so. Before I finished its content., I felt much better. Three more were takeii,and now lam completely cured; can eat everything wi hunt being distressed after eat in. Indigestion and dizziness hare entirely left me, and, wVlinur any hedtation, I shall be most happy to arawer any questions with reference to Inv cure, which is the more wonder fol in view of iny ape (sixty years) arid the leng'h of time I had been a sufferer."</p>
      </div>
