      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. WARRINGTON v. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. WARRINGTON v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>The Warrington first team were engaged to play Leigh at Leigh on Saterday, but owing to a dispute between several of the regular players and the Committee as to the amount that should be paid for broken time, the team that met Leigh really consisted, of the " A" team assisted by three of the first`tearn players, namely, Bar- ber, Burton and Broady, to whom too much praise can- not be given for showing in so marked a manner that their love for the game is above " greed of gain." It is to be regretted that a dispute should have occurred with regard to the subject named, especially at this stage of the Northern Union's career, but we have no doubt that in it the Committee ot the Warriagton Club will not only receive the suppoit of the public of Warrington but of all lovers of the Rugby code. It will be remembered that the basis upon which the Northern Union is founded is that reasonable payment shall be made for broken time to all players who demand it. This reason- able payment was fixed at the sum of tb. Three or four men of the Warrington team refuse to accept anything, preferring to continae to ph.y the game tor their love of it and not with the object of making money, but the others demand the payment of more than 63. The dis- pute arose over the Bradford match. The players left Warrington at five minutes to one in the afternoon. Whatever broken time there might be it would appear that 63. would have been ample compensation, but it is said that some of the players demanded 93. and one or two even 123. Regarding the demand as exorbitant the Committee de- clined to comply with it, with the result that the players in question withdrew their names from the team that had been selected to play Leigh on Saturday. The Com- mittee accordingly selected a fresh team consisting prin- cipally of" A" team players but so well did they play against Leigh that in future they will probably form the first team, which means of course, that the discontented players will no longer be considered as belonging to the team. The team that journeyed to Leigh on Saturday was as follows r—Evans, full back; Barber (captain), Burton Lawless, and Broady, three quarters; Houghton - and Price, half backs: Burrows, Saunders, Barrett, An- drews, Thornton, Willeocks, Fairhurst and Savage, for- wards. The Leigh team consisted of their usual players namely, Cook, full back ; Cottarn, Parkes,Anderton and Wallwork, three-quarters ; Molyneux and T. Taylor. half hacks; Smith, Mac Masters, P. Taylor, Cheetham, Pemberton, Davies, Eccleston, and Boardm in forwards. In previous matches against Leigh,the Warrington team have gone to Leigh by 'bus, and this is probably the quickest means of reaching the rlace. On this occasion however,the train was given a trial,and in something over an hour Leigh,l2i miles away, was reached, this being considered good time. The Warrington players did not go in too confident a mood, but they went determined to give a good acceunt of themselves, and that they did so even the Leigh spectators were ready to admit while the score shows that it was by no means a runaway match. Having got over their nervousness, we fook forward to the team which went on the field on Satur- day doing credit to the Warrington club. They are a smart lot of fellows, and there are not a few superior players amongst them. The backs played well together, while the forwards played a dashing game, and elicited the admiration of th:. spectators. The Warrington team had decidedly hard lines. While it cannot be said that they had the best of the game, they had certainly more of the game than the score indicates. Within a very few minutes in the second half, they scored two tries,and near the finish they scored another,hut it was disallowed by the referee apparently on t'aa ground that the ball had been kicked over the line by a Leigh player, though a Warrington man dropped on it. Instead of giving a try he ordered a five yards scrimmage. The game was a moderately fast .ne. The weather was very hot, too much so to play football in comfort, but nothing daunted the captains decided to play two 35's. Leigh kicked off, but for a long time were unable to make headway against the visitors. Ultimately Leigh were awarded a free kick in front of the Warriugton goal on the ground that one ef the visitors had failed to play the ball. How he could have played the ball when he was " held " was not very apparent, but anyhow Coop took the free kick and had no difficulty in placing a penalty goal. Cheered by the fact that they had made first score, Leigh played with a little more spirit, and T. Taylor getting possession from the scrimmage managed to run in, Cook improving the point. Not disheartened, Warrington played up remarkably well, and a run by Barber was one of the most brilliant bits of play during the whole game. He looked likely for getting over, but , was brought down near the line, and half-time arrived with the sore, Leigh 8 points, Warrington nil. In the second half, however, Warrington scored as often as Leigh. Play was near the Leigh quarter flag, when the home team tried to effect a pass. Wilcocks got posses- sion and settled matters by grounding the ball over the line at the corner. Barton made a splendid try for goal. Immediately afterwards, Warrington again got over, the scorer this time being Broady. Barber took the place kick, but failed to improve the point. It now began to look as though Warrington had a chance, but Leigh by a spurt succeeded in putting on two more tries, the scorers being Anderton and T. Taylor, and a good game ended in favour of Leigh by 14 points to 6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MANNINGRAM v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MANNINGRAM v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Manninghain team had for their opponents at Valley Parade the Runcorn fifteen. The weather was again too hot for football. The teams selected were—Manningham ; Back, G. E. Lorimer ; threequarter hacks,Newton,Pickles,Brown,and Thomas; half backs, R. Sunderland and J. Williamson ; forwards A. II msclough, F. Clegg, Proctor, Wilkinson, Broadley, Bamford, Donaldson, and Whiteoak. Runcorn : Back, Butterworth ; threequarter backs, Warder, Robinson, Mvers,and Dunbavin ; half hacks, Abrams and J.Faulk- ner ; forwards, W. Faulkner, Langley, Gray-ter, Farmer, Johnson, Walker, Moores, and Williams. Faulkner started and a flying kick into touch soon caused a'scrimmage in the field. The Runcorn forwards got away with the ball at their toes, and Newton was called upon to save. He stopped the ball pluckily, and the game settled or a few moments within the home 25 line. Manningham had the best of the scrimmages which followed, and relieved the pressure, but smart passing by Faulkner and Abrams again sent the home stets back. Pickles contributed a serviceable kick, but the leather was quickly reterned, and scrimmages were fniwtit near the Mariningham p,st. WArder further im- prwed the Runcorn position, and the Paraders experi- enced a very warm time of it. From a scrimmage on the line Abrams picked ep and threw to Faulkner, who scored near the post. Butterworth's shot at goal.though failed to reach the mark. The home side dropped out, and at once invaded, Newton and Wilkinson being pro- minent. Runcorn put in some smart footwork, and Sunderland and Neehatn in the Manningham team ren- deted excellent service. Pickles was also conspicuous, and the b,ll was taken close to the Runaorn flag. The game VCIFI hotly enntested,both sides working very hard. Lorimer by a splendid kick sent the hall into touch near It's opponents' goal line, but Warder relieved by ; good play, and serimmaging ensued on the 25 line. This was followed by some grand passing cn the part of Faulkner, Abrams and Warder, who trans- ferred play 'o the other end of the field, Manningham I were then busily defending their lines, but got relief through a penalt7 kirk for off-side play on the part of their opponents. Abrams, Faulkner,and Warder passed prettily, and the last-named kicked into touch near the line Needham next got off with the ball at his toes, and dribbled up to the Runcorn 25 line before he was stopped. Runcorn was penalised. and the ball was placed for Lorimer, whose shot went a long way wide. I The drop out still found play in the visitors' half, and after a couple of long kicks by Lorimer the visitors had Fto concede a minor point. Clegg and Wilkinson were to the fore after the drop-out.andwere the means of eatlll- - ing Runcorn to defend their line. Newton kicked over the line,and Whiten-1k following uo felt en the ball, het the try was not allowed,and the scrimmage was ordered at the 25 line. FI tlf time arrived immediately after the , score—Runcorn, one try (three points ; Manningham, nil.</p>
        <p>„... Barraclough started the second half, and W. Faulkner replied to the centre. Runcorn immediately got away with some pretty paasing,and Myers ran along the tmach line,after a pass hr Rohinson,and was very nearly over. scrimmage to,ic place near the line, and Westmetting possession from Mcers,scored wide of the posts. Butter- worth failed at god. Even play followed the k;ck-out. the game far some time being warmly contested near the centre. The Runcorn back by superior tactics caused the Paraders to retire, and then J. Faulkner who picked up in the loose,scored another try. Butterworth again essayed to land a goal. Williamson and Sunder- land passed and kicked nicely, hut their efforts were neutralised hy Butterworth.who sent the bill into touch at the home quitter line. Sunderland. Williamson, and Needham tried another bout of passing,but the Runcorn hacks WorP far ten smart for them.and completely MS- tered them in every movement. Soon after,in the course of sore lomie play,tVilliamson go possession and scored and Lorimer landed a beautiful goal. J. Faulkner .earred from the c,incre,and the going to I...rimer he got in a grand kick to Ahrams. The latter sent the hall hack to Lorimer,who kicked into touch. A line-out and ' scriu,mage Premed. and then Newton got the bale from an rppnriPtit and ran in a grand trY,the spectators cheer- ing heartiiv. Lorimer landed a goal, and thus put the home side one point ahead. Even play followed the V.- V.:3 rt. The ba!l travelled rapidlr about the field,and the , game been MP VPrV fast . • The home forwards attacked, I and PMliles dropped at goal. The ball travelled wide, and Needham collocoing up smartly got the ball and placed a try. Lorimer made hut a poor shot at goal. hotne side were now playing up in much better form, and infm:d more spirit into the game than in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WIDNES v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>There were 3,066---i3-0-;:ciZTis.------ai this match on Saturday, which was played on the Lowerhouse ground,Widnes. Broughton started hostilities, and took up the attack, which was, however, of short duration, Archer relieving in fine style. Not to be deterred the visitors gave Brennan much trouble, and Clapham became dangerous. Again Brennan relieved the tension, and Barber getting hold made away amid cheers. He passed to Nevitt, who in turn passed to the right, but a grand interception by Hacking reversed affairs, and play was transferred to midfield. Widnes again made headway, and it was remarkable that they failed to score. The visitors now showed a tendency to roughness, and Mr. Slevin had to censure Brennan and Clapham, the latter of whom was the aggressive party. Widnes continued to hold their own, Barber being con- spicuous with a good sprint. Another ungeemly attack by the visitors' forwards ought to have been dealt with by the referee, but the incident was allowed to pass by, and matters cooled down. Rispan and Nevitt carried the leather into the visitor,' b.rritory, but Hacking effected a grand transference. Neither team gained an advant- age up t ) half thne,when the figures were Widnes none, Rangers none. In the second half a laudable attempt to score by Widnes was frustrated, and play on both sides deteriorated. The visitors' tactics became decidedly dubioug, and notwithstanding admonitions from the referee, rough play became the order of the day. Nevitt received a nice pass from Risnan, but failed to take ad- vantage of it and the opportunity was lost. Result, a pointless draw.</p>
        <p>Want/moron " A " v. LEIGH "A."—On Saturday the Leigh " A " team played Warrington on the Wilderspool ground. Owing to a number of" A" team players go- ing to Leigh, some cld members were on the ground at Warrington. The weather was extremely hot, making the game uncomfortable. Leigh kicked off and War- rington returned the ball to the centre. In the midst of some fiiie passing, one of the vii ors got possession of the ball and had a splendid eleane but did not seem to know whether to run or not. Just as he began to go ! for the goal line,he was collared and the ball went to the other end of toe field. Isherwood passed to Carey, who made a dash but was forced over the touch line. Soon a erwards Edelsten made a similar attempt,but he was not allowed to go far. Mason get away from a scrim- mage, but on being pressed, he kicked the ball, which fell short. Ile soon afterwards recovered it and pissed to Carey,out the latter failed to catch it. Hell time was now called, leaving both sides nil. During the second half, both teams played well, but Leigh were the most successful. Soon after resuming, Warrington were awarded a free kick, which Ashton took, but the ball went wide. Randles ran with the ball and got over the line but he was pulled back into play. Carey, however, soon got possession, and went over the line in fine style, leaving his opponents well behind him. Edelsten made a good attempt for the goal but he missed it. The Leigh men made severel unsuccessful attempts to break away, and finally, Hatton shot for the posts and sent the ball over.. Sion after that Roberts for the visitors scored a try, which was not converted. Warrington began to play hard, but although Carey, Edelsten,and Isherwood made some very good rushes, they failed to score and the game concluded with Leigh a drop goal and a try to Warrington one try. Although Warrington were beaten, they played an excellent game. The following were the players :—Warrington : Full back,Ashurst ; threequar- ters, Foster, Edelsten,lsherwood and Carey ; half backs, Foden and Mason ; forwards, Handles, M. Hughes, H. Ashton, Sinkey, Thorniley, Fox, Hampson,and Hughes. Leigh : Full back, J.Short ; threequarters,Price,Pendle- bury, Hatton,aud Lloyd ; half backs,Officer and Shovel- ton ; forwards, Roberts, Shaw, Hindle, Taylor, Arrow- smith, Phcenix, Ridyard, and Lawson. WARRINGTON' Sr. MARY'S V. RUNCORN "A."—Played at Runcorn on Saturday. St. Mary's lost the toss, and Thomas kicked off with the sun in his eyes. The visitors pressed from the start, and a rush by the forwards en- abled Gregory to score. at the corner, Harnpson failing at goal. A spell of passing by the Runcorn backs followed but Warburton's defence was excellent. The Saints were soon Pack, and Foy scored, Rou,gbley's kick going wide. After the restart the Runcorn men attacked,and Nickson receiving the bell from a line out, ran strongly to the line, but lost possession, Lightfoot completing the try, and the same player converting. Just before half time was called Bennett scored from a scrimmage for the visi- tors. Soon after the restart Robinson scored for the ! home team, Lightfoot kicking a goal. Runcorn added I another try shortly afterwards, the kick at goal this time failing. The Saints now livened up, and grand passing by the backs, took the ball to the line, where Edgar scored ,the game ending with St. Mary's attacking strongly, in a win for Runcorn by 13 points to 12 All the visitors played in fine form, a like remark applying to the Runcorn back division. Eatecesrown v. LIVERPOOL SOUTH END.—Earlestown on Saturday visited the Liverpool South End at the Shorefields enclosure. The encounter was a friendly one and Earlestown kicked off. The home team were first prominent, and after being forced back they forced their way towards Lee's charge, but a clearance was effected. Soon after this the visitors' forwards came with a bound. and when going strong Harvey came to the assistance of the home team and returned in splendid style. Then tracks were made for the Earlestown goal and after some pretty play on he home left-wing Hulse made a clever shot for goal, but John Lee was there and sent the ball to back division, who took it along at a nice pace, but the home defence was too good. Again play was taken inte the visitors half, Flulse and Morris on the left wing going very strong. Here Gordon got possession, and passing to Deighton he sent to Hulse who shot,hut it fell just a wee bit short. The visitors now went off with a big spurt and got well up the field. The right wing took the ball in hand wills the result that C. Lee sent the leather into the net,se.oring first for the visitors. After- wards, South End up to the finish of half-time continued to press. but without any reward. Half-time :—Earlee- town, 1 goal ; Liverpool South End, nil. On recnm- raencing the game South End were the first to show up prominently, and after being forced back for a brief period they came again, and then the Earlestown cus- todian received considerable attention. The home forwards were well on the ball, and Downes sent in a lovely shot, which went just outside the pest. Immedi- ately after this a clearance was effected, but the home left-wine got hold of Hulse and had the misfortune when shooting from the corner ot sending the ball just over the net. Soon after this a foul was awarded to the home team, and the kick being taken by Farmer h pissed over to the left wing, when by clever play Hulse shot the leather past Lee and put the Sonth End team on level terms with their opornents. The homesters were within an ace of scoring No. 2 immediately afterwards, and the final result was Liverpool South End 2 goals, Earles- town 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH V. THELWALL.—This match was played at Stockton Heath on Saturday. The start proved disastrous for Thelwall, as Webbe, security, from Probert, easily eluded Kyte, and racing down the left centred in fine style. Podinare having no difficulty in notching a goal one minute from the start. The homes- ters, infused with this early success, were soon again on the attack, Heald this time doing the needful. Thel wail now had a look in, Attewell being at fault, but the ball was kicked over. The goal kick brought relief, a series of corners falling to Stockton Heath,but Williamson kept his charge intact. The visi'ing forwards next raced away, hut their eff irts lackod finish. The Uleathens were now placing in a determined manner, and after Williamson and Jones had cleared several shots. 0 dons scored a somewhat lucky anal, with a long dropping shot. Hive-and-take play followed, and it looked as if the scoring had finished for this half at least, hut this did not prove the case as Pd more put number four through just on sell of time. In the second half the Thelwall forwards looked very iouch like scoring the homesters being compelled to concede a corner. After Probert had struck the bar, the same player, again securing, scored with a Idaily cutter. 'roe game ended in a win for Sockton Heath tw five goals to nil. STocKTON FIKATW RESERVE V. RANE-SV.-4ta.yed on the Bank-st. grimed. The visitors won the toss, and at once pressed, Kimberley shooting through five minutes from the start. A visit was paid to the other end, and the homesters quiekly equalised. Hinds againstSteck- ton Heath gave Bank-st. a free kick close in,and Evans in trying to head away Sent the hall through his own goal. Some good combination lw the Heathen's f.n*Ward changed the venue of play, Holford neatly passing the ball to Kimberley, for that player to quickly ham, the hall through,thus (ci ialising matters. During the second half play ruled even, and the game nded in a draw of three goals ea rh RUNCORN ASSOCIATION V. MOSSLEY HILL.—The visitors to the Union-st. ground, Runcorn, on Saturday were Mossley Hill. The teams lined up as follows :—Hun- corn Hough full backs, Green and Walker; half bscks, Rye, Paton, Woodward ; forwards, Blower, Walker. Bathe, Jones, and Pmaeriek (captain). Mossley Bill: Goal, Evans; full back, McKay and Dow. kin s ; half haeks, SW4 004. Nixon and Atkinson ; for- wards, Roberts, Hughes, Evans. Cottier. and Lodge. The visitors winning he toss, Baylie opened the contest with a brilliant son avtinst him, and the Blues were snort vnalwards. Evans gathered the bill and held it I whilst waitiov. for the deei&lt;inn of the referee on his claim for a goal, and was rushed through the goal with the hall in his possession, the referee allowing the point, iniieh to his surprise. Immodiatelc afterwards Pye secured and sent across the field, and 13;tylio clearing McKay Nril Nixon beat Evans again with a shot just in- side the post The visitors rather taken by surprise,now roused up, and made things rather lively. Lodge sending in some splendid centres, hut nothing came of them. Paton and Pye relieved, and pixy was taken more to- wards the centre, but not to be denied Evans, Cottier, and fridoe again came along' and a weak clearance, by the Blues' custodian allowed Lod to a elan opening. He made a grand shot, which passed heaulfully under the bar. Baylie again put the ball in motion, and Blowe</p>
        <p>and Walker were nusy, the tormer putting in some good centres and giving Dawkins a little outing. Ultimately McKay cleared his lines with an enormous Lick, and Roberts raced away, but Woodward checked his career. The Mossley men now played hard for the needful equalizer, which came in rather a s)tt manner. The ball was sent sailing into the goal-mouth by Atkinson, where the Blues' goalkeeper preferred to nurse the leather, and Evans and Hughes promptly despatched it through the posts. Play was fairly even during theremainder of the first half, neither goalkeeper being troubled much, and at half-time the score was—Runcorn, two goals; Moss- ley Hill, two goals. After a short interval Evans com- menced the second portion of the proceedings, but Paton secured, and Peacock was very soon scudding towards Evans, but a dashing bit of play by McKay nullified his efforts. The homes halves played well, and the ball was soon near the visitors' goal. Lodge and DAwkins now changed places, but Baylie would not be beaten, and put the Blues ahead with a swift low shot. From the kick- off Cottier and Evans made desperate efforts to break through the defence, but were stoutly resisted by Wood- ward, Paton, and Pye, who spoiled all attempts. Blower and Walker were prominent. The former, getting pos- session, made a good centre, which Peacock met, and thereby registered the fourth goal for Runcorn. The visitors were nearly played out, and Feacock again banged through, but the point was disallowed for a breach of the off-side rule. The home halves and forwards played a fine game to a man. Final result : Runcorn, four goals ; Mossley Hill, two goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>APPLETON PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday even- ing in the National Schools, Stockton Heath, under the chairmanship of Mr. C. W. Davenport There were also present—Messrs. H. Eyes, T. Hampson, Pearson, J. Britch, J. Hedgcock, Hugh Derbyshire, R. Roughsedge, A. Hayes, and J. Ellison (clerk). The Clerk reported that the accounts had been audited and that on the 30th March the balance in favour of the Council was £32 193. 6d. Since then certain amounts had been paid which brought the balance down to about £3O. This was quite enough to pay all present , demands.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson drew attention to a nuisance which existed between the north and south approaches to the Stockton Heath swing bridge. He proposed that the Clerk should write to the County Council requesting the Canal Company to put the path in order, as it was understood, that it was the duty of the Canal Company to do so- The Chairman said that the men who were employed by the County Council to scrape the roads very often deposited heaps of the scrapings into very in- convenient places along the new road. I=l,-. asked- Mr. Pearson if it would not be well to embody in his pro- position that the County Surveyor be asked to give in- structions to remedy this nuisance.—Mr. Pearson agreed to this course being taken, and the Clerk was conse- quently instructed to act accordingly.</p>
        <p>Mr. 'Pearson, in accordance with notice of motion, broached the. question of the alleged encroachment made upon the highway in Lffidon-road,near to London Bridge, by Mr. Warburton. He observed that at the last meeting of the Council Mr. Ellison had been in- structed to inquire into the matter,and to ascertain if an encroachment had taken place. Perhaps the Clerk would inform the Council what steps he hadtaken.— Mr. Ellison remarked that from information he had re- ceived from a very good source it appeared that between thirty and forty years ago the land referred to was en- croached upon. It had before then been used for vari- ous purposes by the public. He (the clerk) understood that if any land or property belonging to the Crown or public was encroached upon it could always be re- claimed, no matter how long ago the encroachment had taken place. There were persons now living who could give evidence calculated to prove that the land had belonged to and had been used by the public between thirty and forty years ago.— Mr. Pearson : It is a very useful piec, of land, and if we lose it now we lose it for ever.—The Clerk observed that he had looked up the minute books,but could find no re- oord of any arram*ement whatever having been come to by the Highway Board and Mr. Wirhurton as to the handing ever to him of the land in question.—Mr. Pear- son moved that the Clerk write to the District Council asking them to ask Mr. Warburton to give back the land to the public. If that Council did not take the matter up they could apply to the County Council, and from them to the Local Government Board, but he did not anticipate such difficulty.—Some discussion ensued, in the course of which the Chairman r,-marked that he thought Mr. Warburton was quite gentleman enough to give the land back if he was once satisfied that he was encroaching up-in the public highway.—Mr. Pearson agreed to withdraw 'iis proposition, and in place of it he moved that Mr. Warburton be first asked to return the land, and failing his compliance with the request that the necessary steps be taken to reclaim it.—Mr. Butch seconded, and the motion was carried.—Mr. Hayes ob- served that what he believed to be another encroach- ment had occurred in Pepper-st.—The Council decided not to go in'ro that matter then, but to leave it for a future occasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly riveting of this Board was held in the Board-room at Dutton Workhouse on Tue,day afternoon. Mr. Knowles occupied the chair, and in addition to the members of the Rural Council bring present there were, the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, Messrs. S. Taylor, LP.. C.C., A. R. Norman, S. Yarwood, T. Lea, and Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), and Mr. Cunnah (workhouse master)..,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LOSS OF A MEMBER.</head>
        <p>THE LOSS OF A MEMBER.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Brookes Handley, of Ferncliffe, Runcorn, con- veyed to the Board the sincere thanks of his mother and the family for the kind expri scion of sympathy with them in their bereavement. They had been deeply touched b 7 the many kindly remembrances of them in their sorrow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>THE POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman and Mr. Brandreth gave a brief report of their visit to the Poor Law Conference. They touched upon the most important business brought forward, referring specially to the question of the classification of paupers. They bore their testimony to the usefulness of the conference, stating that they heard there,some sound ideas brought forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>TIE EXTENSION OF THE WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>TIE EXTENSION OF THE WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>With regard to the enlargement of the men's ward at the workhouse,the Chairman explained that the contract of Mr. T. Kirkham, of Runcorn, for £163, had been ac- cepted by the committee —Mr.Aaron moved the accept- ance of the tender.—Mr. Yarwood seconded, and it was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>VACANCY ON THE ASESSMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE VACANCY ON THE ASESSMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>In accordance with notice given, Mr. Heywood pro- posed a new nember for the Assessment Committee in the place of Mr. Handley, whom they grieved to have lost through death. He proposed the name of Mr. Wright, a gentleman whose attendance on that Board,and whose business capacities would meet with approval.—Mr. Williams seconded the proposition.—Mr. Aaron in pro- posing an amendment said he did not doubt that Mr. Wright was a proper person to sit on the Assessment Committee, but they must take into consideration that Runcorn paid one fourth of the rates, and if Mr. Wright was placed on the committee,Runcorn would then only have one member. He thought it would be a great hard- ship to Runcorn if that was so,and he proposed that Mr. Norman b 9 aKointed in the place of Mr.fiandlev.—Mr. 'rajlor s-conded the amendment. He said he would have liked Mr. Wright on the committee,but at the same time he did not think it was in the interests of the Union to have only one representative for Runcorn to take the responsibility of so large a rateable value.—Mr. Yar- wood supported the amendment for the sums reason.— Mr. Heywood thought Halton was entitled to a represen- tative —Mr. Wright wished to withdraw his name with the consent of the proposer and seconder.—This was I agreed to, and Mr. Norman was appointed on the com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>In suhmitting the'report of the Finance Committee,Mr. Heywood stated that £B5 had been raid for out-relief at Runcorn during the fortnight ; £25 Frodsham ; and £lB Bud worth. He was pleased to state that there was not one township in arrears with the (;all. (Rear, hear.)—Mr. Woodward seconded the adoption of the re- port and it was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>VISIT TO THE ASYLUM.</head>
        <p>VISIT TO THE ASYLUM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stokes submittod a report of the nommittee who visited Chester Asylum on behalf of the 13-_,•ard. He stated that the members who attended were Admiral Parry, Mr. Wright, the Clerk, and himself • They were agreeably surprised to find the great comforts which existed for the inmates,aud they had a very satisfActory report to make with respect to the inmates sent from that Union to the Asylum.—Mr. Wright added his tes- timony to the excellent arrangements existing at the Asylum.—The report was adopted. THE LADIES' COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Wood wished to know whether the Clerk had considered the question of the Ladies' Gin, tnittee and their prncedure,which was discussed at the last mepting„and referred to the Clerk for consideration. —The Clerk suggested that when the Ladies' Committee Made their recommendations the should write them on the left hand side of the page only in their report hook, leaving half the sheet for the Visiting Committee to make their sumstions thereto for the consideration of the Board—The Rev. Canon Wood said he felt sure such an arrangement would work satisfactorily,and proposed hat that; method be adopted.—Mr. Aaron seconded, and it was vre.,(l tr.. CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>Tenders were accented from the following Messrs. Yarwond, Runcorn, carting ; Mr. J. H. Weston, Run- corn, drugs ; Jeyes Sanitary Company, London. disin- fectants ; Messrs. Fletcher and LOP, Warrington,dranery and clothing ; Messrs. Singleton and Stephenson, War- rington, meat ; Mr. Jos. Smith, Strettnn, potatoes and wheat straw ; Mr. Joseph Thzley. Runcorn, shoes ; Sanitas Companr. London, disinfectants ; Messrs. R. Evans and c- n., Limited, Haydock, coal ; Mr. Rairnw, Halton. coffins ; Mr. Grounds, Aston, cartage; and Mr. Walter Collier, Runcorn, groceries. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. THOMAS ROBERTS, FonnrEnLY 0</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. The death of Mr. Thomas Roberts, formerly o Warrington, is announced as having taken place 01 Saturday at Oldham. It will be remembered tha the deceased was for some years employed e Messrs. Waring 's tannery, Winwick-st., of whiC firm his father, Mr. John Roberts, is still managincl director. About two years ago, however, he IC'' Warrington, and entered the service of a prominen, tanning firm in Manchester. Mr. Roberts was more than 30 years of age. AMERICAN ROCKER CHAIRS, Sale price, 12s. 5. 1111°3° TABLES, with two trays. sale price, 45. 11d.—J. and W. D VITO ankey-at., Warrington.— .t THE LEAGUE OF THE CROSS. The usual weekly meeting of the League the Cross was held in the League nal Bewsey-st., on Sunday, Mr. John Mullins,presioenl in the chair. The Vice-President, Mr. J. C paltol addressed the meeting on the question of abstinence, urging the absolute necessity of tne": depre purpose amongst temperance workers. He cated the action of a certain minister in the town who was a prominent figure in the Temperanni Federation, making an unfair attack on Catholi3 and Roman faith. They all knew that thl committee of the League of the Cross were In anxious to take a fair share in united temPt"rancrl work, but unless these unfair attacks on their re,' gion ceased to occur, they would be obliged necessity to separate themselves trom the Feflerf!' tion. He was happy to be able to say that so far a he knew, the action of the rev, gentleman refeW( to was deprecated by some of the members of th Federation. He concluded his address by agin/ the workers in the League to work with great! earnestness than ever in the goed cause of tenWe't ance. The President, Mr. Mullins,gave a brief o.,tie line of early Irish history, which was very instr. tive and amusing.—The annual ball of the Lean was held in the Co-operative Ball, Winwick-st., Monday night, and was a great success, the hal being well patronised. el WRINGING MACHINES. GARLAND, ironinong</p>
        <p>Warrington. SERIOUS ILLNESS OF DIRS. QUEIKETT. We understand that Mrs. Quekett, widow of th late Rev. William Quekett, for many years rector° Warrington, is lying dangerously ill at the Wax rington Rectory. Mrs. Quekett cmme to Warrit4 ton on Friday last week.on a short visit, to the 111 Daresbury, arid on Sunday spent the day with t, Rev. Canon and Mrs. Willis, and also attended harvest festival services at the rarish Church. Su,, was out visiting on Monday, and when at the, heti; of one of her former friends, at about one 0cloe! it was apparent that she was indisposed, and en expressed a wish to return at once to the Reel She was driven there, and while in the act of lehaa ing her carriage was seized with a stroke end ofl to be carried into the house and placed on a s_ Subsequently she was carried into her bedrooteoe,. her condition had by this time become aerl.o,; that it was deemed necessary to call in Me di]) assistance, and she has since been attended bYwi White. On Tuesday a telegraphic message sent to her son, Mr. R. G. Webster, Mfg des`r" his immediate attendance. SEWING MACHINES, Hand z-6a. oil. Treadle £4 4'• handsome walnut coven—Y and W. DUTTON, 16 f'n Sankevlt.. Warrington.— 4dvt. ST. ELPHINS CLERGY DAUGHTERS' Sell°°l"</p>
        <p>W ARRINGTON. The following list of successes of Itl of this institution in the Cambrido , Local Examination has been published Bryan, arithmetic, history ; M. 111 Cottinql".l.7 Scripture Class ii, English literature, metic, history ; I. Harris, French' ' • C. M. liedle3r!'''frEiri lish literature, arithmetic • M Sullivan, Ser!t'd- -1 Class ii., French, arithmetic ' • S. Withers,Serln?,- Class ii., arithmetic. The Ox ford SnlOr q loca'q amination list is SR follows :—W, Harry. Citlrtl distinction in Scripture and English B• Class iii., distinction in Scripture and EnFrende ghs- I i, D'Ombrain,Pass, distinction in English and e?ccused responsions in French • H. Giles, Pa`,°?ii•i; tinction in English : M. Norris' pass •, C. Fr,ul,4-I.r Paso, distinction in scripture slid English. v°o Junior Local Examination M. North, Bin!.`s distinction in Scripture ; B. Hedley, Class i tinction in English ;B. Cotton ass' distinction.° French and English ; M. Holdi;ch = Pass, distinctli in English ;E. Ash, pass • 3 Jo'hnstone, Patino Jones, Pass. Oxford Preliminary 'Local tion :E. Knowles, pass ;L. Willington, o°' t risri LANN NIOWRIIS. -GARLAND Ironmonger.</p>
        <p>and Earleetown.</p>
        <p>CHURCH LADS'.BRIGADE. f The Warrington (2nd Liverpool) Battalion °- above, on Saturday. left Bank Quay at, 2.40 ' lio Wigan, to join the Wigan (Ist Liverpool) Batta,ei at drill in a field belonging to Dr. Stuart, ALT, the td Oaks, Standish. Dr. Stuart not only asked vide battalions to drill in his field, but also ls substantial meal for the lads after wards.and he °de invited a large number of private guesta t° 8129'11'1ot party to see the lads. The Warrington beta— _ numbering 12 offieers and 132 boys, under the Tau mand of its colonel (Captain 13uckton), marenli° the main street of Wigan, headed by the hand (Sankey Comnanv) and soon joined the battalion. numbering 250. which was under),ao hatta lf nte command of its own colonel (Major Darline°lllget marched togeth,-r to Standish. On reaching, „ c Oaks the lads were formed into one which Major Darlington took command, a" - 591 h giving the general salute, Timeeedwi to marc go and, considering the uneven surface of tb.:3l,,f,vei and the great beat,acquitted themsel ai ve. fr" MYAfter again forming line, cohaai ed inspected the ranks, and afterwards addr°,soBrast few kindly word of approval and encolVfti to the lads. The Warrington Battalion reaerte-mrat, rington at 8.45, rather tired, but thorough'U'e et r fill for R very pleasant afternoon, and f() d the couragernent they had received from an ness of Dr. and Mr.. Stuart. SRWIN6 MACHIN:RR Hand £2 10 T R 4 ' handiorne w,lnut crwer.—J and 4, DUTTON, 16 Sn</p>
