      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>Edmond Abont, in one of his last contributions to the " Revue de Deux Mendes," suggested that the political history of several nations could be written in the form of a compendium of national epigrams and vaudevilles—a sort ot facetious ditties in which the French are rivalled only by their Italian neighbours. A collection of historical nicknames would, however, writes F. L. Oswald, in the" North American Review," serve the same purpose in a still more physical and moral characteristics of an individual, and sometimes of a party or even a whole nation. " What are the main tendencies of your Liberal and Serviles,as your Highness has begun to call them ?" a German politican asked Prince de Lig,ne, the Austrian Chesterfield. " Well, you see, our Serviles want ' sehr vieles ' (a good many things), but our Liberals want ' Heber ailed ' (rather everything)," said the keen-wittai courtier. When the braggard Bernadotte had got himself elected Crown Prince of Sweden, he did his Lest to propitiate public opinion of all around by assuming the name of Charles Jean. loading foreign diplomatists with decorations, and offering his services as meditator between France and the victorious allies ; but his old companions-in-arms had seized him up to an inch, and nicknamed him " Charles Jean Charltatan." Complacent King Joseph they called " le roi par ordre ;" and the depredations of General Vandamme were commemorated in the epithet " Jacques Brigand." For Napoleon himself, his soldiers had only affectionate nicknames—" The Little Cor- poral," " Little Wideawake ;" but Madame de Stael, in a fit of resentment, called him " Robespierre on horse- back" (Robespierre a cheval), and the nicknl,me stuck like the pun of that Ghent alderman who !bribed the retail butchers of his city (locally known as les petits bouchers) to get up a transparency with the inscription " The Little Butchers of Ghent to Napoleon the Great." The Grand Butcher was not apt to forgive a personal squib of that kind, but, nevertheless, almost choked with laughing when Count Las Cases at Longwood ventured to acquaint him with the popular nickname of his royal brother-in-law—Murat. The parvenue King of Naples was incorrigibly tend of dressing in theatrical finery—gold lace jackets with broad lace collars, and blue velvet surtouts, and in allusion to that foible the Parisian wits called him " King Franconi," Franconi's opera being a flashy pleasure resort of the French capital. Louis XVlll.they called " Gres Revenue." to commeme- rate a highly treasonable pun of a witty Imperialist, who had heard his comrades complain of the enormous taxes of the new regime. " Never mind, payons, payons,nous avons un gros revenu "—" We have a large revenue," the three last words meaning also "a returned fat man." Alter the battle of Waterloo, they called their well-led Sovereign Louis " deux fois neuf "—"twice nine,"with the additional meaning of " twice two." Those puns had much to do with the expulsion of the Bourbons,and it might be questioned if all the speeches of the Jacobite hurt the cause of the Royal family as much as the Queen's nickname " Madame Veto." That those same Jacobins were capable of self-banter is, however, proved by the sobriquet of the frivolous cut-throat Barere, " the An- acreon of the Guillotine." With a similar humour, the wits of the Napoleonic era called the flunkey naturalist Lacepede (a great authority on snakes) "Time chief of the reptiles.' " The Deity rested after the creation of Napoleon the Great," the eloquent professor concluded one of his characteristic speeches. " A pity that the Deity did not rest a little sooner," said the Count de Narbonne. As a rule, the Imperalists would not per- mit the humorists of any other nation to quiz their new-made potentates, but they could not help endorsing the verdict of the Hessians who called their profligate king (Brother Jeromi) "Koenig Don Juan." In the Crown Prince phase of his existence, Kaiser Wilhelm, the Victor of Sadowa and Sedan, had made himself so unpopular that the Berliners had called him the " Kartatschen Prinz" (the Grape and Canister Prince) and demolished his metropolitan palace. The brother of the Canister Prince had a constitutional horror of gunpowder, and worshipped Bacchus rather to the neglect of Mars,but he was so affable to interviewers of all parties that he got off with the nickname of " Champagne Freddie" (Der Champagner Fritz)'. All in all, he was about the easiest going king that ever con- trived to maintain himself on a storm-tossed throne, and when the burgomaster of a rather democratic Rhineland city presented him with a bumper of wine, " warranted as pure as our citizens' loyalty to your royal house," his majesty merely held the glass against the light, and whispered : " Vintage of forty-eight ?"—the year of the Rhenish insurrection. Most people have naturally an utter detestation of a nickname ; but it is related of Marshal Vendome that.he used to say he would willingly forfeit all his titles sooner than his soubriquet, " General Bonhemme." With all his cynicisms, be was indeed " bonhomie" personified, and once pardoned a petty marauder for the sake of his ready wit. "So they are going to bang you ? Serves you right; only a scoundrel will risk his life for ten francs." " Ah, my general,how often had I to risk my life for ten coppers?" (the daily pay of a French soldier), said the delinquent, and was at once dismissed with a laugh and the admoni- tion to" keep his neck greased for the next time." The slang-loving old campaigner had a vein of pathos, too; and in his last moment, when a friend tried to draw the stiff curtains of the Spanish chateau to keep the moon from shining into the sick room, the dying veteran beckoned him to desist, saying :—Laissez-ca ; je vois la grande Ombre de l'Eternite qui s'avance "—" Never mind that ; I see the shadow of Eternity is fast approach- ing. It will save you the trouble."</p>
