      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>lettero to the ehttor.</head>
        <p>lettero to the ehttor.</p>
        <p>41.1-MR. W. IL JUDE'S "MUSICAL EVENING." shoula be obliged if you will allow me to state that the" Musical Evening " advertised through the medium of y-ur pap,r had to be postponed in conse- sequence of a sudden attack of acute rheumatism. Fur- ther notice will be is•med•as soon as arrangements can be made. In the meantime we beg to ask the indulgence ef those who have purchased tickets. On behalf of the dancert Committee, I remain yours respectfully, JAMES RICHARDSON, (Hon. Secretary.) 7, South Bank Terrace, Runcorn, Oct. 3, 1895.</p>
        <p>TRE PROPOSED TESTIMONIAL TO TEM CHIEF</p>
        <p>CONSTABLE OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>filir,—At present there seems to be considerable con- fusion in the public mind about the suggested presenta- tion to our respected Chief Constable. The Watch Com- mittee having recommended the voting of sum of money towards this object, does it not seem singular tkat this movement having originated from an official source shpiild now pass into the hands of a few private individuals ? I have asked several of our Magistrates, Town Councillors, and other prominent townsmen about the matter, and lam astonished to find that they were net invited to attend the meeting which has been held upon the subject, and I greatly fear that tbe ill-advised people who have constituted themselves the committee must be field responsible if the movement is a failure. Surely this is a matter which might very properly have been left to the Watch Committee who could have re- quested the Mayor as its chairman to call a meeting of our leading inhabiiants and invite subscriptions. Hop- ing that my suggestion will commend itself to those in authority.---Yours,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>OBSERVER.</head>
        <p>OBSERVER.</p>
        <p>THE WATER. POLO FINAL. Sir,—Although the result of last Tuesday's match be- tween Warrington and St.fielens is not yet accepted ow- ing to a protest being lai,l against St. Helens on account Of the eligibility of Shasvcross as a player, yet, the vapathv shown by the Warrington team in the first half, when defending the shallow end was highly culpable. in the second half, when defending the deep end, which naturally is much mare difficult,they appeared to realise ifor the first time that they were playing in an important match and then, entirely held their opponents in check. Jia the event of the protest being upheld and the game replayed—this time at Warrington—if the home team will display the same form as they showed in the latter stages of Tuesday'4 game vic‘ory most assuredly will rest with them.-1 ours. kc., •</p>
        <p>SPECTATOR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>October 2, 1895.</head>
        <p>October 2, 1895.</p>
        <p>AFTER THE POLITICAL BATTL'E.</p>
        <p>- Sir,—The masaes have been sold again, and I have no doubt tt use who have sod th^m are getting the money. And I have no doubt they will also ultimately get the tensions there has been so much talk about. There will be none for the aged poor, but there will be some for Joseph and his Brethren when they resign their places in the present Parliament. Now. I think voters require protecting from unscrupulous politicians. The law Should not allow voters to be duped with impunity, and I think those who dupe them should be convicted kr the .f,-nce like anyone else who obtains articles by false pretences, or at least elections so won should be declared Bull and void. The most deplorable result of the late general election is that it perpetuates Lord Rule in Eng- 'land. It appears to me that the men who promised the moat to the electors have the leaqt power to fulfil these promises even if they had the inclination to fulfil them. . 4 coalition government seems one well qualified to dupe the electors. One section can promise the electors a great deal, and the leader of the other section can tell the country these promises caunot be fulfilled. I con. rider the ceuntry should not heed anyone but the leaders ei the different parties. They should not heed such a 000 as Mr. Chamberlain for instance, for his supporters are not numerically strong enough to carry out his pro- ,grams.—Yurs truly, R.M.B.</p>
        <p>-TRAGIC DEATH OF A SUPPOSED RUNCORN EN GIN ERR. AN INQUIRY FOR 'ILE RELATIVES. Sir,—Will you kindly publish in your paper the facts -that I here send to you. On November 13th the last year (11594) while on our way from Bombay to San Francisco Mr. William Thomason. the engineer on duty, Was examining one of the bcilers which had begun to leak. He had just opened the furnace door, and was looking in the furnace when one of the tubes burst blowing the fire out of the door. Between the steam and the fire he WIS terribly burned, so much so that we could not take off his clothes: both his eyes were burned out, and all the hair off his head. Be only lived for two sours and the same day he was buried it sea. I have tried to find his friends but could not do so, and as he had a wife living somewhere in England,l write as a last trial to you. The reason I wrote to you is this. Oa stir last trip home I had to clean out a tool chest in the engine room which had not been touched since his death and at the bottom I found an old waistcoat belonging to him. also part of a newspaper which was the •' Runcorn Examiner.' In the ID, kot of the coat I found the en- closed ticket, which has 115 name on it showing that he left Liverpool on July 3 , 1893. Seeing that by the: ticket he Came from Runcorn and that a Runcorn news- paper was with his clothes I thought the best thing I could do would be to write and ask you to publish the facts. I enclose the ticket and if any of his friends apply for it please give it to them. We were four days sail out Irons Bombay when the accident happened. If you will do this it will be a great deal of satisfaction for me as I ;bays done what I could to find his friends,as he was one o the best of friends to me.—Yours truly, THOMAS B. ARMSTRONG.</p>
        <p>Steamer Cotonna. San Francisco, California, September 13 h, 1895. I have no address of my own but letters sent to me Set the Central Pose Offie in San Francisco will find me. [The ticket or" inspection card " referred to in the above letter gives William Thomason as the " name of Immigrant" and ftencorn as his last residence. Thomason bad evidently left Liverpool for the United States on July 28th, 1893, in the vessel Lord Gough. We shall be glad to comply with the rrqueet of the writer of the letter, and hand the card over to any of the deceased man's friends who may apply for it.—En., iiftaattaiser].</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>DROWNING CASES AT SANKEY</head>
        <p>DROWNING CASES AT SANKEY</p>
        <p>B ttl DO ES.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouae held an inquest at the Black Horse, ridankey Bridges, on Wednesday afternoon, on the body of John Forster,aged 7 years, who was found in the Sankey and St. Helens Canal on Seturday.—alice Fors- ter, mother of the deeeesed, said that she resided at 182, Idverpool-rna.d. She last saw her son alive on Saturday znorning. It about 19 361 the some morning, a little boy named William Farmer came to her house and said that her son was in the canal. The body was brought home half-an-hour atterwards.—The boy Farmer was called a,nd said that the deceased was trying to get a stick out oif the canal when he slipped in. Witness told a man who was some distance away, and then went to tell the bey's mother.—James Thornley, waterrnan, gave evi- denoe to grappling for the hnd Is and pulling it out near the aide of tilt canal. In reply to the Coroner, P.S. Devlin said that the road along the canal was a towing path. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," Mr. Brighouse also held an inquest am the body of Richard Worsley Cooper, aged 58 years, who was found in the Sankey and St. Helens Canal on Tuesday morn- hag.—Maud Clensy said th.t she was the daughter of -deceased and she rosided at 77 Talieson-et., Liverpool. Tier father was a cabinet maker, and on Wednesday he -me from her sister's house in Rainhill to stay with ber until Monday morning. At 8.30 that morning, he left her house to go to another sister's house in Kirkdale at which place he arrive.l about seven o'clock He left the latter place the same day to go back to Rainhill. Witness knew of no reason why he should come to 1 Sankey Bridges. Reload a distant cousin in Latchford ' but he never said anything about ker. He never threa- tened to take away his life and there was no reason for hins to do so. He had no 'waled home, hut lived at his daughters' homes in turn.—Rmily Duncan, 101 Roslyn- at., irk dale, another daughter of deceased, said ti A her father only stayed with her from 820 until 9.30 on Mondav morning, and then lett saying he was going to Rainhill. le might have been making his way to Latch- ford. She never suspected him of any designs to take away his life.--Jan Thompson, platelayer, said that on the merning in question he was engaged at " fogging " at Sankey Bridged. It Was very dark and foggy. At about 3.25 he saw a body in the water, and on going to it he found that it was the body of deneased. It was boo far from the side to get It cut, add as witness could sot leave his work at the time, the body Was not got out of the water until nine o'clock. lie found a jacket and cap on the bank at about five o'clock.—The Coroner : Why Was not the body pulled out before nine o'cloek. The man might have been alive when first seen.—Witness: Re WAR alive when I first saw him I Raw him en down. He got into the water at about 320. I s4TV him drowning and spoke to, him, but he never spoke bark. It WAR very foggy at the time and I had a train to attend ro.—The Coroner It is strange to leave the body in the water so long. Could you not get assistance P—Witness : I could not. I had any work on the line to attend to. The man never at- tempted to help himself.—The Coroner in summing up said that it was a very curious thing to leave the body In the water so long. It was also very singular that de- cant/4Pd should be near Warrinetrin at all. The fact that the jacket and cap were on the bank seemed to point to suicide, but he would suggest to the jury that the safest verdict to find would be" Found drowned.".—The jury eturned a verdict is aceordaneewith itheVorenerie dvite.</p>
      </div>
