      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN &amp;ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRIINKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Ear:estown police station on Monday morning. before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Thomas Wil- liams of 13 Parr Mill, St. Helens. was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street Earles- town on Saturday evening. He was Rued 2s, 6d. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>BOWLING HAN DICAP AT.EAIILESTOWN.</head>
        <p>BOWLING HAN DICAP AT.EAIILESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The --iiii.jcuct Institute bowling club played off their handicap for Mr. A. Tunstall's prize, on Satur- day afternoon, in splendid weather. A large num- ber of spectators were present, who watched the various beats with interest. The first prize (Mr. Tunstall's) was secured by Mr. J. Edgar, 2nd prize (copper kettle) Mr. R. Partington, 3rd prize (set of jugs) Mr. J. Be lworth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>SONS OP TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>SONS OP TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above a social evening was held in the Co-operative Hall, on. Wednesday eve fling. The proceedings included a tea,at which there was a good number preseet. The subsequent proceedings consisted of an address by Bro. Fedder, G.S., of Manchester, and an exhibition of sleight of hand by Bro. B. Teighlor, of Manchester, was much enjoyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLAR&amp;</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR&amp;</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templars, VMS held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. One candidate was admitted to membership. Bro. France, V.D., of Leigh, was present, and gave a short address. The lodge was formerly closed at 8 o'clock. The programme of the evening was then proceeded with, being an "At Home" for which Bro. Barker was responsible. Various games were indulged in, and at 9 o'clock light refreshments were handed round, and a pleasant evening was spent. LADIES' PICNIC FRONK EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Monday a ;Umber of ladies connected with the Wesleyan ladies' sewing meeting class, had a thoroughly enjoyable out to Halshead Park, the residence of Captain Willis, Whiston, near Prescot. The day was beautifully fine, and leaving Earles- town in wagonnettes at 11 o'clock, the drive through the country was of the pleasantest kind. On arrival at the park they dispersed to enjoy the beautiful grounds, which they commented upon enthusiastically. Tea was partaken of at the Wes- leyan Church, Whiston, and the return journey was completed about eight o'clock. 1.0.G.T. (EBENEZER LODGE). The usual weekly session ot the Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge, No. 752, was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Tuesday evening. Bro. W. Hatton, CT., presided, and there was a good attendance of members. A vote of condolence was passed and the Secretary instructed to tender the same to Bro. and Sister Singleton in their sad bereavement. Arrangements were also made for the visit of the Tyldesley members on October &amp;h, and it was de- cided to have an open night. Afterwards the pro- gramme, " A Night with Sankey," was proceeded with, Sister Mrs. Brown, chap., being responsible. A verypleasant evening was brought to a close at 930. FARM SCHOOL REFORMATORY, NEWTON.—PRIZ</p>
        <p>- DISTRIBUTION. On Friday afternoon Alderman Watts,Lord Mayor of Liverpool,attended at the Liverpool Farm School Reformatory,Newton-le-Willows, tor the purpose of distributing the prizes to those of the inmates who had been successful in obtaining the awards. Amongst those present were the Revs. J. T. Cam, J. Allatt, Child (chaplain), Captain Hicks, Mr.E. W. Cropper (treasurerl,Mr. R. S. Blease (socretary).and others, including a good sprinkling of ladies. Pre- vious to the distribution the school band played a selection of music in the yard, which was much en- joyed. From the official report of the school it ap- pears that the number of boys under detention at the end of 189 i was 144, on license 40, discharged during the year 29. Addresses were given by the Lord Mayor of Liverpool and the Rev. J. T. Ceram, and the first named afterwards distributed the prizes. At the close,cheers were given for the Lord Mayor, and the Governor (Mr. Digweed), and the company dispersed. THE VALUB OF PROPERTY AT EARLEsTOWN. On Tuesday evening at the Wellington Hotel, Earlestown, Messrs. Walton and Wood, auctioneers brought under the hammer several lots of property but only succeeded in disposing of two of them. There was a fair company present. but it cannot be said that the bidding was at all brisk. The lots sold were, lot 4, comprising two messuages or dwelling houses, Nos. 273 and 275 on the south side of Crow- lane, Earlestown, occupied by Messrs. Waring and Leyland, the gross annual rental being £3l 4.1. per annum. These were sold for £379 103. Lot 5, con- sisted of two messuages or dwelling houses Nos. 338 and 340, on the north side of Crow-lane, Earlestown, occupied by Messrs. Naylor and Pickup ' as tenants, at a gross rental of £26 per annum. These were sold for .£3Ol. Mr. Thomas Molyneux be- ing the purchaser in each case. The bidding in the other lots offered did not reach the reserve, and they were withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>II'ARVEIST HOMI3 COSCERT AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>II'ARVEIST HOMI3 COSCERT AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The eleventh Harvest Home, and Concert pro- moted by the Earlestown Primitive Methodists was held in the large school-room, on Thursday even- ing. The night was exceptionally wild and stormy but this did not deter a large number of people from patronising the concert, and as in fcrmer years the room was crowded with a large and ap- preciative audience. As before the room was de- corated with all kinds of nature's products, the platform being arranged in tiers, upon which were artistically placed a nice collection of fruit, veget- ables, flowers and plants. The entrance to the platform was from the hack, and was heavily laden with flowers,surmounting the whole being a minia- ture haystack, in the back ground being the in- scription " Welcome to our Harvest Home." The wh,le of the arrangements were carried out by several committees ; the decoration committee be- ing Mr. W. Barnton, as chief, assisted by the Rev. E. R. Davies and Mr. J. Bailey. The platform com- mittee were Messrs. J. Sargenaon, J. Rawlings, assisted by Messrs. W. Llanwarne and W. Barnton. The motto committee comprised the Rev. E. R. Davies, Messrs. J. Austin, W. H. Dale, E. lindnett, and W. Barnton. Entertainment committee, Messrs. E. Dickens, W. Austin Jun.. J. Austin, W. Barnton. P Tomlinson and T. Ormand. The committee for attending to the comforts of the inner man in- eluded Mesdames W. Austin, sen., W. Austin jun.. Pendry, Penile, Creed, Rawlinson, Sproston, Barn- ton and Holleyock. The plan's for decoration were very kindly lent by Mr. .1. W. Emmett. The lion's share of the work, which was of an arduous character fell to Mr. T. Ormand as general secretary. Some very good talent. had been engaged. but at the opening of the concert the Rev. E. R Davies announced that he had a disappointment to announce, owing to Mister H. Dryland, chorister of Chester Cathedral, having been unable to fulfil his engagement, but they had engaged Miss Collins of Liverpool to till the gap. The programme was opened with a pianoforte solo, by Mr. E, Leyland, after which owing to the non-arrival of Mr. A. J. McCormack of Liverpool, Mr. H. Collins, played and sung a serenade " Daddy's baby boy," following which was the humorous song " My Martha, dear." Miss Gertrude Kemp, gave a very careful and pleasing interpretation of the song "The Holy City," for which she was encored, but she simply bowed her acknowledgments. Miss Davies of Earleetown. A pleasing contralto, was most stiere.eis- ful in the difficult song "The promise of Life." and although she was heartily applauded, her efforts certainly deserved a recall. By way of a change, Miss Alice Collins played a selection of Irish airs on the mandoline and in response to an encore she Played "The Gondolier." Mr.A.J. McConnack.whe had now arrived, sang in a pleasing baritone voice " The Star of the iesert," but did not respond to the encore. The fir,t part of the programme was brought to a close by Mr. J. Howard the local " siffluese." who gainod an encore for the solo " Death of Nel4on," giving in response" Killarney." The second part was opened by Mr. McCormack end Miss Kemp in the duet " What will you do, love ? " both parts in which were cleverly sustained. Miss Collins, who is only 12 years of age, followed with a nice rendering of "The Children's Home." Mr. Harry Collins caused much amusement with his sketch, "John Evans Thomas. from Walea."and was encored. "There is a Green Frill far awayrreceived very careful treatment by Miss Davies, and she was deservedly applauded. Mr. J. Howard received a decided encore for the really clever manner in whin?' he whistled "The Lost Chord.' Miss Kemp gave a capital rendering of the song "The Kissing Bridge," and receiving an encore she sang " Uncle John." The song " Off to Philadelphia." received proper treatment by Mr. Nt'Connack,his efforts gain- ing a vociferous encore, in response to which he gave "The Postilion." Mr. Harry Collins concluded the programme with the sketch "The coloured gentlema, " and song " The dandy coloured Coon,' for which he was enc, red. The concert all through was meet enjoyable, and no doubt the committee will have profiled financially.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, OCTOBER 5, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, OCTOBER 5, 1895.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRICT SCHOOL. -R E PORTS 07 THE - . . INSPECTORS FOR 1895.</p>
        <p>Religious Instruction: The excellent reports of the Diocesan Inspectors have already been pub- lished. Drawing: For the third time in succession the highest mark "Excellent" has been awarded by the Science and Art Department. Secular: The report of H.M. Inspector has just been received by the correspondent, Dr. Handystde, a verbatim copy of which appears below. Mixed School: "This is an excellently conducted school. The discipline is maintained in full efficiency, and the teaching car- ried on with sound and skilful methods and on in- telligent lines. The children do their written exer- cises in creditable style, with intelligence and accuracy and good mechanical finish, and display in their oral answering brightness and thought de- cidedly above the average. These results are all the more creditable when in view of the fact that they have been attained in a somewhat crowded achool." Infant School: "This department is conducted with energy and zeal, and with remarkable success. The neat personal appearance of the little ones, their bright and cheerful manner, and the interest they take in their work speak highly for the dis- ciplinary powers of the teacher, while the marked proficiency they display in the elementary subjects and vlried occupations and their smart and sensible aaawering in the object lessons testify to the employment of skilful methods of instruc- tion. The class-rooms are too small for some of the classes,and I would suggest to the managers the advisability of providing larger ones as soon as possible." About 700 children were examined. We congratulate the heed master of the Mixed School vild the head mistress of the Infant School (Mr. and Mrs. Brown) and their respective staffs on the excellent results obtained. It is worthy of note that the Mixed Department, only opened in August, IS9l,has been awarded " Excellent" in all braachs of instruction during the past three years. Dr. Handyside, justly proud of Ilie schools in which he has taken so keen and active an interest, and the rest of the committee of managers, are determined to give the school full scope for extended usefulness by providing for the teaching of higher branches of knowledge. Domestic economy and algebra have already been added to the school curriculum, and it. is intended that cookery and mechanics be added at an early date. Recent appointments : Nis. S. Moss, who at his final examination at Chester Training College in July last obtained a high place in the first division, and Miss Halsall, who obtained a first-class at the Home and Colonial Training College, commenced duties at the school immedi- ately after the summer vacation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL,</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL,</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council will be held on Wednesday next at the Town Hall, Earlestown, when the following minutes of committees will be sub- mitted :</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS, &amp;C., COMMITTHIS. The minutes of this committee contain the following matters .</p>
        <p>Mr. Brierley submitted the arrangements entered into with Mr. Marsh for the nightsoil from Newton, War. grave. and Viaduct Works which was approved with in- structions to make similar arrangements for Crow-lane and Town Elall wards.</p>
        <p>Correspondence was read with Messrs. R. Evans and Co., re Haydock Railway Bridge Parapet Wall, and with Mr. G. Ronayne re building frontage,and it was resolved that Mr. Ronayne be informed the committee cannot see their way to deviate from their former resolutions. An amended plan was submitted by Mr. A. L. CoHinge re his back premises in Wargrave New-road, to property in erection by him, and it was resolved that the same be referred to the Law Clerk</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. Sanderson suggesting a fixed scale of charges for the use of his slaughter-hpuse off Cross-lane as the public slaughter-house. and it was resolved that consideration of same he deferred.</p>
        <p>Letters were read from Mr. Beesley and Messrs. R. Neill and Sons on the decay in the Town Hall floor, and it was resolved that Mr. Brierley take such steps he may consider necessary to stop the decay, . .</p>
        <p>It was resolved.' that IClPssre. H. Evans and Co. be called upon to fence off the Nu zeroua pit of water at the junction of Market-street and S. Helens-road, S.E. aide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>GAS AND WATER COMMITTED.-PROPOSED REDUCTION IN THE</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTED.-PROPOSED REDUCTION IN THE</p>
        <p>PRICE OH GAS. The Committee having considered the cost of provid- ing new mains and lamps at Common-road, Park-road, &amp;c., resolved that additional lamps be fixed where gas mains are already ordered or laid, and that gas mains be laid with additional lamps in Derby-street, Hotel-street, Stanley-street, Clarence-street, Regent-street, Booth- street, Lawrence-street, Chandos-street, Rathtme-street and Athol-street, also that a main and lamp be fixed between Market-street and St. Helens-road, to supply gas to Heather Brae Cottages. It was also resolved that incandescent burners and special lamps be fixed in High- street, opposite Park-road, and in S. Helens-road, opposite Market-street. The His Manager was instructed to examine all prepayment indices quarterly as a check on the inter- mediate receipts. The carbonizing account for the past month was read. giving fuel used 249 tons, 4 cwt. • gas made 2,528.800 feet, being an avtrage of 10,138 feet per ton of fuel used.</p>
        <p>Tenders for tar were suhmitted, and read as follows : EL Dale, Tykiesley, 184. per ton ; J. E. C. Lord, Clacton, 16g. per ton ;J. Peak and Co., W;gan, I.Bs. 81.; It. S. Clare and Co., Liverpool, 19s. 31. per tcn. It was resolved that the tender of Messrs. Clare and Co. be accepted. It was resolved that the price of gas be reduced to all Consumers, except prepaym9nt meters, by twopence per 1,000 teat.and that prepayment consumers be reduced to 28 feet for id., 3s. per 1,000 feet, the reduction to com- mence from Ist October. Resolved that the Law Clerk be instructe I to report on the powers of the Council to take up loans for gas purposes by bonds redeemable at stated periods or other- wise.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ArTaNntNcs COVIMITTHH.-•—THE SCHOOL ATTHND•</p>
        <p>ANCE OFFICER AND FATIER LENNON.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this committee was held on the 4th September, to consider the communications from Father Lennon of 23rd May and 2ad September, also Mr. McMahon's charge against Mr. Ball for untruthfulness in Kenny's cases. The Chairman (Mr. Wood), reported that he had made a personal visit to the cases of irregu- lars submitted by Father Lennon with his letter of 23rd May, and in eacil C 499 found the Attendance Officer's explanations to be correctly given, as verified by the parents. It was resolved that Father Lennon be Informed that the Attendance Officer's report respecting the &amp;IMP is in itself satisfactory to the Committee, and that most of the cases have been previously reported upon by the Attendance Officer to the school teachers ; and that in Kenny's eases Mr. Ball's statementa at the last meeting have been proved correct, Kenny's boys having subsequently made a declaration before the teachers of the Hari-hock R C. School, that " Peter hal previously told Mr Ball he was sent home from Crow- lane schools for money,of which a copy is given." . . .</p>
        <p>It was revived that a letter herwritten it; Father Lonnon with particular+ of the furecorning, the same to be approved by the Chairman.. _ .</p>
        <p>At a subsequent m.etine nt the Committee on the Clerk read his letter sent to Father Lennon. Mr. Mc- Mahon being in pasies4ion of the same, made objections to some of the Retro,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>TWO NGINE DRIVERS KILLED AT</head>
        <p>TWO NGINE DRIVERS KILLED AT</p>
        <p>GLAZ EB I WOK.</p>
        <p>Willitin Vickerstaffe. LivPrpool, driver of the 8.30 ex,oress from Central Station, Liverpool, to Man- chester, met with a terrible death at Glazebroqk on Menday. It appears that as his train was approaching Glaz-bro,,k at full speed the packing of the frame of his engine got on fire. Deceasel,svith the object of getting a bucket of water to extinguish it,got upon his tender, :41111 whits in this position tihr train rushed undsr Glstebrook Bridge.with the result that the girder of the bridge knr:cked Vickerstaffe on the head, killing him instantaneously. The train drew up at cilazebrook,and the body was taken (rota the tender to await an inquest. Deceased leaver' a widow and six children.</p>
        <p>A distressing fatality to a Manchester, Sheffield, and Littcolnaiire engine-driver named George Shepherd oc- curred at o'4'x-brook at ten minutes past four on Satur- day afternoon. The train left Glazebrook for Wigan, and When et the signal bog a tube in the engine burst. causing the fire to ny hack on the angine-driver and torclng him off the engine. When picked up he was found to he dead. His body waa taken to his home in Wigan. The stoker esi-ap-ri uninjured. Shepherd leaves widow and three children.—an inquest was held on Monday, fit the Silverwell Hotel, Wigan, before the borough deputy coroner, relative to the death of Shep- herd, avtd thirty-six, of 2. spring-grove, Darlington-st., who was killed bv falling from his engine whilst runn- ing a train from Manchester. The evidence of the fire- man, Peter Bell, was to the eifsct that when they got to Hlaxebrook Mesa there WV4 a sudden report on the engine, and ste•ni escaped ie volumes. Witness jumped on the framework of the en2ine to avoid the steam, and when he reiumeci Ilk position Again, after the steam had abated,he found that deceased was missing,and immedi- ately atopp•nd the engine, coining to the conclusion that deceased had lost his taming end fallvn off. He was discovered some distance away qui.e dead, his neck having been apparently brokea. A verdict of " Acc-- dental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>FrUITFICOMINO MARRIAGIL—The marriag a Mr. Cyril Dewhurst, of Oughtrington Park, Lymm, and NUM Eleanor Wrigley, of Formby, has been arranged to take place on Thursday, november 14th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>SANAGE MURDER NEAR WIGAN.</head>
        <p>SANAGE MURDER NEAR WIGAN.</p>
        <p>A RAILWAY DETECTIVE KILLED.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night the inhabitantS of Wigan were startled by the report of the murder of a railway detec- tive and a savage assault on another by a gang of thieves. It appears that, owing to extensive robberies from railway waggons on the London and Nrarth Western system in the district, Sergeant Detective Kidd, of the Manchester Railway Police. and Detective Osborne, of the local railway force, were on Sunday night stationed at Kay's Rouses siding, about half a mile from Wigan Station, at a spot not much frequented. Here laden waggons are left, and it was whilst watching these that the detectives noticed a gang of eight or nine men approach and commence to cut the tarpaulin sheets covering the merchandise. The detectives rushed 'on the men in the hope of capturing some of ' them, but the thieves turned upon them, slashed away with their knivos, and stabbed Detective Kidd frightfully in the face and neck. o,borne was also set updn, and was badly ill-treated. The thieves managed to escape, leaving the two detectives on the ground. Osborne was able to make his way to the signal box not far distant, and an engine which was at hand, was requisitioned. Returning -to the spot, Kidd was found quite dead, and Osborne fainted on being placed on the locomotive. Kidd, who is a married man and well- known in railway circles, had been most savagely attacked, the wounds about the face numbering from eight to a deem. Medical aid was aiimmoneo, and on Dr. Graham's advice Osborne, who was too ill ta make a statement, was taken to the Wigan Infirmary. Kidd's body was removed to the Wigan passenger station. Information was at once given to the police, and Super- intendent Brassington, of the Wigan county division, was quickly on the spot with a number of constables, whilst the news of the murder was at once communic- ated throughout the district. Interviewed at the Infirmary, Osborne made a state- ment to the effect, that he had been srationed at Wigan for about eight months. In conaequence of repeated robberies from railway waggons asaistanne was sent from Manchester for the purpose of investigation. Kidd ar- rived at Wigan about eight o'clock on Saturday night, and shortly after that hour he and Kidd wont iota the railway goods' yard. On getting there they noticed a man creeping behind some waggons and a number of other men moving about in other directions. He and Kidd without hesitation challenged the intruders, and closed with several. During the struggle one of the men tried to stab Osborne wi,h a formidable-baking knife, but the officer drew his staff, and knocked it out of the miscreant's hand. Thev struggled together for some time, and whilst Osborne wal on the ground he was kicked most unmercifully all over the body. He lost consciousness for a time, and on recovering found that his assailants had disappeared. He crawled to where Kidd was lying,and found him in a dying condition. The latter asked for a glass ef water, which Osborne was unable to procure, owing to his weak state. He tried to move the dying man to a neighbouring signal-box, but fa Red, and then crawled himself to the hex, where he got assistance. Kidd was found lying dead, and near to him were two blood-stained clinkers,w:dch had evidently been used as weapons. In addition to four caps, a seuf also stained with blood, was found. EIGHT ARRESTS.</p>
        <p>During the night Chief Detective-Inspector Richards, Thtee.tioe-Sergeants Davern and Buckingham, of the London and North-Western Hsi'way police, and Super- intendent Braasington, and about 40 local police officers were actively engaged investigating the affair. Some men's cap, found on the ground near where the struggle took place gave a slight clue, and ultimately five men were arrested on suspicion of being concerned in the trigedy. They are all colliers,and reside in the vicinity of the railway yard. In the case of Kidd, the carotid artery was severed, and he practically bled to death, having crawled seven or eight yarcia in the direction his assailant or asisailants are supposed to have fl.d. A sixth arrest was made on Monday. A collier named William Halliwell was taken into custody at his house at Lower Inca during Monday night. Two further arresta were made, bringing the number of prisoners to eight. About noon on Wednesday Sergeant Mackinnon arrested a collier who goes by the name of Elijah Winatanley, but who, it is stated, is a brother to William Kearsley, the first men apprehended. About the sarae time Inspector Longworth took into custody James Wellena, collier. Both men were captured at Kay's Houses, where seven out of the eight men in custody resided. It was reported during Wednesday that the prisoner William Halliwell, who was arrested at mid- night on Monday, had made a most important statement to the police. He is alleged to have said, in the course of his confession. "I may as well tell you all about it. I did Ism', use the knife. There was no one with Kidd but prisoner mentioned the names of the two men] I was engaged with Osborne, as Detective Osborne said, and I believe that after they left Kidd they came to him to give him a turn over."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Dri'PASRD KNOWN IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>Dri'PASRD KNOWN IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A interest in the sad tragedy has been excited in Warringten,narticularly amongst railway'employes,by the fact that both men haves been connected with War- rington, having formerly lived in the town. Osborne Wag xtationed in Warrington twelve months, but he left some time ago for Wigan. to join the railway detective department. Detective Kidd was here for a longer time, but left four years ago. He was, however, here a few days ago to give evidence against a man named Alexander Clare, who was charged with having on September 15 stolen 70 exes and several tins of condensed milk from the railway at War- rinrftnn.when the prisoner WWI fined .£5. Much indigna- tion is expressed in Warrington against the perpetrators of the outrage, and it is hoped that they will speedily he brought to justice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>PRISONERS BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.-FIVE</head>
        <p>PRISONERS BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.-FIVE</p>
        <p>MEN DISCUARGED.</p>
        <p>At the Wigan Police Court, on Thursday morning, the proceedings were resumed in connection with the Wigan tragedv.after a remand since Monday,before a full bench of IrßeifitTateb, the Mayor presiding. The precincts of the court Were crowned with spectators, all the available snare in the interior being taken up. Captain Bell, the heal c.matablo, and Mr. J. J. Chsrnock, the town clerk, represented the police ; Mr.Lambert,of London,snlicitor to the London and North-Western Railway Companr, and Sonarintendect Copping, of the detective depart- ment at Ellaton, wers alio present. Tha Drisonera were placed in the dock amid a simpressed feeling of excite- ment. The prisoners were charged with tieing in corn. Pant; with others and wilfu.ly murdering Robert Kidd, stahhiag him with a knife, at a goods siding on the London and North-Western Railway on Sunday nieht lat.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lamhert. in opening thec. said he understond that the Irma! communication WI hen made to the r4E.141, P•ho Trea•ory, hut up to the prvsent no answer had been word/red at to the nroweutinn. Awaiting such , inatroetion, he tought a remand In the case of three of the ntianoera only. They were named E. Winstanley, W. K.‘erslcv, and W. lialliwell. As to the other five men. from the inquiries nmde by the police, he did not we his war tn offer Rei• evident:a against them. and they had thought it fit to take the earliest opportunity of awing thiq, aid nreeumed the 13eneh art-mil order thelr discharge. As to the other three, he applied for a remand. G*liha M woe railed. and stated that Osborne wag nnt in a ennd,tinn to appear there that morning, but wouhs he ahle to annPar in a w.Piv Tho rfp2rl Con•table trFtdo a atstement as to onrnriuni- e+tinw with the Treasitry,and having gone carefully into the ea.. Th• prisoners named were then.dianhareed._</p>
        <p>The three nthera in custody said they had nothing to sq. 00airn,t I remand A erd awaited the annearance of the discharged mrn. Winatsnlev being met tic, his wife. A remand for one week was zranted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>F ROD FE AM.</head>
        <p>F ROD FE AM.</p>
        <p>INV ?MULL ESTATH.—Tt leßted that Mr. Philip SpPakman. J.P ,of Runnorn, has purnhasPri this Pqtßto through Mr. Charlos E. Linalcer, o Frodsham. artina on behalf of tha trustees of the late Mr. James 11,rnolds.</p>
        <p>FRISE CITURCR, FROISHAII(—The harvest, thanksgiving mai-vice.' in connefrion with this church wore hal Inn Sunday afternnnn and nveling. The Rev. Ft Percival preached. The interior of the church had been taste. fully dee -.rated forth. nceaxion,and the a.rrangement of the large assnrtment of frnitç vegetables, land flower' were equal to that of previous years. Toe hymns and anthems by the choir were tastarnily rendered. POLICA News—At Frrvisham Police Cnnrt on Tueaday. before Mr. Reynnitis and Mr. Tlinturis, a lad 14 years of age, named John Bushell, Newtown, Prodsham, was chnter'd with stealing quantity of apples of the value or le 8,1.. the property of Peter Hall, farmer, K;ngsleb. —P.C., Price met the defendant in company with two ather youths on the' 23rd August, at Frodshern Bridge. Re searched them and found twelve lbs. of apples in their noascasinn. They admitted having stolen them from Mr. Hall's orchard, and Mr. Hall identified them as haine similar to thee., grown by him.—A fine of Sc. was 1,71rn.i.d...Th(111110.1 Daman, BrAMPV. ADA f`har"d Frnrisham Police Court on Monday, before Etr. Steele. with heir." drunk on Sunday night at Overton. He We. fined 2.. 6-1. or in default seven data' impriannment. DRATTL OP AN OLD INPIABITAIST.—An old Frndsham resident in the person of Samuel Kilgraas. of Main-at.. Prodsham, died on Wednesday morning, at, the advanced age of 87 rears. In August last he met with an accident whilst distrituatine the Parish Magazines. It appears that he was proceeding along Chtirch-at, when he Was knocked down by a wacennettp containing n Pleasure party from Lnwtnn. and his left side was injured. r),•. Sutton soap called in, hut despite all efforts. Kiiersss succumbed .. Stew/. The deceased was horn at Fred. sham Brides and had resided in the tieighhourhn-id all his lifetime. For about 30 years he carried cm the husiness of grocer in the town. He was a staunch Churchmen, and frequently anted as scripture reader.</p>
        <p>ANGLO-AFRICAN GOLD PROPERTIES. Lettere of allotment and regret will be pested in batcbes. The first batch bss been posted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Special thanksgiving services for the ingathering of the harvest were held in St. Peter's Church, on Sunday As usual, the church was eery prettily decorated, the font being the special work of Mrs. Gillespie, reredos, Miss J. Smith ; pulpit, Misses Airey ; Lady chapel, Mrs. Royse, and Mrs. Elindley Smith. The anthem at the morning service was "0 praise the Lord," and at the evening service " Praise the Lori, 0 Jerusalem." The preachers were Rev. Henry Siddall,vicar of St. Thomas', Ashton, in the morning, and Rev. A. P. Watkins, assist- ant priest of St. Michael and All Angels, in the evening. There were large congregations at each service, the offertories amounting to £26 33. 61, in aid of the, church debt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Special services in connection with the harvest thanks- giving were cemmcnced on Sunday at St. John's Church, and were continned every evening during the week. The church was profusely decorated with fruit, flowers, and vegetables, amongst those assisting in the decora- tions being Mesdames Latimer, Bentley, Bowes, Lane, Martindale, and Cellingwood, Misses Matthews,Brother- hood, Mansell, Messrs. Clough, Foden, Latimer, Dale, Monks, Martindale, Anderton, and Andrews. At eight o'clock on Sunday morning holy communion was cele- brated, there being a tair number of communicants. At the ordinary service the choir sang the anthem "0 Lord how manifold," and the sermon was preached by the Rev. J. La keman, B.D. (vicar of St. Mark's, St. Helens). In the afternoon there was a short service,when the Rev. T. R. Matthews gave an address, the Earlestown prize band also playing several selections of sacred music, in- cluding " Adestes Fideles," "Star oflßethlehem," and "Comfort ye." At the evening service the choir sang the anthem "I will feed my flock," and the sermon was preached by the Rev. W. A. Jones. The Earlestown band played after the service " The Austrian Hymn," "The Gloria," and " Hallelujah Chorus." The collec- tions during the day amounted to £l4 17s. 3d. On Monday evening a sacred recital was given by the organist and choir, when the following programme was very ably submitted :—Anthems " 0 Lord how mani- fold are Thy works ;" son:, " The peace of Mr. James Heade ; organ solo, " Allegro Maetitoso ;" tenor solo and quartette (a) " With all your hearts " (b) "Cast thy burden," Mr. William Monks ; organ solo, " Vesper bells ;" solo, " Lord, remember David," Master Wood- floe ; solo, " WiWtt I think upon Thy goodness," Miss Ada Mee ; organ solo, " Offertoire." On Tuesday even- ing there was an ordinary service, when the sermon was preached by the Rev. J. Potter, of Heywood. On Wed- nesday evening the harvest cantata " Crowning of the Wheat," with selections by the Earlestown Orchestral Society, was rendered. PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL, HAYDOCK. The abwe chapel was effectively decorated with corn, flowers, fruit and vegetables, and the specially erected stage and platform resembled a well stocked fruiter's stall on the occasion of the thanksgiving services which took place on Sunday, when the Rev. H. C. Wilson, of Manchester, preached appropriate sermons. The annual fruit banquet and concert was held on Monday evening, when a most interesting programme of music, speeches. and songs was capitally rendered by the glee party, from Earlestown, consisting of MiS3 Jennie Roberts,Miss Stronach, Masters G.O. Edwards and N. Stevens,Messrs. A. Barnton, F. P. Clare and 0. Edwards (ccnductor), with Mr. S. Drina as accompanist, and Mr. J. Taylor as reader. Mr. Dowling who occupied the presideetal chair, opened the meeting in a brief felicitious speech, and proved to be an ideal chairman by not allowing a moment's delay between the pieces,and thus making the somewhat lengthy programme to be completed in a rea- sonable timetalthough frequent encores were demanded. The glees and part songs were sung in good style and called forth much applause. Miss Roberts and Miss Stronach received well merited applause for the intelli- gent rendition of their selections. Mr. Clare was especi- ally effective in Sullivan's popular " Once again," and with Mr. Edwards in 7Parry's celebrated duet " Flow gently Deva." A word of praise is due to Mr. A. Barn- ton for the manner in which he sang ;" The Holy City," and to Mr. Edwards for his artistic singing of the items allotted him. Mr. J. Taylor with his Lancashire sketches and Messrs. Clare, Barnton and Edwards in their humor- ous trios kept the audience in roars of laughter. Mr. S. Drina played the accompaniments creditably. Fruit and I biscuits were distributed during the interval and at the close of the corked when an auction sale of the exhibits I took place. Totes of thanks to the chairman and to all who had contributed to the evening's enjoyment, were passed, and the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>BEWSEY-ROLD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROLD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival of the above was held on Sunday and there were very large congregations both morning and evening. The decoration,' were very extensive,and the manner in which the various flowers, fruit, and cereals were laid out reflected great credit upon the de- corators. The pulpit especially was beautifully decor- ated, a beautiful anchor being much admired. Ia tho morning the Rev. G. D. Mason preached, taking his text from 1. Thessalonians, v. 1, "In everything give thanks." In the course of his remarks the rev.gentleman observed that once again had there been a fulfiment of the promise "Seed time and harvest shall not fail," and their worship that day was an acknowledgment that the yearly ingathering of the harvest was a result of God's gracious rule, and not of an irresistible law in the , mechanism of the universe. Notwithstanding the nutne- rims improvements that had been made in agriculture, and our present advanced state of civilisation, it was God's hand alone which created and trained everything , living. Persons in large towns, no less than those in villages and agricultural districts, should give thanks to God, for he not only enabled them to gather a rich agri- cultural harvest,llia influence was spread over our com- mercial enterprises as well, and as we reaped a harvest in the field, so did we in our large manufactories, shipp- ing houses, and so forth. Everycne, therefore. whether living in town or country,sheuld with one voice express their thankfulness to the Almighty for His gracious goodness. The Rev. Owen Watkins was the preacher in the evening, and based an admirable discourse on Mat- thew vi.3o, "Wherefore, if God so clothe the gram of the field," The collections were liberal, and are to be devoted to the funds of the church and general circuit expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>RECEPTION AT TIM WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>RECEPTION AT TIM WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>A reception to the members of the Y.M.C.A.,was given by the president, Mr. J. Charlton Parr, () .L. C.C., on Wednesday. in the Lecture room of the Y.M.d.A.,Friar's Green. There was a very good attendance considering the inelement night, and a most enjoyable entertain- ment 'was held.</p>
        <p>Mr. PARA, who WAS received with acclammation, said that he was very hapoy to see them that night, and he extended a hearty welcome to those who had been able to attend that meeting. He felt sure that had the night not been so inclement the benches would have been much better filled than they were that evening. But they could not regulate the weather, and the fine weather they had had he felt would make it still more deterent. In offer- ing that welcome, he also felt that he must say a word of congratulation to the Association far the very marked change for the better which had come over its prospects. Ne hope ei that that was only a presage of the future. (Applause.) When first he met the Association it was in its infancy. It was a very promising infant no doubt, but still it was struggling with the weaknesses and dia- -1 abilities attending infancy. They,however,all felt quite sure that it would rise into something more. They felt very anxious that that better time should Come. He thought that now they might consider their Association as a very promising youth—(hear, 'marl—and he hoped that they [night look forward to its majority before very many years were over—(apolause),—when it might be , able to go along without any assistance. Flo was also proud to believe that the As..aciation wouldincrease and advance, 118 WAS also sure that the progress which had been so marked during the last few months was due to the. energy and activity of the secretary, Mr. Stewart. (Applause.) He WAS happy te be informed that since October last,when the members of the Association num- bered 120, it had now reached 170, an increase which he considered to augur an extremely satisfactory future of the Association. (Applause.) Mr. WYLLIR having engaged in prayer, the programme of the evening was proceeded with. The programme was of 3 very excellent and varied character, and was thoroughly enjoyed lay all present. Pianoforte solos were cleverly given by Miss Pairclough, Miss Mabel Fair- dough, and Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield, Mrs. Pearson upheld her reputation be the way in which she gave the solos. "The Gift," and "Fiddle and I," for the latter of which she was encored and gave " Gene- vieve." Mr. W. H. Wallington was warmly applauded for his rendition of "Time Arena' "and " Come lasses and lads," and was encored for the latter but declined to respond. Mr. Payton was the accompanist. Mr. Ash- to,, gave a very humorous reading describing the game of football and was heartily encored, and gave in res- ponse "Otto Schmidt." Mr. Newitt, the secretary of the I Manchester Y M.C.A., 9103 gave an address upon the work of the Y.M.C. A. At the close of the entertainment, Mr. Parr expressed the pleasure he had experienced with the various artistes. He called upon the audience to show their appreciation which they did very heartily. Mr. A. H. CAOSPIELD said that he had been asked to express the general feeling which he was sure was one of gratitude to Mr. Parr for his presence that evening, and for the assistance in so mans. ways which he had given to the Society. (Applause.) He did not feel called upon to sing Mr. Parr's praises in the matter, and he should think it bad taste to do so, but he must say on behalf of the members of the Association there, and of all Warringtonians, that they valued every tie and association which linked Mr. Parr to the town he had been so long associated with—(applause)—and he hoped, and he was sure all hoped, that they would see 89 much as possible of Mr. Parr in the future. (Renewed ap- plause.) There must be present in all their minds, the extremely happy and most valuable gift to the town, which would be a general boon to all, and he was glad l to have the opportunity of moving a vote of thanks to Mr. Parr. (Applause.) Refreshments were altsrwards partaken of, and the meeting was brought to a termination.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
