        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>NOTES ON GiAM.ES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GiAM.ES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Bradford match of last Saturday but one has been the cause of a complete change in the composition of the Warrington team, and with the exception of the threequarters, it will be quite unrecognisable to out- siders. It is rather singular that the Warrington club who play a team composed almost exclusively of War- ringtonians should be the first to bear the brunt and fight the battle for the Northern Union against out and out professionalism for that is what it practically amounts to. Had the Warrington committee yielded to the demands of the players and given them 93.0 r 12g. for the Bradford match, there was no guarantee that the players would not soon be asking for still more.</p>
        <p>On Saturday week there was practically no broken time, for the team did not leave Warrington for Brad- ford until the 1.55 p.m. train from the Central Station. Consequently the meet that could be lost by any of the players was about an hour, and the amount given was surely adeqoate for the logs. The committee have re- mained firm at the risk of losing all the players,and they have been strongly supported in the matter by the Sorthern Union.</p>
        <p>Tho team which went to Leigh on Saturday, in Conse- quence of the " strike," was composed of the "A "team with a sprinkling of the first. Of the team which went to Bradford only five remained, viz., Barber, Burton, Broady, Burrows, and Saunders, so that it was not with any feelings of confidence that Leigh,who have been go- ing so strong of late, were tackled. However, as one of the players said, all the team were triers, and the game was not a runaway affair at all. In fact, Warrington were really unfortunate in being beaten as much as they were. The penalty goal was very questionable,for it is difficult to see how the ball can be played when a man is held. What a great many considered a fair try was also disallowed near the end of the game, when one of the Leigh players kicked the ball over his own line, and a Warrington man grounded it. The referee refused to give a try, but ordered a five yards scrimmage instead.</p>
        <p>The team did so well on Saturday that for the match to-day at Runcorn, there are only two changes, the fol- lowing being the team selected :—Evans, full back ; Barber, Heesom, Burton, Broady, threequarters ; Hough- ton and Nevins, half backs; Burrows, Saunders, Thorn- ton, Wilcocks, Hill, Barrett, Andrews, and Fairhurst, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SIR GILBERT GREENALL'S VOTE AT Sr. HELENS. The revision of the lists of voters for the St. Helens district of the Newton County division took place on Saturday by Mr. J. T. Foard, revising barrister, at St. Helens County Court. The Liberals were represented by Mr. J. R. Renshaw, of Newton, and the Conservatives by Mr. Holland Owen. A claim had been entered on behalf of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.,in respect of ownership'of Eccleston Hall, near Prescot. The Revising Barrister asked if anyone supported the claim, and there being no reply it was struck out.—Later Mr. Owen asked his Honour to refer again to Sir Gilbert's claim. A representative now appeared in court. Mr. Philip Halton proved the ownership of Ecaleston Hall,and the vote was allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>STOCKTOg HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTOg HEATH.</p>
        <p>APPLISTON PAROCHIAL COMMITTHIS.—This: committee met at the London Bridge Inn, Stockton Heath on the 25th ult. Mr. H. Eyes occupied the chair and there were also present Messrs. Pearson, Richardson, Herniation, Ashton Hayes, Fledgcock, Roughsedge, John Derbyshire, Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. Swinton (surveyor), and Mr. Farrington (inspector of nuisances). The correspondence between the Town Clerk of Warrington, the Clerk to the Cheshire County Council, and the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was read, and after a lengthy discussion it was resolved on the motion of Mr. Pearson, seconded by Mr. Hugh Derbyshire That this committee are pre- pared to join with the Borough of Warrington in under- taking to construct the sewer arranged to be made for taking the sewage from Stockton Heath into the system of sewers within the Borough of Warrington on the understanding that when the provisional order for the extension of the Borough is confirmed by Parliament, the Borough of Warrington shall repay the money expended by the Rural District Council of Runcorn ; an agreement to be entered into between the two authorities embodying the terms and conditions upon which such sewer shall be constructed and setting forth the propor- tions in which the costs thereof shall be defrayed.—The minutes were confirmed at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council on Tuesday, on the motion of Mr. Darling- ton, seconded by Mr. Baxter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>STORM.-REPORTED DROWNING OF RUNCORN MEN.</head>
        <p>THE STORM.-REPORTED DROWNING OF RUNCORN MEN.</p>
        <p>The report that the steam flat Renfrew had been sunk off Rhyl in the gale on Wednesday, has been a topic of conversation in Runcorn, from the fact that she traded there, and that two of her crew belonged to the town. The engineer is Thomas Lloyd. of Poa-at.. who leaves a widow and eight children, and the captain is John Bur- ton,Cavendish-st., who leaves a family of three children. The mate is named Parrington, of Liverpool. The flat belonged to Mr. W. Burton, Sankey Hall, near Warring- ton. Early on Thursday morning the dead body of a man, supposed to be the remains of Parrington, was picked up in the Rock Channel by the steam fiat, Han- nah Beckett. All on hoard are reported to be lost. The wreck can now be seen in an upright position as the tide ebbs. Her hull is entire,but her mast is gone. The boat left Penmaenmawr with stone for Runcorn on Tuesday night, The news of the sinking of the flat was received at Rhyl by telegram from the Vael Rant coastguard station. The Rhyl lifeboat was at once launchecl,but they failed to find any trace of the wreck. The lifeboat was afterwards beached near the Point of Ayr, and the lifeboat crew were informed that the Point of Ayr boat had been out previously, and that between seven and eight o'clock two men had been seen clinging to the rigging, and that the mast went overboard with them.</p>
