      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-05_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>RUNCORN BOLTS IN TFIE GALE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOLTS IN TFIE GALE.</p>
        <p>The schooner Ellesmere, belonging to Messrs. Brundrit and Co., of Runcorn. arrived at Runcorn Docks on Thursday, and Mr. Kirkham reports that he had a very severe passage from Penmaennsawr, with macadam. Coming through the Rock Channel the bulwark of the ship were greatly damaged, and nearly all the sails were carried away.--John Waterworth, master of the coasting flat John and Sarah, of Runcorn, had a most adventurous passage frorn the Welsh coast. The boom and mainsail of the flat were lost, and, although tappearcd as though the boat would founder, Mr. Waterworth managed VI run her with only the foresail as far as New Ferry,where he beached her.</p>
        <p>THE FLIT "CHAMPION."</p>
        <p>A rumour gained currency in Runcern that the list Champh.n, belonging to Messrs. F. Speakman and Sons, had gone down during the gale on Wednesday. Mr. Carey was cemmiasioned to make enquiries and went into Wales na Thursday morning. As a result of his en- quiries, a telegram announcing that the vessel was safe was received in Runcorn on Thursday afternoon. SINKING OF A SCHOONER.</p>
        <p>The schooner Selina was sunk at Runcorn on Thurs- day morning, during the prevalence of the gale. It con- tained a cargo of china clay from Fowey, and in en- deavouring to enter the Bridgewater dock, the vessel was thrown against the pierhead owing to ths violence of the gale. The anchor was stoved into the bows, and the vessel began to sink almost immediately. A steam tug was requisitioned and the schooner was towed into the Ship Canal and placed on a bank near the canal wall. It, however, soon tilled with water and sank. The crew landed in safety. MISHAPS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The gale made navigation of the Shp Canal danger- ous. At one o'clock noon on Thursday the steamer Elma, bound from Manchester to St. Petersburg, was moored at Runcorn through stress of weather. At four o'clock the steamer Merriona, from Huelva to Man- chester, with ore, grounded at Saltport and then at Run- corn Bend. but was gc t clear in each instance without damage. She was tied up at Runcorn for the night. The steamship Lodore, from the Baltic to Manchester, also tied up for the night at Runcorn. he steamship Musk, for ManchPster, was delayed at Eastham for the day and night In consequence of the storm. In attempting to anchor in the river, this ship lost her anchor and 65 fathoms of chain.</p>
        <p>SI7DDEN DEATII OF A WELL KNOWN RITNCORNIAN AT</p>
        <p>LrvEßpooL.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Knowles, 56 years of age, proprieter of the Royal Hotel, Lime-street, Liverpool, died suddenly on Wednesday evening, in the bar of the hotel. He had been ailing for some time past—being a sufferer from heart disrasp, but during the day he did not show signs of being worse in health than he had been recently. The deceased cr,i9 exceedingly well known in Runcorn. He was native of the town, and served an apprenticeship with Mr. Righy,dra per, then of Church-st. He afterwards conducted the business on his own account at these premises for a number of years. Relinquishing this husi- ness, he conducted Wilson's Hotel, Runcorn, for several years, and afterwards became local traveller for Messrs. .00ke and Townsend,of Liverp.)ol.Some time afterwards he again entered the "public" line,and became proprietor of the hotel at which he died as suddenly, having con- ducted it about ten years. He leaves a wife and seven children. The funeral is to take place at Anflsid [Cemetery.</p>
