        <p>EVERY inhabitant of Warrington The Parr Hall will endorse the words of thanks to Mr. J. C. PARR which Were 41)°Icen by the MAYOR and by Mr. ROBERT DAVIES .(:atk the occasion of the opening of Hall. Mr. PARR has by his gift the magnificent Hall which will henceforth be centre of civic life in War.rington certainly en- ‘it6l himself to the unqualified gratitude of the 14resent and of future generations of Warring- The gift is undoubtedly the most 4144ropriate and usef al that could have been blade at the present time. It will be generally l'iraitted that the public life of the town has for a eW Years past been checked and, so to speak, dis- c,nlraged by the lack of a suitable building in IWhich large social and political: meetings could 48 conveniently held. And if Mr. PARR 1,44 not so generously come forward when 1113 did it is probable that the town would a" suffered from these disadvantages for tl/1114.113' years to come. It is needless to say that •13 gift loses none of its value by the manner ' WhiCh it has been bestowed. From the very til,cePtion of the scheme till the delivery of the ;aracteristically appropriate speech which accom- hied his transference of the deeds to the Cor- 4°t4tiou on Thursday, Mr. PARR has displayed fine Ft and consideration, and it is, of course, only fir to say that he has been met in a similar spirit 1,1 the members and officials of the Corporation tr Hall as it is now handed over-to the Corpora- is free from debt and free from all restric- tlo4s. It could net been more completely devoted ° Public objects if the nine or ten thousand 114°1131d8 which have been spout upon the building tailLl 48 equipments had been drawn from the 1 rather than from the resources of a private giteutleman. In a word, the Parr Hall is in every t 44? Worthy of the public-spirited donor, and of . town to which it has been presented. tOE'S GOUT &amp; RHEUMATIC PILLS I SHOULD NOW BB ILL IIY BED. B"rs puts. 107, Jubilee-terrace, Wolverharn pt on-road 