        <p>" CUT IT SHORT." Apropos of the elevation of Baron Henry da Worms to the peerage, the " Saturday Review " tells this story concerning him alai Lerd Randalph Churchil. Lord Randolph, as it is well known, was the first parliamen- tary general to make of his followers a disciplined army- One night he had put Baron de Worms up to speak, hoping to " draw " Mr. Gladstone. As soon as the G.O.M. began to take notes, Lord Randolph hoped the Baron would sit down ; but, alas; he went on. Lord Randolph pulled his coat-tail. " Cut it short, for good- ness sake ! " But in spite of the hint the Baron con- tinued. Suddenly Lord Randolph lost his temper; lean- ing forward, he plucked the Baron's coat again. " Sit down, I say," and down tne Baron sat. By-the-bye,dear old Smith once ventured to pull Mr. Matthew's coat- tails,whereupon Lord Llandaff turning round, and Oar,- ing through his spectacles at Old Morality, said with much asperity, " Really, I cannot undertake to conduct the debate if I am to be interrupted in this way." Old Smith shrugged his shoulders,but never took the liberty again. Baron de Worms was more :humble. HE WRITES "MAIDEN" SPEECHES. "I think I may claim to belong to an absolutely uni- que profession," observed a man to the writer of an article in " Casselle' Saturday Journal," mot long ago. "In fact, I form a profession quite by myself. The pro- fession is that of speech-writing. You know very well that there are scores of M.P.'e and County Councillors who, although most (stimable men of business, are yet, from lack of practice, quite unable to make a respectable speech. Well, in such cases I promptly offer my aid. After the last General Election in 1892, I circularised over two hundred members of the Lionse, offering to write them a speech on any subject and of any length. Many more than you might suppose availed themselves of the opportunity, graceful little five-minute maiden' orations with an effective wind-up being in especial demand. I also do a great deal for members of debat- ing societies who are anxious to distinguish themselves before their friends. For a debating society in the north of London, I wrote two papers taking diametrically opposite views of the subject of the House of Lords. Two members of the society had applied to me for assistance, each knowing nothing of the other's application. In regard to M.P.'s some of them who can make a speech, often employ me in collecting notes on a subject on which they wish to speak, and I often have the gratifi- cation of seeing my words and figures reproduced in the daily papers, and praised or attacked, as the case may be, in the leading articles." SOME insTo RIC NICKNAMES</p>
